window.PLAIN = {"scenarios": [{"id": "s1", "title": "Scenario 1 — Customer Support Resolution Agent", "domains": "D1 · D2 · D5", "brief": "A meal-kit company runs a customer support agent on the Claude Agent SDK. Its MCP tools are <code>get_customer</code>, <code>lookup_order</code>, <code>process_refund</code>, <code>escalate_to_human</code> and <code>pause_subscription</code>. The agent may refund up to $150 per box; larger refunds go to a human."}, {"id": "s2", "title": "Scenario 2 — Code Generation with Claude Code", "domains": "D3 · D4 · D5", "brief": "A startup uses Claude Code in one TypeScript repository with <code>server/</code>, <code>mobile/</code> and <code>packages/shared/</code> folders. The repository commits a <code>.claude/</code> directory and a root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. Engineers use Claude Code to generate endpoints, screens and tests from tickets."}, {"id": "s6", "title": "Scenario 6 — Structured Data Extraction", "domains": "D4 · D5 · D1", "brief": "An insurance broker extracts data from certificates of insurance sent by many carriers. Claude returns the insured name, carrier, policy number, limits, deductibles and dates through a tool with a JSON schema. A validator checks each result before it is posted to the broker's system."}, {"id": "s5", "title": "Scenario 5 — Claude Code for Continuous Integration", "domains": "D3 · D4 · D2", "brief": "A travel-booking company runs Claude Code in <b>GitHub Actions</b> on a Kotlin and TypeScript repository. It has four jobs: a pull-request review that posts inline comments, an autofix job for failing builds, test generation for new endpoints, and a weekly dependency-risk report. The review job uses an MCP server for code search and API guidelines. <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and <code>.claude/</code> are committed."}, {"id": "s3", "title": "Scenario 3 — Multi-Agent Research System", "domains": "D1 · D2 · D5", "brief": "A hospital's evidence team runs a research system on the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent hands work to subagents through the Task tool: literature search, trial-registry search, document analysis, citation checking, synthesis and reporting. Journals and the trial registry are reached over MCP. Summaries go to guideline committees, and every claim must cite a paper and section."}, {"id": "s4", "title": "Scenario 4 — Developer Productivity with Claude", "domains": "D2 · D3 · D1", "brief": "A language-learning company's platform team uses Claude Code on a large Ruby on Rails app with a React front end. Engineers read and change code with the built-in tools: Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep and Glob. MCP servers connect Claude to Linear and a staging Postgres database. Some engineers also script Claude with the Agent SDK for internal tools."}], "questions": [{"id": "m7p-s1-03", "was": "m7-s1-03", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Prompt guidance vs programmatic enforcement", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "Customers who want to cancel are handed to a person through <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Product would like the agent to suggest pausing the subscription first, before it hands the customer over. This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat. What is the <b>most appropriate</b> way to add it?", "options": [{"t": "Add a system-prompt instruction to suggest a pause, plus two short example chats.", "why": "Best. A soft behaviour, where an occasional miss does no harm, is what prompt guidance is for. One instruction and two examples are easy to maintain."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks <code>escalate_to_human</code> until a pause has been offered.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The hook would make the offer happen every time, but it needs code to spot cancellations and track what was said. Guarantees are for business rules.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add the pause offer to the <code>escalate_to_human</code> description, so Claude offers it before calling the tool.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Claude does read tool descriptions, but they explain when and how to call a tool. How to talk to the customer belongs in the system prompt, where it is easy to find and change.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Route every cancellation request to a new retention subagent before it reaches a human.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A retention subagent could make the offer well, but it adds a handoff and another prompt to maintain, for one sentence of behaviour.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat.", "explanation": "Programmatic enforcement is for steps that must happen every time. A best-effort suggestion is shaped with a clear instruction plus a few examples.", "eli5": {"question": "Product wants the agent to ask “would you like to pause instead?” before it hands a cancellation to a person. The clue: it's a retention idea, not a business rule.", "answer": "A shop asks staff to mention the loyalty card at the till. A note and a quick demo do the job; nobody installs a till that locks until the card is mentioned. Here the note is a <b>system-prompt instruction</b> and the demo is <b>example chats</b>. <b>Rule: must happen every time → code. Nice to have → prompt and examples.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A plain instruction plus examples sets the nudge.", "A lock in code (a <b>hook</b>) is for things that must never be skipped. Here skipping is allowed.", "Tool instructions would work, but they're the wrong place for how to talk to customers.", "A whole new helper agent for one friendly sentence is too much."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the most reliable option is always the best one</b>. The question asks for the most <i>appropriate</i> one. Code-level enforcement (a hook) is right when a step must happen every time, such as checking identity before a refund. For a nice-to-have it is extra machinery: something has to detect cancellations and remember what was said.", "diagram": "Must it happen every time?\n  yes → hook or prerequisite gate (code)\n  no  → prompt instruction + examples", "mapTitle": "How firmly must this behaviour hold?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["A suggestion or tone; an occasional miss is harmless", "System-prompt instruction + a few examples", "Cheap and easy to change"], ["A business rule; a miss costs money or breaks policy", "Hook or prerequisite gate in code", "Prompts have a non-zero failure rate"], ["Sensitive data must never reach the model", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook that strips it", "The model can't repeat what it never saw"], ["Guidance on when and how to call a tool", "The tool's description", "Claude reads it when choosing tools"], ["Instructions are clear but output still varies", "Few-shot examples", "Shows the pattern"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Product would like the agent to suggest pausing the subscription first, before it hands the customer over", "means": "The goal is a suggestion in the conversation. That belongs in Claude's instructions, not in a tool's description (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat", "means": "The decider. Missing it now and then is harmless, so a hook (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a new subagent (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more than the goal needs."}, {"quote": "What is the most appropriate way to add it?", "means": "“Most appropriate” means the right size: an instruction with examples, <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Business rule → enforce it in code. Suggestion or style → prompt instruction plus examples.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Programmatic enforcement (hooks, prerequisite gates) vs prompt-based guidance."}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-06", "was": "m7-s1-06", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Tool descriptions: input formats", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Customers usually describe a box by when it arrived, for example “last Tuesday's box”. The agent passes that date to <code>lookup_order</code> as the box ID, and the call fails. Each customer's box IDs are listed in their <code>get_customer</code> record. The description of <code>lookup_order</code> is one line: “Looks up a box.” What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add few-shot examples to the system prompt that turn a delivery date into a box ID first.", "why": "Works, but leaves the cause in place. Examples would teach the date-to-ID step, but they add tokens to every request while the one-line description stays unclear. They are a later step.", "trap": "works around the cause"}, {"t": "Add a routing layer that detects dates and calls <code>get_customer</code> before <code>lookup_order</code>.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt for a first step. Code that spots dates and calls get_customer first would fix this case, but it is fragile and far more work than a better description.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Expand the <code>lookup_order</code> description with the ID format, an example, and where IDs come from.", "why": "Best. Claude learns how to call a tool mainly from its description. This one gives no ID format and no route from a date to an ID, and fixing that is cheap."}, {"t": "Add a <code>lookup_order_by_date</code> tool that takes a delivery date and returns the box.", "why": "Works, but more than a first step. A date-based tool would handle this request, but it is new code, and with equally thin descriptions the agent can misuse it too.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The description of lookup_order is one line: “Looks up a box.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the main signal for how to call a tool. A minimal description should first be expanded with input format, examples, edge cases and boundaries.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent keeps putting a date where a box ID should go. The clue: the tool's instructions are four words long.", "answer": "If a form field just says “Box”, people write all sorts of things in it. Write “Box ID, like BX-20431, found on the customer record” and most mistakes stop. For Claude, the <b>tool description</b> is that label. <b>Rule: tool misused and its description is thin → fix the description first.</b>", "options": ["Examples help later, but the label itself is still unclear.", "A program that hunts for dates in messages is a lot of work for a first step.", "Correct. Fix the label: say what an ID looks like and where to find it.", "A second tool with an equally vague label can go wrong the same way."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent needs more examples or smarter routing</b>. But Claude decides how to call a tool mainly from the tool's description. “Looks up a box” gives no ID format and no hint that IDs come from <code>get_customer</code>. Fix that first: it is cheap and it fixes the cause.", "diagram": "\"Looks up a box\" → Claude guesses → date as ID → fail\n+ ID format, example, where IDs come from → right call", "mapTitle": "A tool is called wrongly. What do you fix first?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "First fix"], "rows": [["Wrong input passed; the description is one line", "Description lacks format and examples", "Expand the description"], ["Two similar tools get mixed up", "Overlapping descriptions", "Sharpen each description's boundaries"], ["Descriptions are detailed; behaviour still varies", "Ambiguous cases", "Add few-shot examples"], ["Agent prefers Grep over a better MCP tool", "The MCP description undersells it", "Improve the MCP tool's description"], ["Bad calls happen and the agent can't recover", "Error says only “failed”", "Structured error naming the expected format"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Customers usually describe a box by when it arrived", "means": "The agent gets a date, so the tool must explain how to get from a date to an ID."}, {"quote": "The agent passes that date to lookup_order as the box ID, and the call fails", "means": "The agent doesn't know what a box ID looks like. That is a description problem, not a routing problem (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Each customer's box IDs are listed in their get_customer record", "means": "The data to fix it already exists. The agent just isn't told where to look, which a description can say."}, {"quote": "The description of lookup_order is one line: “Looks up a box.”", "means": "The decider. The cause is a thin description, so examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a new tool (<b>[[3]]</b>) work around it."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "First step = the cheapest change that fixes the cause: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "A tool gets the wrong input and its description is thin? Expand the description first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism LLMs use for selection"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s2-02", "was": "m7-s2-02", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.3 Path-scoped rules", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team's accessibility conventions apply to every screen file. Screen files are named <code>*.screen.tsx</code> and live in <code>mobile/</code> and <code>packages/shared/ui/</code>. The conventions sit in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so they also load during server work. Where should they <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in <code>mobile/</code>, and one in <code>packages/shared/ui/</code> that imports it.", "why": "Works, but tied to folders. Folder CLAUDE.md files would load the conventions for screens, but they need one per tree and also load for every other file in those folders.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "An <code>/a11y</code> skill that engineers invoke when they work on a screen.", "why": "Works, but only when someone remembers. A skill loads when it is invoked, so screens edited without it miss the conventions.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "A file in <code>.claude/rules/</code> whose <code>paths:</code> frontmatter is <code>**/*.screen.tsx</code>.", "why": "Best. A path-scoped rule loads whenever Claude edits a matching file, in any folder, and stays out of server work."}, {"t": "A separate markdown file pulled into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. @import keeps the conventions in their own file, which is tidier, but they still load in every session, including server work.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Screen files … live in mobile/ and packages/shared/ui/.", "explanation": "Conventions tied to a file type that spans folders belong in .claude/rules/ with a paths glob. They load only for matching files, wherever those files are.", "eli5": {"question": "Some rules apply only to screen files, which sit in two different folders. Right now Claude reads those rules even when it works on the server.", "answer": "Picture a sticky note that pops up only when you open a certain kind of file, wherever it is. That's a <b>rule file with a path pattern</b> in <code>.claude/rules/</code>. A folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is a note stuck on a folder: fine when everything is in one folder, clumsy when the files are spread out. <b>Rule: convention follows a file pattern → path-scoped rule. Convention follows a folder → folder CLAUDE.md.</b>", "options": ["Notes stuck on two folders: copies to keep in sync, and they show up for other files in those folders too.", "A skill waits until someone asks for it. People forget.", "Correct. The note appears for screen files only, in any folder.", "Importing into the root file still loads it for everyone, every time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>conventions belong in a CLAUDE.md near the code</b>. That works when the code sits in one folder. These conventions follow a <i>file name pattern</i> across two trees. Claude Code can load a rule file only when the file being edited matches a glob, wherever it lives.", "diagram": ".claude/rules/a11y.md   paths: **/*.screen.tsx\n  mobile/home.screen.tsx          → loaded\n  packages/shared/ui/x.screen.tsx → loaded\n  server/api.ts                   → not loaded", "mapTitle": "Where does this instruction live?", "map": {"head": ["Where it applies", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Files matching a pattern, in many folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with a <code>paths:</code> glob", "Loads by file, in any folder"], ["Everything inside one folder", "That folder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loads when working there"], ["The whole project, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (or an @import from it)", "Shared with the team through git"], ["Only you, in every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not in git"], ["A task someone runs on request", "A skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team's accessibility conventions apply to every screen file", "means": "The convention follows a kind of file, which is what a <code>paths:</code> glob matches."}, {"quote": "Screen files are named *.screen.tsx and live in mobile/ and packages/shared/ui/", "means": "The decider. Two trees, so folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files (<b>[[0]]</b>) need a copy per tree."}, {"quote": "The conventions sit in the root CLAUDE.md, so they also load during server work", "means": "The goal is to stop loading them everywhere. An @import from the root (<b>[[3]]</b>) still loads everywhere."}, {"quote": "Where should they go?", "means": "A rule that loads by itself for matching files: <b>[[2]]</b>. A skill (<b>[[1]]</b>) needs someone to invoke it."}], "rule": "Convention follows a file pattern across folders → .claude/rules/ with a paths glob.", "guide": {"obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules for conditional convention loading", "quote": "Glob-pattern rules beat directory CLAUDE.md for conventions spanning directories"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s6-01", "was": "m7-s6-01", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Nullable fields against fabrication", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "Many certificates do not show a deductible at all. In the schema, deductible is a required number. For these certificates, the extraction returns 0 or copies the coverage limit into the field. Those values are posted as if they were printed, and reviewers cannot tell them apart from real ones. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add two example certificates with no deductible to the prompt, each with its expected output.", "why": "Works, but only partly. Examples show what to do when no deductible is printed, but while the schema demands a number the model has no honest value to return.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Make deductible nullable in the schema, and state in its description when to return null.", "why": "Best. A required number forces the model to invent one. A nullable field gives it an honest way to say “not printed”."}, {"t": "Keep deductible required and have the model enter -1 whenever no deductible is printed.", "why": "Works, but fragile. -1 marks the gap, but it is still a number: every system downstream must know the convention, or -1 posts as a real value.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Add a <code>deductible_printed</code> flag beside the number, and have reviewers ignore the number when it is false.", "why": "Works, but the invented number stays. The flag tells reviewers to ignore it, but the required field still puts a made-up value in the record, where other systems can use it.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "In the schema, deductible is a required number.", "explanation": "Required fields push the model to invent values when the source lacks them. Optional or nullable fields, with a description of when to leave them empty, prevent that.", "eli5": {"question": "When a certificate has no deductible, the extraction makes one up (0, or the limit). The clue: the schema says the field must always hold a number.", "answer": "A form with a box that must contain a number, and no “not applicable” option, makes people write something, anything. Add “leave blank if not printed” and the made-up values stop. In a JSON schema that's a <b>nullable field</b>. <b>Rule: the model invents values when the schema leaves it no honest answer. Make missing values nullable.</b>", "options": ["Showing examples doesn't help while the form still has no blank option.", "Correct. Let the field be empty when nothing is printed.", "-1 is still a number someone can mistake for a real one.", "A “please ignore” sticker helps, but the made-up number is still on the form."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>made-up values are a prompting problem</b>. Here the schema causes them: a required number has no valid way to say “not printed”, so the model fills it. Fix the schema first; examples help only once the schema allows an honest answer.", "diagram": "required number + nothing printed → model invents 0\nnullable number + nothing printed → null (honest)", "mapTitle": "Extraction output is wrong. Schema, prompt or retry?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Values invented when the source lacks them", "The field is required", "Make it optional or nullable"], ["Categories forced into the nearest option", "Closed enum", "Add <code>other</code> plus a detail field"], ["Field allows null but the model still guesses", "Unclear when to leave it empty", "Describe the rule and add examples"], ["Accuracy drops on unusual layouts", "Too few patterns shown", "Few-shot examples of each layout"], ["A value fails a validation check", "The model misread the document", "Retry with the specific error"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Many certificates do not show a deductible at all", "means": "Missing is a normal case, so the output needs a way to say so."}, {"quote": "In the schema, deductible is a required number", "means": "The decider: the schema forces a number. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) can't fix a schema with no honest answer, and a flag (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the invented number in the record."}, {"quote": "Those values are posted as if they were printed, and reviewers cannot tell them apart from real ones", "means": "Invented values look real. A marker like -1 (<b>[[2]]</b>) has the same problem."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Change the schema: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Model inventing values for missing data? Make the field nullable and say when to use null.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "Optional/nullable fields prevent fabrication"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-10", "was": "m7-s1-10", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Case facts across summarisation", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Some refund disputes go on for a long time. When a chat gets close to the context limit, the SDK replaces older turns with a summary. After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier. It also forgets which items the customer said were missing. Which change <b>best fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Before each refund offer, have the agent call <code>lookup_order</code> again to reload the order and its items.", "why": "Works, but only for what the order system knows. It reloads the order, but the promised amount and the items the customer reported missing were said in the chat, not stored there.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Raise the threshold for summarising older turns, so more of each chat stays word for word.", "why": "Works, but only delays it. More of each chat stays word for word, but long disputes still cross the threshold and the same facts get blurred.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Tell the summariser to quote every amount and item mentioned in the earlier turns.", "why": "Works, but still lossy. A careful summary keeps most amounts, but the facts go through a summary on every pass, and each pass can drop or change them.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Keep a case-facts block of promised amounts, missing items and order IDs in every request.", "why": "Best. Facts kept outside the summarised history survive every summary, and the agent sees them on every turn."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier.", "explanation": "Summarising blurs numbers, dates and promises. Pull them into a persistent case-facts block that is sent with every request instead of being summarised.", "eli5": {"question": "To save space, old parts of the chat get squeezed into a summary. After that, the agent forgets what it promised. The clue: the facts get lost <i>in the summary</i>.", "answer": "When a long meeting is summarised, exact numbers get rounded off. A good assistant keeps a separate card with the key facts: “promised $40 credit; salmon and rice missing”. The summary can be rough; the card stays exact. That card is a <b>case-facts block</b> sent with every request. <b>Rule: numbers and promises that must survive go in a facts block, not the summary.</b>", "options": ["Re-reading the order helps, but the promises were made in the chat, not in the order.", "Summarising later only moves the problem further down the chat.", "A better summary is still a summary; numbers can still slip.", "Correct. Keep the key facts on a separate card that never gets summarised."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better summary</b> will keep the facts. But every summary is a lossy step, and a long chat gets summarised again and again. Exact facts such as amounts, items and promises should not depend on a summary at all. Keep them in a separate block that goes with every request.", "diagram": "older turns   → summary (blurry)     ┐\ncase facts: $40 credit, salmon      ├→ every request\nrecent turns  (word for word)       ┘", "mapTitle": "Facts go missing in a long session. Where are they lost?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Promised amounts or items drift after summarising", "Facts blurred by summaries", "Persistent case-facts block"], ["Context fills fast with long tool output", "Verbose tool results", "Trim results to the needed fields"], ["Details in the middle of a long input are missed", "Lost in the middle", "Key facts first, clear section headers"], ["Claims lose their sources after summarising", "No claim-to-source mapping", "Keep the source with each claim"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When a chat gets close to the context limit, the SDK replaces older turns with a summary", "means": "Facts from early turns now exist only inside summaries."}, {"quote": "After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier", "means": "The decider. The loss starts after summarising, so the summary is the cause. Reloading the order (<b>[[0]]</b>) doesn't bring back what was promised in the chat."}, {"quote": "It also forgets which items the customer said were missing", "means": "More than one kind of fact is lost, so a better summary prompt (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a later threshold (<b>[[1]]</b>) only reduces or delays it."}, {"quote": "Which change best fixes this?", "means": "Take the facts out of the summarised history: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Exact facts that must survive a long chat go into a case-facts block, not into summaries.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Extract transactional facts into a persistent \"case facts\" block"}}}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-1", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Blocking work stays synchronous", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments. Each review request sends every file in the changed modules, though comments go only on changed lines. A manager wants to move the job to the Message Batches API to halve its cost. Which change <b>best</b> cuts the review's cost?", "options": [{"t": "Move the job to the Message Batches API and poll for its results every minute.", "why": "Works, but blocks the merge. It halves the price, but a batch can take up to 24 hours with no latency promise. Polling faster doesn't make it finish sooner.", "trap": "blocks the merge"}, {"t": "Keep the job on the standard API and send only the diff and changed files.", "why": "Best. Developers are waiting, so the review stays synchronous. Most of the tokens are files the review never comments on, so trimming them cuts the cost."}, {"t": "Send the job as a batch, with a timeout that falls back to a standard call.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Developers still wait out the timeout, slow batches end up paid for twice, and there are now two paths to run and test.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Review only larger pull requests, and let small ones merge without a review.", "why": "Works, but drops coverage. It saves money by not doing the job. Small changes can still break things.", "trap": "drops coverage"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments.", "explanation": "Batch processing suits latency-tolerant work, not blocking checks like a pre-merge review. To cut the cost of a blocking job, keep it synchronous and send only what it needs.", "eli5": {"question": "A review that blocks merging costs too much, and a manager wants the cheap batch service. The clue: developers are <i>waiting</i> for it.", "answer": "A half-price dry cleaner that returns shirts “within a day” is no use if you need the shirt for a meeting in an hour. Keep the fast service, and stop sending the whole wardrobe when only one shirt is dirty. <b>Rule: someone waiting → synchronous. To save money, trim what you send.</b>", "options": ["Phoning the slow cleaner every minute doesn't get the shirt back sooner.", "Correct. Fast service, and send only the dirty shirt.", "Trying the slow cleaner first, then paying for express when it's late.", "Saving money by not cleaning some shirts at all."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>batch is simply the cheaper way to call Claude</b>. It is half price because it has no latency promise: results arrive within 24 hours. A check that people wait on can't take that deal. The saving for a blocking job has to come from somewhere else, such as sending fewer tokens.", "diagram": "push -> review (standard API, diff + changed files)\n     -> comments in minutes -> merge", "mapTitle": "Batch or now?", "map": {"head": ["Job", "API", "Why"], "rows": [["Blocks a merge or a person", "Standard (synchronous)", "Batch has no latency promise"], ["Report read days later", "Message Batches API", "Half price, results within 24 hours"], ["Large backfill due next week", "Message Batches API", "Half price, time to spare"], ["Needs tool calls mid-task", "Standard (synchronous)", "A batch request can't loop through tools"], ["Sends far more than it reads", "Trim the request", "Fewer tokens on either API"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments", "means": "The decider: a blocking job. Batch (<b>[[0]]</b>) and batch with a fallback (<b>[[2]]</b>) both make people wait."}, {"quote": "Each review request sends every file in the changed modules, though comments go only on changed lines", "means": "Most of the tokens are never used. Trim them: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Which change best cuts the review's cost?", "means": "Cut cost without dropping reviews, which rules out <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Someone waiting on it? Keep it synchronous, and cut cost by trimming the request.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Good for non-blocking/latency-tolerant (overnight reports, weekly audits); bad for blocking (pre-merge)."}}}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch for latency-tolerant work", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "When a support chat ends, a script sends the transcript to Claude to tag the reason for contact. The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review. Each tag needs a single call with no tools. The script uses the standard Messages API, one call per chat as it ends. Which change <b>best</b> cuts the tagging cost?", "options": [{"t": "Send each day's transcripts every night as one Message Batches API job.", "why": "Best. Nobody reads the tags until Friday, and each tag is a single call. The batch costs half as much, and results are back within a day."}, {"t": "Tag a random sample of chats each week instead of tagging every chat.", "why": "Works, but drops coverage. It saves money by doing less, and the quality team loses the full picture.", "trap": "drops coverage"}, {"t": "Put twenty transcripts into each request to cut the number of calls made.", "why": "Works, but crowds each request. Fewer calls, but still full price, attention spread across twenty chats, and one odd chat can spoil the rest.", "trap": "crowds each request"}, {"t": "Have the support agent tag the reason itself as the last step of each chat.", "why": "Works, but still full price. The tag moves into the live chat, which still pays the standard rate for speed nobody needs.", "trap": "full price"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review.", "explanation": "Work nobody waits on, made of single calls with no tools, belongs on the Message Batches API: half the cost, results within 24 hours.", "eli5": {"question": "Every chat gets tagged straight away at full price, but the tags are only read on Friday. The clue: nobody waits for them.", "answer": "If a letter only has to arrive by Friday, you don't send it by express courier. You drop the day's letters in the cheap bulk mail each evening. The <b>Message Batches API</b> is that bulk mail: half the price, delivered within a day. <b>Rule: nobody waiting + one call each → batch.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Bulk mail each evening, still well before Friday.", "Saving stamps by sending only some of the letters.", "Stuffing twenty letters in one express envelope: still express, and they get mixed up.", "Handing letters to the courier at the door: still express price."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>sending each item as soon as it exists is the normal way</b>. It is only needed when someone is waiting. When results are read days later, the instant answer is speed you pay full price for and never use.", "diagram": "chats end -> collect the day's transcripts\n  -> nightly Message Batches job (custom_id per chat)\n  -> tags ready long before Friday", "mapTitle": "Is anyone waiting?", "map": {"head": ["When the result is used", "API", "Cost"], "rows": [["Seconds later, by a person or a merge", "Standard (synchronous)", "Full price"], ["Next day or later", "Message Batches API", "Half price"], ["Next week, in bulk", "Message Batches API", "Half price"], ["Mid-task, after a tool call", "Standard (synchronous)", "Full price"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review", "means": "The decider: nobody waits for a tag. Instant answers (<b>[[3]]</b>) are speed nobody uses."}, {"quote": "Each tag needs a single call with no tools", "means": "A batch request can do this; it doesn't need a tool loop."}, {"quote": "Which change best cuts the tagging cost?", "means": "Cut cost and still tag every chat: <b>[[0]]</b>, not a sample (<b>[[1]]</b>) or crowded requests (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}], "rule": "Nobody waiting, one call each? Send it as a Message Batches API job at half the cost.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Message Batches API: 50% cost savings, up to 24-hour window, no latency SLA."}}}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-3", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch has no multi-turn tool calling", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Each quarter the team audits about 3,000 past summaries. For each one, the citation-check subagent fetches the cited sections over MCP, then confirms each claim matches its source. Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites. The audit is due in two weeks, and the team wants it to cost less. What is the <b>best</b> approach?", "options": [{"t": "Keep running the audit through the subagent on the standard API, as it runs today.", "why": "Works, but full price. It finishes in time, but nobody needs the answers this week, and every call pays the standard rate.", "trap": "full price"}, {"t": "Fetch the quoted passages in code, then batch one request per summary.", "why": "Best. The summaries already say what to fetch, so code can fetch it. Each request then needs no tools, which a batch can run at half the cost."}, {"t": "Batch one request per summary, and send each tool result back in a follow-up batch.", "why": "Works, but slow round trips. A batch request can't loop through tools, so every fetch becomes a new batch that can take up to 24 hours.", "trap": "slow round trips"}, {"t": "Run several citation-check subagents in parallel to finish the audit within a day.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It is fast, but it pays full price and adds coordination for speed nobody needs.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites.", "explanation": "The Message Batches API does not support multi-turn tool calling in a single request. When the data a step needs is known in advance, fetch it in code and send single-call requests as a batch.", "eli5": {"question": "A big, slow-deadline audit uses tool calls to fetch papers. The clue: each summary already says exactly which sections to fetch.", "answer": "The cheap bulk service can't phone you halfway to ask for a missing page. So put every page it needs in the envelope before you post it. Fetching the sections in code first turns each check into one self-contained request, which the <b>Message Batches API</b> runs at half price. <b>Rule: batch requests can't call tools, so hand them everything up front.</b>", "options": ["Keeps working, at express price, for an audit due in two weeks.", "Correct. Put the pages in the envelope, then use bulk mail.", "Posting a letter, waiting a day for a question, posting the answer, and so on.", "Hiring more couriers to finish in a day when you have two weeks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>an agent job can move to batch as it is</b>. A batch request returns once. If Claude needs a tool result mid-task, the request can't loop. But when the tool calls are predictable, code can make them first, and each request no longer needs a tool.", "diagram": "code: fetch cited sections for each summary\n  -> batch: one request per summary (custom_id)\n  -> results within 24 h, half price", "mapTitle": "Can this job go to batch?", "map": {"head": ["Job shape", "Fit for batch?", "What to do"], "rows": [["One call, no tools, nobody waiting", "Yes", "Batch it"], ["Tool calls Claude must choose as it goes", "No", "Keep it synchronous"], ["Tool calls known in advance", "Yes, after a change", "Fetch in code, then batch"], ["Someone waiting on the answer", "No", "Keep it synchronous"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the citation-check subagent fetches the cited sections over MCP", "means": "Today the job uses tool calls, which a single batch request can't loop through (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites", "means": "The decider: the fetches are known in advance, so code can do them first."}, {"quote": "The audit is due in two weeks, and the team wants it to cost less", "means": "Nobody is waiting. Full price (<b>[[0]]</b>) and parallel speed (<b>[[3]]</b>) buy nothing."}, {"quote": "What is the best approach?", "means": "Fetch in code, then batch: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Batch can't run a tool loop. If the tool calls are predictable, make them in code, then batch.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Batch API does not support multi-turn tool calling in a single request."}}}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-1", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Trim verbose tool results", "trap": "More room instead of less noise", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Customers with several boxes often ask about each one in turn. Each <code>lookup_order</code> result holds about forty fields, such as packing station, nutrition data and courier scans. The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price. In these chats the context fills early, and older turns are summarised after a few lookups. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Move the agent to a model with a larger context window, so summaries start much later.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The window still fills with fields nobody reads, just later, and every turn re-sends all of them.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Keep a case-facts block with each box's items, dates and refund amounts outside the summary.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. The facts survive the summaries, but the window still fills with unused fields and the summaries keep coming.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Cut each order lookup down to the few values the agent needs before Claude reads it.", "why": "Best. It removes the cause: about forty fields shrink to the few the agent needs, so the context stops filling early."}, {"t": "Ask the summariser for a longer, more detailed summary that keeps every order field.", "why": "Works, but makes room tighter. A longer summary keeps more, but it takes space the order fields already crowd, so passes still come early.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price.", "explanation": "Tool results pile up in the context and use tokens out of all proportion to what the agent needs. Trim them to the relevant fields before they accumulate.", "eli5": {"question": "Every order lookup brings back a huge record, and the chat runs out of room. The clue: the agent only ever uses four of the fields.", "answer": "If a nurse stapled every lab printout into the handover folder, the folder would be too thick to read. Copy the four values that matter instead. <b>Trimming tool results</b> is that copy. <b>Rule: if a tool returns far more than the agent uses, cut it down before it enters the context.</b>", "options": ["A bigger folder holds more printouts. It is still full of pages nobody reads.", "Writing the key facts on a chart keeps them safe, but the folder is still overflowing.", "Correct. Copy the values that matter; leave the printout out.", "A longer retelling takes even more room in a folder that is already full."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the window is too small</b>. Here the window is full of things nobody uses. The guide's line: tool results <b>accumulate and consume tokens disproportionately</b>. Fix what goes in, not how much fits.", "diagram": "lookup_order → ~40 fields → [trim to 4] → context\nbefore: window full after a few lookups\nafter:  window stays low, no summary pass", "mapTitle": "Where is the context going, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Window fills after a few tool calls", "Verbose tool results", "Trim results to the relevant fields"], ["Amounts and promises drift after a summary", "Facts retold by each summary", "Case-facts block outside the summary"], ["One long input; middle facts missed", "Lost in the middle", "Key facts first + section headers"], ["Claude Code session full of old output", "Old output still in the window", "/compact, plus a scratchpad file"], ["Wish the window were bigger", "Usually one of the above", "Find which one first"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each lookup_order result holds about forty fields", "means": "The weight is in the tool results, not the chat."}, {"quote": "The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price", "means": "The decider: most of each result is unused. Cut it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "older turns are summarised after a few lookups", "means": "A bigger window (<b>[[0]]</b>) or longer summary (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the unused fields in place. A facts block (<b>[[1]]</b>) protects facts but not the room."}], "rule": "Window filling with tool output? Trim the results to the fields the agent uses.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Tool results accumulate and consume tokens disproportionately (40+ fields when 5 are relevant)"}}}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Facts block across progressive summarisation", "trap": "Better retelling instead of a facts block", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Long evidence runs pass through many search and analysis steps. To stay inside the context limit, the coordinator replaces older steps with a running summary. Committees now read lines such as “a large trial showed a modest benefit”. The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section. Those details are gone by the time the report is written. Which change <b>best fixes</b> this?", "options": [{"t": "Tell the summariser to copy every number, date and citation exactly as it appears.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear instruction cuts the slips, but the summary is still a model rewriting the facts, pass after pass.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Give the coordinator a model with a larger window so it can skip the running summary.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Longer runs still hit the limit, and until then the findings sit in the middle of a very long context.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Have the report subagent rerun the analysis for each paper it cites in the summary.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It recovers the numbers by doing the analysis twice, at the cost of a second pass over every paper.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Keep each finding's numbers, dates and source in a block passed on unsummarised.", "why": "Best. The exact figures live outside the running summary, so no pass can blur them, and the report reads them as they were."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section.", "explanation": "Progressive summarisation condenses numbers, dates and sources into vague text. Keep transactional facts in a persistent structured block that is carried forward unchanged.", "eli5": {"question": "The numbers were right after analysis, then turned into “a large trial” after a few summaries. The clue: the exact details existed and were lost on the way.", "answer": "Retelling a patient's dose each shift turns “40 mg at 6 pm” into “some pain relief”. Writing it on the chart keeps it exact. A <b>facts block</b> passed on unsummarised is the chart. <b>Rule: facts that must stay exact go somewhere no summary rewrites.</b>", "options": ["Asking each nurse to retell carefully helps, but it is still a retelling.", "A longer shift means fewer handovers, not none.", "Re-examining the patient finds the numbers again, but that's a lot of work to redo.", "Correct. Write it on the chart; nobody retells the chart."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better summary keeps the numbers</b>. Each pass is still a model compressing text, and numbers, dates and sources are the first to go. Put them where no summary touches them.", "diagram": "analysis → finding {n, effect, date, paper §}\n  ├─ running summary: rewritten each pass\n  └─ facts block: copied forward unchanged → report", "mapTitle": "Which part of the context keeps a fact exact?", "map": {"head": ["Where the fact lives", "What happens to it", "Use it for"], "rows": [["Running summary", "Rewritten on every pass", "The story of the run"], ["Facts block outside the summary", "Copied forward word for word", "Numbers, dates, sources, promises"], ["Recent turns", "Exact until the next pass", "Current step only"], ["Middle of a very long context", "Easy to miss", "Nothing you need exact"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator replaces older steps with a running summary", "means": "Every fact in the history gets retold, again and again."}, {"quote": "The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section", "means": "The decider: the details existed. They are lost in the retelling, so give them a home outside it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Those details are gone by the time the report is written", "means": "A firmer instruction (<b>[[0]]</b>) lowers the loss rate; a bigger window (<b>[[1]]</b>) postpones it; redoing the analysis (<b>[[2]]</b>) is costly."}], "rule": "Numbers blurring after summaries? Keep them in a facts block the summary never rewrites.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Progressive summarization risks condensing numbers, %, dates, customer expectations into vague summaries"}}}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Scratchpad and /compact in long exploration", "trap": "More room instead of saved findings", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer has spent a long Claude Code session tracing how lessons are priced. Early on, Claude named the exact classes that apply discounts and taxes. Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other. The context is nearly full of earlier output. Several pricing questions are still left. What should the engineer <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude save its key findings to a notes file, then /compact the session.", "why": "Best. The class names and decisions are kept exact in a file, and /compact clears the old output that is crowding them."}, {"t": "Switch the session to a model with a larger context window and keep asking.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The early findings stay buried in the middle of a longer session, and the answers keep drifting.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Start a fresh session and ask Claude to explore the pricing code from scratch.", "why": "Works, but throws away work. The early findings were right; a fresh start pays to rediscover them and may drift again later.", "trap": "throws away work"}, {"t": "Add a CLAUDE.md line asking Claude to name the specific classes in each answer.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The instruction asks for names, but the names it found early are what is getting lost.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other.", "explanation": "Inconsistent answers and talk of typical patterns are signs of context degradation. Persist key findings in a scratchpad file and reduce context use with /compact.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude found the exact classes early, and now speaks vaguely and contradicts itself. The clue: the session is nearly full of old output.", "answer": "When a long meeting runs on, write the decisions on the whiteboard, then clear the table of old papers. A <b>notes file</b> is the whiteboard; <b>/compact</b> clears the table. <b>Rule: save what you found, then make room.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Write the findings down, then clear the clutter.", "A bigger table holds more papers; the decisions are still buried among them.", "Starting the meeting again repeats all the work done so far.", "A rule to quote names helps only if the names are still in front of Claude."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude has forgotten because the window is too small</b>. Vague, contradictory answers are context degradation: early findings are buried under later output. The guide's fix is structure: persist findings in a file, then compact.", "diagram": "early findings → buried under test output\n  → write notes file → /compact → answers cite notes", "mapTitle": "A long Claude Code session is degrading. What fits?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Move", "Why"], "rows": [["Key findings must survive a reset", "Scratchpad file", "Persists across boundaries"], ["Window full of old output", "/compact", "Reduces context usage"], ["Exploration will produce lots of output", "Delegate to a subagent", "Isolates verbose output"], ["Long task may crash midway", "State manifest", "Resume where it stopped"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Early on, Claude named the exact classes that apply discounts and taxes", "means": "The findings exist; they are worth keeping, not rediscovering (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other", "means": "The decider: classic context degradation. More room (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a rule (<b>[[3]]</b>) does not bring the buried findings back."}, {"quote": "The context is nearly full of earlier output", "means": "Save the findings, then compact: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Answers turning vague in a long session? Save findings to a scratchpad file, then /compact.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Scratchpad files persist key findings across boundaries; subagent delegation isolates verbose output"}}}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Narrow task decomposition", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "A committee asks how to prevent falls among hospital inpatients. The coordinator splits the work into three searches: exercise programmes, balance training and strength training. Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic. The committee replies that the summary leaves out bed alarms, medication reviews and ward layout. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give each search subagent wider search terms and a higher limit on papers per topic.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagents. They did their assigned topics well. Wider terms inside “balance training” still won't turn up bed alarms or ward layout.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have synthesis check each draft for missing topics and send out more searches.", "why": "Works, but late. A gap check is a good safety net, yet here it catches the hole only after a full wasted round, and the narrow split repeats on the next request.", "trap": "catches it late"}, {"t": "Have the coordinator split the request by type of prevention first.", "why": "Best. The fault is upstream: all three topics were kinds of exercise. A split by type of prevention (exercise, alarms, medicines, environment) covers the whole question."}, {"t": "Ask the committee to list every subtopic it wants covered in each new request.", "why": "Works, but it moves the coordinator's job onto the committee. Breaking a broad question into parts is exactly what the coordinator is for.", "trap": "shifts the work to the user"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic.", "explanation": "When every subagent does its assigned job well and whole topics are still missing, the coordinator's decomposition was too narrow. Fix the split, not the subagents.", "eli5": {"question": "Every helper did a good job, and the report still misses whole topics. The clue: all three topics were kinds of exercise.", "answer": "A head chef asked for three dishes: soup, salad and bread. All three were perfect, and there was no main course. You don't retrain the cooks; you fix the chef's list. The <b>coordinator</b> is the chef. <b>Rule: if every subagent succeeded and topics are missing, the split was too narrow.</b>", "options": ["Better searching inside “balance training” still never reaches bed alarms.", "A check at the end finds the hole, but only after a wasted round.", "Correct. Fix the list of dishes, not the cooks.", "Asking the guests to write the menu is the chef's job handed back."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a gap in the report means a subagent did a poor job</b>. Here each subagent did exactly what it was asked. The gap was created one step earlier, when the coordinator chose the topics. Overly narrow decomposition gives incomplete coverage even when every subagent works perfectly.", "diagram": "falls prevention\n  -> exercise | balance | strength   (all one kind)\nmissing: alarms, medicines, ward layout", "mapTitle": "Where did the gap start?", "map": {"head": ["Symptom", "Where it started", "Fix there"], "rows": [["Whole topics missing, each subagent fine", "Coordinator's split", "Split the request more broadly"], ["Subagent ignores a rule the user gave", "Coordinator's Task prompt", "Write the rule into the prompt"], ["Subagent's results are poor for its topic", "The subagent itself", "Improve its prompt or tools"], ["Draft has a thin spot on a covered topic", "One round of research", "Gap check, then re-delegate"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator splits the work into three searches: exercise programmes, balance training and strength training", "means": "All three topics are kinds of exercise. The split itself leaves out every other kind of prevention."}, {"quote": "Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic", "means": "The decider: the subagents worked. Tuning them (<b>[[0]]</b>) fixes something that isn't broken."}, {"quote": "the summary leaves out bed alarms, medication reviews and ward layout", "means": "Whole topics, not thin sections. A gap check (<b>[[1]]</b>) would find them, but only after a wasted round."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Fix the split where it was made: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Every subagent succeeded, yet topics are missing? Fix the coordinator's split.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "Risk: overly narrow decomposition → incomplete coverage of broad topics."}}}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Complete prior findings in the Task prompt", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "When the searches finish, the coordinator writes a short paragraph about the papers they found and sends it to the analysis subagent. Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out. The analysis subagent's findings often cite the wrong paper or no section at all. The citation checker then rejects them. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Let the analysis subagent run its own journal searches and find each of the papers again.", "why": "Works, but it repeats finished work. The searches already found the papers; the coordinator just didn't pass them on in a usable form.", "trap": "duplicate work"}, {"t": "Send the findings to it as a list, with paper titles and section numbers as fields.", "why": "Best. The subagent inherits nothing, so the coordinator passes the complete findings, with the citation details kept apart from the text where they can't be lost."}, {"t": "Add a rule to the analysis subagent's prompt to cite a paper and section for each claim.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagent. It can't cite a section number it was never sent. The gap is in the prompt the coordinator wrote.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have the citation checker find the right paper and section for each claim it rejects.", "why": "Works, but at the end of the line. It repairs citations after the fact, one by one, instead of sending the right details in the first place.", "trap": "catches it late"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out.", "explanation": "Subagents receive only what the coordinator writes into the Task prompt. Pass prior agents' complete findings, in a structured form that keeps metadata such as DOI and section apart from the content.", "eli5": {"question": "The analyst cites the wrong papers. The clue: the coordinator's note mixed the titles into sentences and dropped the section numbers.", "answer": "If a manager hands a new colleague a chatty note instead of the file, the colleague can't quote page numbers that weren't in the note. Hand over the file, with the labels on. A subagent starts empty: it knows only the <b>Task prompt</b>. <b>Rule: pass complete findings, with the citation details in their own fields.</b>", "options": ["Sending the colleague to fetch every file again wastes the work already done.", "Correct. Hand over the file with its labels.", "Telling the colleague to quote pages doesn't help if the pages were never handed over.", "Fixing the quotes afterwards is slower than sending them right the first time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a subagent that cites badly needs a stricter instruction</b>. But a subagent knows only what is in its Task prompt. Here the coordinator summarised the search results into prose and dropped the section numbers, so the details the citations need never arrived.", "diagram": "search results -> coordinator prose (no sections)\n               -> analysis subagent -> bad citations", "mapTitle": "What the coordinator should hand on", "map": {"head": ["Need downstream", "Put in the Task prompt", "Why"], "rows": [["Cite paper and section", "Each finding with title, DOI, section fields", "Metadata stays attached to the content"], ["Apply this request's rules", "The rules agreed with the user", "Subagents don't see the coordinator's chat"], ["Build on earlier work", "Complete prior-agent findings", "No re-searching, nothing lost in a summary"], ["Do the job well", "The goal and quality criteria", "Lets the subagent choose how"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator writes a short paragraph about the papers they found", "means": "Prose written by the coordinator, not the complete findings."}, {"quote": "Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out", "means": "The decider: the citation details were mixed in or dropped before the subagent saw them. A citing rule (<b>[[2]]</b>) can't bring them back."}, {"quote": "every claim must cite a paper and section", "means": "The details that went missing are exactly the ones every claim needs."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Send the findings complete, with metadata in separate fields: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Subagents inherit nothing. Pass complete findings, with citation details in their own fields.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Include complete prior-agent findings in the subagent prompt; use structured formats to separate content from metadata (URLs, doc names, page numbers) for attribution"}}}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-3", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Goals and quality criteria, not step lists", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The coordinator writes each search Task prompt as fixed steps: search the drug name with the outcome, take the first ten results, and summarise each one. Subagents follow the steps exactly. They miss trials that use a brand name or another word for the outcome. They also summarise reviews the committee did not need. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "State the goal and what counts as good evidence, and let each subagent plan its searches.", "why": "Best. With the goal and a quality bar, a subagent can adapt: try brand names, skip reviews nobody asked for, and stop when the evidence is good enough."}, {"t": "Add steps to also search brand names and outcome synonyms, and take the first twenty results.", "why": "Works, but it patches the list. This request's misses go away; the next request will find a gap the steps didn't foresee.", "trap": "patches the step list"}, {"t": "Give the search subagents a synonym tool that expands each drug name before they search.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagents' tools. Brand names get found; the subagents still take the first ten results and still don't know which papers the committee needs.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have the analysis subagent drop summarised papers that don't answer the committee's question.", "why": "Works, but late, and only half. It removes unneeded reviews after they were summarised, and it can't add the trials the steps missed.", "trap": "catches it late"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Subagents follow the steps exactly.", "explanation": "Coordinator prompts should state goals and quality criteria, not step-by-step procedures. Subagents that know what good looks like can adapt their searches; subagents given a recipe follow it, gaps and all.", "eli5": {"question": "The helpers did exactly what the steps said, and missed what mattered. The clue: they follow the steps exactly.", "answer": "Tell a helper “buy the first ten items on aisle 3” and they'll come back with ten items. Tell them “we're cooking for six, one is vegetarian, keep it under $40” and they'll shop well. The <b>goal and quality bar</b> let them adapt. <b>Rule: brief subagents with goals and quality criteria, not recipes.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Say what you need and what good looks like.", "A longer recipe fixes today's miss; tomorrow brings a new one.", "A better tool helps, but the helper still follows the recipe.", "Throwing out the wrong shopping later doesn't bring back what was never bought."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a subagent that misses things needs more instructions or a better tool</b>. Here the subagents followed their steps exactly; the steps were the problem. A step list can't foresee every request. A goal and a quality bar let the subagent decide how to search.", "diagram": "steps:  search X -> first 10 -> summarise  (rigid)\ngoal:   what the committee needs + quality bar\n        -> subagent plans its own searches", "mapTitle": "Recipe or goal?", "map": {"head": ["Brief style", "What the subagent does", "Result"], "rows": [["Fixed steps", "Follows them exactly", "Misses whatever the steps didn't foresee"], ["Longer fixed steps", "Follows more of them", "Fixes today's miss only"], ["Goal + quality criteria", "Plans and adapts its searches", "Finds what the goal needs"], ["Goal only, no criteria", "Adapts, but guesses the bar", "Uneven depth and sources"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator writes each search Task prompt as fixed steps", "means": "The brief is a recipe. The coordinator decides how, so the subagent can't adapt."}, {"quote": "Subagents follow the steps exactly", "means": "The decider: the subagents did what they were told. A new tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) works on the wrong layer."}, {"quote": "They miss trials that use a brand name or another word for the outcome", "means": "More steps (<b>[[1]]</b>) cover this case; the next request brings another one."}, {"quote": "They also summarise reviews the committee did not need", "means": "They don't know what the committee needs. Filtering later (<b>[[3]]</b>) can't add what was missed."}], "rule": "Brief subagents with the goal and quality criteria, not a step list.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "write coordinator prompts as goals + quality criteria, not step-by-step procedures."}}}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-1", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 tool_use with a JSON schema", "trap": "Fixing text instead of using a tool", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An internal Agent SDK script reads each incident write-up in Linear. It asks Claude to reply with a JSON object holding the service, severity, start time and root cause. A parser then reads the reply. On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket. The parser skips those incidents. The values themselves are usually right. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a complete example reply to the prompt that shows the JSON and nothing else.", "why": "Works, but only usually. An example cuts the stray sentences, but text replies can still break, and each break loses an incident.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Resend any reply that fails to parse, along with the error from the parser.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. The retry usually fixes the reply, but every broken reply costs a second call that a schema would avoid.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Define a <code>record_incident</code> tool with a JSON schema and have Claude call it.", "why": "Best. Tool input arrives as structured JSON that matches the schema, so syntax errors and stray sentences go away at the source."}, {"t": "Make the parser cut out the text between the first and last brace before reading.", "why": "Works, but only for one kind of break. It drops the stray sentence, but a missing bracket still fails, and the next new quirk will too.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket.", "explanation": "Tool use with a JSON schema is the most reliable way to get schema-compliant output; it removes JSON syntax errors. Prompts, retries and lenient parsers only reduce them.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude writes the JSON as text, and sometimes the text breaks. The clue: the values are right; only the format fails.", "answer": "Asking someone to write a form out by hand, neatly, mostly works. Handing them a form with boxes always gives you the boxes. A <b>tool with a JSON schema</b> is the form with boxes. <b>Rule: if structured output breaks as text, return it through a tool.</b>", "options": ["A neat example helps, but handwriting still slips sometimes.", "Sending messy forms back works, but costs a second round each time.", "Correct. Use the form with boxes.", "Tidying up the handwriting fixes one kind of mess, not every kind."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>better instructions will fix the format</b>. Text replies are probabilistic; a tool call with a JSON schema is structured by design. The guide: tool use with a schema <b>eliminates JSON syntax errors</b>. It does not fix wrong values, but here the values are fine.", "diagram": "text reply:  “Here it is: {…”  → parser fails\ntool call:   record_incident({service, …})\n             → structured input, no parsing", "mapTitle": "What goes wrong, and which layer fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["Problem", "Layer", "Fix"], "rows": [["JSON breaks: stray text, missing bracket", "Output format", "Tool with a JSON schema"], ["Field has no value on the page", "Schema design", "Optional or nullable field"], ["Category not in the list", "Schema design", "Enum with other + detail"], ["Value on the page, extracted wrong", "Validation loop", "Retry with the specific error"], ["Odd layouts read wrong", "Prompt", "Worked examples of those layouts"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It asks Claude to reply with a JSON object", "means": "The output is text, so its syntax can break."}, {"quote": "On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket", "means": "The decider: syntax failures. A tool call removes them: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The values themselves are usually right", "means": "Nothing to fix in the content. An example (<b>[[0]]</b>), a retry (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a lenient parser (<b>[[3]]</b>) works around the format instead of removing the cause."}], "rule": "Structured output breaking as text? Return it through a tool with a JSON schema.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "tool_use with JSON schemas = most reliable schema-compliant output (eliminates JSON syntax errors)"}}}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Nullable fields against fabrication", "trap": "Forcing a value that isn't there", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent extracts each paper's details through a tool with a JSON schema. The schema makes <code>trial_registration_id</code> a required string. Many older papers do not print a registration ID. For those papers, the subagent returns IDs in the right format that do not exist. Reviewers then spend time searching the registry for them. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a prompt rule telling the subagent not to guess an ID that isn't printed.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The rule lowers the guessing, but the schema still demands a string, so the model still has to put something there.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Make <code>trial_registration_id</code> nullable, to be left null when not printed.", "why": "Best. The schema now has an honest answer for “not printed”, so the model has no reason to invent an ID."}, {"t": "Have the citation-check subagent look up every extracted ID in the registry.", "why": "Works, but catches it late. The lookup finds the fake IDs, but they are still invented first, and every paper costs an extra registry call.", "trap": "catches it late"}, {"t": "Add worked examples of papers both with and without a printed registration ID.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples show the right behaviour, but a required field still leaves no valid way to say “none”.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The schema makes trial_registration_id a required string.", "explanation": "When a field may be absent from the source, make it optional or nullable. A required field pushes the model to fabricate a plausible value.", "eli5": {"question": "Old papers have no registration ID, but the form insists on one, so the model makes one up. The clue: the field is required.", "answer": "A screen that won't save until every box is filled gets fake answers in the boxes. Add a “not given” option and people stop faking. A <b>nullable field</b> is that option. <b>Rule: if a value may be missing, let the schema say so.</b>", "options": ["Asking people not to fake helps, but the box still has to be filled.", "Correct. Give the box a “not given” option.", "Checking every answer later finds the fakes after they were made.", "Showing examples helps, but the screen still won't save an empty box."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the model needs to be told not to invent</b>. Here the schema is what demands a value. Give the schema an honest answer for “not printed” and the pressure to invent goes away.", "diagram": "required string, nothing printed → plausible fake ID\nnullable, nothing printed        → null (honest)", "mapTitle": "A value may be missing. How does the schema handle it?", "map": {"head": ["Schema choice", "What the model returns", "Effect"], "rows": [["Required field", "A plausible value", "Fabrication that looks real"], ["Sentinel such as 0 or N/A", "A fake value", "Read downstream as real"], ["Optional or nullable field", "null", "Honest gap, easy to route"], ["Enum with other + detail", "other, plus what it is", "Honest new category"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The schema makes trial_registration_id a required string", "means": "The decider: the schema has no valid way to say “not printed”. Make it nullable: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Many older papers do not print a registration ID", "means": "The value is absent from the source, so a required field forces a guess."}, {"quote": "the subagent returns IDs in the right format that do not exist", "means": "Fabrication. A rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) or examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) fight the schema; a registry check (<b>[[2]]</b>) catches fakes after they're made."}], "rule": "A value may be missing? Make the field optional or nullable, not required.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "Optional/nullable fields prevent fabrication; enum \"unclear\"/\"other\" + detail for extensible categories"}}}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-3", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Retries can't recover absent information", "trap": "Retrying the impossible", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The broker's system needs a policy number on every certificate. When none is extracted, the pipeline retries with the specific error, up to two times. On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge. These fail each retry. Sometimes the last retry returns a number that is not on the page, and it gets posted. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Raise the retry limit and say in the message that the number must be found.", "why": "Works, but presses harder for the impossible. More retries help only when the number is on the page; here they invite more invented numbers.", "trap": "retrying the impossible"}, {"t": "Check each policy number against the carrier's format before it is posted.", "why": "Works, but catches only some cases. A broken number fails the check, but an invented one in the right format passes.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}, {"t": "Add worked examples that show where each carrier prints its policy number.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. Examples help find a number that is printed somewhere unusual; this one is not on the page.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Return null when the policy number isn't printed and send it to a person.", "why": "Best. A retry can't recover what the scan lost. Null says so honestly, and a person can get the number from the carrier."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge.", "explanation": "Retries with error feedback fix values that are in the document. When the information is absent from the source, retries fail or invite fabrication; return null and route to human review.", "eli5": {"question": "The policy number is torn off the scan, and the system keeps asking for it until the model makes one up. The clue: the number isn't on the page.", "answer": "If the date is torn off a form, telling the temp “find it” three times won't bring it back. It might get you a made-up date. Hand that form to someone who can phone the customer. <b>Rule: retry only when the answer is on the page. If it isn't, say so and send it to a person.</b>", "options": ["Asking more firmly makes a made-up answer more likely, not a real one.", "Checking the format catches messy numbers, not neat fakes.", "Examples help find a number printed in an odd place, not one that's missing.", "Correct. Say it's missing and hand it to someone who can find it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a specific retry fixes any validation failure</b>. It fixes values that are on the page but extracted wrong. When the information is absent, every retry fails, and pressure to fill the field produces an invention.", "diagram": "scan: Policy NG-44▒▒▒▒\n  retry “policy number required” → NG-448126 (fake)\n  null + person                  → real number found", "mapTitle": "Validation failed. Is the value on the page?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["On the page, wrong field or format", "Retry with the specific error", "The model can correct it"], ["On the page, odd layouts every time", "Worked examples", "Fixes the first pass"], ["Not on the page", "Null, then a person", "No retry can find it"], ["Invented value in the right format", "Not caught by format checks", "Prevent it at the schema"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the pipeline retries with the specific error", "means": "The retry is already the good kind. Its content is not the problem."}, {"quote": "On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge", "means": "The decider: the information is absent from the source. Examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) and more retries (<b>[[0]]</b>) can't bring it back."}, {"quote": "the last retry returns a number that is not on the page, and it gets posted", "means": "Fabrication under pressure. A format check (<b>[[1]]</b>) passes a well-formed fake. Return null and route it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Value missing from the source? Don't retry; return null and send it to a person.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Retries are ineffective when info is absent from the source (vs format/structural errors)"}}}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-1", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 User-level memory for personal preferences", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "A developer added a line to the committed root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. It says: “Explain every change in detail, I am new to Kotlin.” Now the CI review job posts long teaching comments on every pull request. Teammates' own sessions have started explaining everything too. The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions. Where should the line <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches only the Kotlin files.", "why": "Works, but still shared. The rule is committed, so CI and teammates load it whenever a Kotlin file is touched, which is most of the time.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In the developer's own <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, removed from the committed project file.", "why": "Best. User-level memory is personal and not shared through git. It loads in every one of the developer's sessions, in every project, and nobody else's."}, {"t": "In a project skill that the developer invokes at the start of each of their sessions.", "why": "Works, but only when remembered. It is also committed, so the skill shows up for the whole team.", "trap": "only when invoked"}, {"t": "In the CI job's <code>-p</code> prompt, as an override asking for short review comments.", "why": "Works, but for CI only. The review gets shorter, yet teammates' sessions still load the line from the root file.", "trap": "treats one symptom"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions.", "explanation": "A personal preference belongs in user-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. It is not shared through version control, so it changes only that person's sessions.", "eli5": {"question": "One developer's personal wish (“explain everything to me”) went into the shared file. Now CI and teammates get it too.", "answer": "A sticky note on the office noticeboard is read by everyone. A note in your own notebook is read only by you, wherever you go. The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is the noticeboard; <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> is your notebook. <b>Rule: only for me → user-level memory. For the team → the project file.</b>", "options": ["A note that pops up on Kotlin files is still on the noticeboard: CI and teammates see it.", "Correct. Your own notebook: you get it everywhere, and nobody else does.", "A note you must remember to fetch each morning, and the whole team can see it.", "Telling CI to ignore it helps CI, but teammates still read the noticeboard."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the problem is CI's output</b>, so the fix goes in CI. The real fault is the <i>home</i> of the line: a personal wish sits in a file that every clone and every CI run loads. Move it to the file that only this person loads.", "diagram": "~/.claude/CLAUDE.md  → this developer, every project\nrepo/CLAUDE.md       → every teammate and every CI run", "mapTitle": "Who should this instruction reach?", "map": {"head": ["Who it is for", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Only me, in every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not in git"], ["The whole team and CI, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Committed, loads for everyone"], ["Files matching a pattern", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Committed, loads for matching files"], ["A task run on request", "A skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"], ["One CI job's run", "That job's <code>-p</code> prompt", "Affects that run only"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A developer added a line to the committed root CLAUDE.md", "means": "Committed means shared: every clone and CI load it."}, {"quote": "Teammates' own sessions have started explaining everything too", "means": "The leak is not only in CI, so a CI-only override (<b>[[3]]</b>) fixes one symptom."}, {"quote": "The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions", "means": "The decider: personal, and in every project. A committed rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) or skill (<b>[[2]]</b>) is still shared, and stays in this repo."}, {"quote": "Where should the line live?", "means": "In the file only this developer loads: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Only for me, everywhere → ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. For the team and CI → the committed project CLAUDE.md.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "User-level is not shared via version control."}}}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-2", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Project commands shared through git", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer wrote a <code>/new-screen</code> command that creates a screen, its test and its story in the team's layout. It lives in their <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>. Teammates copied it from a chat message, and the copies have drifted, so new screens come out in different layouts. The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code. Where should the command <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In the repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code> folder, committed alongside the code.", "why": "Best. Project commands live in the repository, so every engineer gets the same version on clone or pull, and changes go through review."}, {"t": "In the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, as a section that lists the steps for a new screen.", "why": "Works, but wrong kind of file. Claude can follow the steps, but they load into every session and there is no <code>/new-screen</code> to run.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches <code>*.screen.tsx</code>.", "why": "Works, but loads too late. A path rule loads when a matching file is touched, and the screen does not exist until the command creates it.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In a team dotfiles repo that copies it into each engineer's <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>.", "why": "Works, but outside the project. Each engineer must install and update the dotfiles, so versions can drift again.", "trap": "copies to maintain"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code.", "explanation": "A command the whole team should share lives in the project's .claude/commands/, committed to git. ~/.claude/commands/ is for personal commands.", "eli5": {"question": "A handy command lives on one person's laptop. Everyone copied it, and the copies no longer match.", "answer": "If the whole crew needs the same tool, leave it in the shared toolshed on site, not in one person's van. The repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code> is the shared toolshed: whoever clones gets the same tool, and any change is reviewed. <b>Rule: a team command → <code>.claude/commands/</code> in the repo. A personal command → <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>.</b>", "options": ["Correct. One tool in the shared toolshed; everyone gets the same one.", "Written instructions on the wall aren't a tool you can pick up, and everyone reads them all day.", "A note that appears only next to finished screens can't help you make a new one.", "Copying the tool into every van works until someone forgets to update theirs."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the drift is a sharing problem to solve with a sync tool</b>. Claude Code already has a shared home for commands: the repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code>. Committed there, the command arrives with the code and changes go through review.", "diagram": "repo/.claude/commands/new-screen.md → every engineer\n~/.claude/commands/new-screen.md     → one engineer", "mapTitle": "Who needs the command, and where does it live?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Whole team, this repository", "<code>.claude/commands/</code>, committed", "Same version for everyone, reviewed"], ["Only me, every repository", "<code>~/.claude/commands/</code>", "Personal, follows me"], ["A standard that always applies", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded, not invoked"], ["Conventions for a kind of file", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads when a matching file is touched"], ["Shared copies kept in sync by a script", "Dotfiles repo", "Outside the project; can drift"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It lives in their ~/.claude/commands/", "means": "A user-level command: personal, so teammates had to copy it."}, {"quote": "the copies have drifted, so new screens come out in different layouts", "means": "Any copy-based fix, such as a dotfiles sync (<b>[[3]]</b>), keeps the copies."}, {"quote": "The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code", "means": "The decider: shared through the repository. A CLAUDE.md section (<b>[[1]]</b>) is shared but isn't a command."}, {"quote": "Where should the command live?", "means": "It must run on request before any screen exists, which rules out a path rule (<b>[[2]]</b>). Answer: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Team command → .claude/commands/ in the repo. Personal command → ~/.claude/commands/.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Project commands in .claude/commands/ (shared) vs user ~/.claude/commands/ (personal)"}}}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 @import for modular references", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team's API style guide lives in <code>docs/api-style.md</code>, and the API team updates it often. The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> holds a pasted copy of the guide. That copy is months out of date, so Claude follows rules the guide has since changed. The guide applies to every endpoint in the app. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Replace the copy with an <code>@import</code> line naming the guide file in <code>docs/</code>.", "why": "Best. The import pulls the live guide into memory, so there is one copy to maintain and Claude always reads the current rules."}, {"t": "Add a CI step that copies the latest guide into <code>CLAUDE.md</code> after every merge.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It keeps the copy fresh with a moving part you must build and maintain, when an import line does it with no copy.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Move the guide into an <code>/api-style</code> skill that engineers invoke for endpoint work.", "why": "Works, but only when invoked. Endpoints written without the skill miss the guide, and the skill is another copy.", "trap": "only when invoked"}, {"t": "Paste the guide into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with a <code>paths:</code> glob on the controllers.", "why": "Works, but still a copy. It loads more precisely, yet it drifts from <code>docs/</code> exactly like the pasted copy did.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "That copy is months out of date, so Claude follows rules the guide has since changed.", "explanation": "@import lets CLAUDE.md reference a file instead of copying it, so memory stays in step with the source document.", "eli5": {"question": "The rule book was photocopied into Claude's notes. The real rule book changed; the photocopy didn't.", "answer": "Instead of a photocopy pinned to the wall, pin a note that says “read the rule book on shelf 3”. Whoever reads the note always gets the current book. An <code>@import</code> line in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is that note. <b>Rule: the content already lives in a file → import it, don't copy it.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Point at the real book, so there's nothing to keep in sync.", "A machine that re-photocopies the book after every change works, but it's a machine to keep running.", "Locking the book in a cupboard means people only read it when they remember to open the cupboard.", "A neater photocopy in a better spot is still a photocopy that goes stale."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>this is about where the guide should load</b>. The guide applies to every endpoint, so always-loaded memory is already the right place. The fault is that memory holds a <i>copy</i>. <code>@import</code> makes <code>CLAUDE.md</code> point at the source file instead.", "diagram": "CLAUDE.md:  @docs/api-style.md\n            ↓ loads the current file\ndocs/api-style.md  (the API team edits only this)", "mapTitle": "Copy it, import it, or load it somewhere else?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Content already lives in a repo file", "<code>@import</code> it from <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "One source, no copy to drift"], ["Conventions for a file pattern across folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads only for matching files"], ["A workflow run now and then", "A skill", "Loads only when invoked"], ["Only you need it", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not shared"], ["Memory doesn't seem to load", "Check with <code>/memory</code>", "Shows which files loaded"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team's API style guide lives in docs/api-style.md, and the API team updates it often", "means": "The source of truth is a file that changes."}, {"quote": "The root CLAUDE.md holds a pasted copy of the guide", "means": "Memory holds a copy, not the source."}, {"quote": "That copy is months out of date", "means": "The decider: drift. Another copy (<b>[[2]]</b>, <b>[[3]]</b>) drifts the same way; a sync job (<b>[[1]]</b>) is extra machinery."}, {"quote": "The guide applies to every endpoint in the app", "means": "Always-loaded memory is the right place; only the copy is wrong. Import the file: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Content already in a repo file? @import it from CLAUDE.md instead of pasting a copy.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "@import syntax for modular references"}}}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-1", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 Project .mcp.json with env var expansion", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "One engineer connected Claude to the Linear MCP server through their own <code>~/.claude.json</code>, with their API key written in it. Teammates now want the same Linear tools, and so does a new CI job. Each person and the CI job have their own key. No key may be committed to the repository. How should the team <b>set it up</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have each engineer add the server to their own <code>~/.claude.json</code>, as a README describes.", "why": "Works, but personal. Each engineer configures it by hand, the entries drift, and the CI job needs its own setup too.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Add the server to a committed <code>.mcp.json</code> that reads the key from <code>${LINEAR_API_KEY}</code>.", "why": "Best. Project <code>.mcp.json</code> is shared through git, so every clone and CI get the server, and each sets the variable from its own environment or secret store."}, {"t": "Add a script to the repo that calls the Linear API, and let Claude run it through Bash.", "why": "Works, but loses the MCP tools. Claude gets one script instead of described tools with structured errors, and the team maintains it.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Copy the engineer's server entry into the CI runner's <code>~/.claude.json</code> as well.", "why": "Works, but for CI only. Teammates still have nothing, and the server now has two hand-made entries to keep in step.", "trap": "covers only CI"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "No key may be committed to the repository.", "explanation": "Shared MCP servers belong in the project-level .mcp.json. Env var expansion such as ${LINEAR_API_KEY} keeps secrets out of the committed file.", "eli5": {"question": "One engineer's laptop knows how to reach Linear. The team and CI want it too, but nobody's key may go into the repo.", "answer": "Put the address book in the shared office, but leave a blank where the door code goes. Each person fills in their own code from their own pocket. The committed <code>.mcp.json</code> is the shared address book; <code>${LINEAR_API_KEY}</code> is the blank. <b>Rule: a server the team shares → project <code>.mcp.json</code>, with secrets as environment variables.</b>", "options": ["Everyone writing their own address book works, but they drift, and CI needs one too.", "Correct. One shared address book, with each person's code filled in from their own pocket.", "A hand-written phone script works, but it is clumsier than the real phone and someone has to maintain it.", "Giving a copy to CI helps CI only; the teammates still have nothing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a secret means the config must stay personal</b>. Claude Code separates the two: the server entry goes in the committed project <code>.mcp.json</code>, and the secret stays outside git as an environment variable that the file expands.", "diagram": ".mcp.json (in git):  \"LINEAR_API_KEY\": \"${LINEAR_API_KEY}\"\nlaptop env / CI secret → fills in the real key", "mapTitle": "Who needs the server, and where is it configured?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Whole team and CI, needs a secret", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code> with <code>${VAR}</code>", "Shared config, no committed secret"], ["Whole team, no secret needed", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code>", "Arrives with the clone"], ["Only me, or an experiment", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal, not shared"], ["A standard service like Jira or Linear", "An existing community server", "No custom code to maintain"], ["Only one CI job needs it", "Still <code>.mcp.json</code>; CI sets the variable", "One config for all"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "One engineer connected Claude to the Linear MCP server through their own ~/.claude.json", "means": "User-level config: personal, so nobody else has it."}, {"quote": "Teammates now want the same Linear tools, and so does a new CI job", "means": "Shared need. Per-person entries (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a CI-only copy (<b>[[3]]</b>) each cover part of it."}, {"quote": "No key may be committed to the repository", "means": "The decider: the committed file names a variable, not the key: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "How should the team set it up?", "means": "Keep the MCP server and its tools; a Bash script (<b>[[2]]</b>) replaces them with something weaker."}], "rule": "Team-shared MCP server → project .mcp.json, secrets as ${VARS}. Personal or experimental → ~/.claude.json.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Env var expansion in .mcp.json (e.g., ${GITHUB_TOKEN}) — no committed secrets."}}}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-2", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 Community MCP servers for standard integrations", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The review job should read the Jira ticket linked in each pull request and check the change against it. It also needs to search related tickets and add a comment. These are ordinary Jira tasks. A developer plans to write the team's own MCP server for them. What is the <b>best approach</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Write the planned server, with detailed tool descriptions and structured errors.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A well-made custom server does the job, but the team now builds and maintains a Jira client that already exists.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Give the job a script that calls the Jira REST API with <code>curl</code> through Bash.", "why": "Works, but fragile. Claude gets raw HTTP output instead of described tools, and the script grows with every new need.", "trap": "fragile"}, {"t": "Configure an existing community Jira MCP server in the project <code>.mcp.json</code>.", "why": "Best. Jira is a standard integration with maintained community servers. Configured in the project file, the review job and every developer get it."}, {"t": "Add the Jira calls as new tools on the team's existing code-search MCP server.", "why": "Works, but still custom code. There is no new server, yet the team writes and maintains Jira code that already exists, inside a server built for something else.", "trap": "still custom code"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "These are ordinary Jira tasks.", "explanation": "For standard integrations such as Jira, prefer an existing community MCP server over writing a custom one.", "eli5": {"question": "The team needs Claude to read and comment on Jira tickets, and someone wants to build a connector from scratch.", "answer": "If you need a kettle, you buy a good one from the shop; you don't build one from parts. Jira is the kettle: lots of teams need the same thing, so a well-tested <b>community MCP server</b> already exists. Build your own only for systems nobody else has. <b>Rule: standard integration → community server; your own system → your own server.</b>", "options": ["A hand-built kettle works, but you'll be fixing it for years.", "Boiling water over a campfire works, but it's clumsy every time.", "Correct. Buy the good kettle everyone uses, and put it in the shared kitchen.", "Bolting a hand-built kettle onto the toaster: still yours to fix."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a custom server is the careful choice</b> because you control it. For standard integrations the exam guide says the opposite: prefer a community MCP server. Custom code is for your own systems, where nothing exists to adopt.", "mapTitle": "Adopt, build, or work around?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best choice", "Why"], "rows": [["Standard service (Jira, GitHub)", "Community MCP server", "Already built and maintained"], ["Your own internal system", "Your own MCP server", "Nothing exists to adopt"], ["The team and CI need it", "Configure it in project <code>.mcp.json</code>", "Shared on clone"], ["Only you, trying it out", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal"], ["Claude explores to learn what exists", "Add an MCP resource", "A catalog cuts exploratory calls"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The review job uses an MCP server for code search and API guidelines", "means": "The team already works through MCP; adding another server fits."}, {"quote": "It also needs to search related tickets and add a comment", "means": "It must act, not only read, so it needs real Jira tools."}, {"quote": "These are ordinary Jira tasks", "means": "The decider: a standard integration, so a community server exists."}, {"quote": "A developer plans to write the team's own MCP server for them", "means": "Building it (<b>[[0]]</b>), bolting it onto another server (<b>[[3]]</b>) or scripting around it (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more to maintain. Adopt: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Standard integration (Jira)? Configure a community MCP server; build your own only for your own systems.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "prefer community MCP servers for standard integrations (Jira)"}}}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP resources for schema catalogs", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Engineers ask Claude questions about data in the staging database. Before each answer, Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist. Most of those queries find nothing useful. The database has several hundred tables, and its structure changes only when a migration runs. What is the <b>best way</b> to cut these exploratory calls?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>describe_table</code> tool that returns the columns of one named table.", "why": "Works, but still exploring. Claude must already know a table's name, so it still calls once per guess.", "trap": "still exploratory"}, {"t": "Paste the full schema into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for every session to load.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Hundreds of tables load into every session, even ones about other work, and the copy drifts after each migration.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Expand the query tool's description with sample queries on the system tables.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. The queries get better aimed, but Claude still runs them to explore.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Expose the schema as an MCP resource: tables, columns and a summary of each.", "why": "Best. A resource is a catalog Claude reads when it needs it. One read shows what exists, so the exploratory queries go away."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist.", "explanation": "MCP resources expose content catalogs, such as schemas, so the agent can see what exists without exploratory tool calls.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude keeps knocking on doors to find out which rooms the building has.", "answer": "Give it the floor plan instead. A floor plan on the wall shows every room at a glance, and you only walk to the room you need. An <b>MCP resource</b> is that floor plan: a catalog Claude can read. Tools are the doors you open to <i>do</i> something. <b>Rule: Claude explores to learn what exists → publish a resource.</b>", "options": ["A door you can open only if you know the room's name: still guessing.", "Printing the whole floor plan on everyone's desk every day, even when they work elsewhere.", "Better knocking technique, but still knocking.", "Correct. One floor plan, read once, and no more knocking."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every MCP feature is a tool</b>, so the fix must be a better or new tool. MCP has a second primitive: a <b>resource</b>, content the client can read, such as a schema or a document list. It answers “what exists?” in one read, which is exactly what the exploratory queries were doing.", "diagram": "Today:  query, query, query … (most find nothing)\nAfter:  read schema resource → one targeted query", "mapTitle": "Tool, resource, or memory?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "MCP primitive", "Why"], "rows": [["Learn what exists (schemas, doc lists)", "Resource", "A catalog read once"], ["Do something with inputs (run a query)", "Tool", "An action with parameters"], ["Claude picks Grep over a better tool", "Better tool description", "Descriptions drive selection"], ["A fact every session needs, short and stable", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded"], ["Standard service such as Jira", "Community server", "Adopt, don't build"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist", "means": "The decider: the calls are exploration. Better-aimed queries (<b>[[2]]</b>) are still exploration."}, {"quote": "Most of those queries find nothing useful", "means": "Wasted calls, not wrong answers. The fix is to make the calls unnecessary."}, {"quote": "The database has several hundred tables", "means": "Too big for always-loaded memory (<b>[[1]]</b>), and too many to guess one by one (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "What is the best way to cut these exploratory calls?", "means": "A catalog Claude reads when needed: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Claude explores to learn what exists? Publish an MCP resource (a catalog); keep tools for actions.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "MCP resources expose content catalogs (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, schemas) to reduce exploratory tool calls."}}}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-1", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Direct execution for a well-scoped change", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "A ticket asks for one new check on the sign-up endpoint in <code>server/</code>. Passwords shorter than twelve characters must be rejected with the team's usual error response. The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists. How should the engineer <b>approach</b> this with Claude?", "options": [{"t": "Enter plan mode, have Claude survey the auth code, then approve a written plan.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The plan would say what the ticket already says. Plan mode is for open design choices across many files, and here there are none.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Send an Explore subagent to map every input check in <code>server/</code>, then edit.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The Explore subagent keeps long discovery output out of the session. Here there is nothing to discover: the engineer already knows both files.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Ask Claude to add the check and a test case directly, then run the tests.", "why": "Best. One rule, two known files, an existing error format. A simple, well-scoped change like this is what direct execution is for."}, {"t": "Have Claude interview the engineer about password rules before writing code.", "why": "Works, but nothing is left to ask. The interview pattern draws out unclear requirements. The ticket already states the rule and the error.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists.", "explanation": "Direct execution fits simple, well-scoped changes such as a single validation check. Plan mode fits large changes with several valid approaches or architectural decisions.", "eli5": {"question": "A small, clear ticket: add one password check to one endpoint. The clue: the engineer already knows which files to change, and the error format exists.", "answer": "Hanging one picture doesn't need an architect. You know the wall, you have the nail, you hang it. <b>Direct execution</b> is hanging the picture: Claude makes the change and runs the tests. <b>Rule: small and settled → just do it. Big or undecided → plan first.</b>", "options": ["Drawing up a plan for one picture: correct in the end, but slow for no gain.", "Sending someone to measure every wall when you already know which wall.", "Correct. Make the change, add a test, run the tests.", "Asking questions when the ticket has already answered them."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>planning first is always the safer choice</b>. Plan mode costs time: Claude explores, writes a plan, and waits for approval. That pays off when the approach is open or many files change. For one clear rule in two known files, the plan adds time and changes nothing.", "diagram": "known files + settled rule\n  -> edit endpoint + add test case\n  -> run tests -> done", "mapTitle": "Which mode fits the task?", "map": {"head": ["Task looks like", "Mode", "Why"], "rows": [["One rule, known files, settled approach", "Direct execution", "Nothing to decide, so just do it"], ["Many files and two valid approaches", "Plan mode", "Agree the design before any edits"], ["Discovery output would flood the session", "Explore subagent", "Returns a summary, not the raw output"], ["Requirements are unclear", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks questions first"], ["Known right answers, fixes keep breaking", "Tests first, share failures", "Each round has a clear target"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A ticket asks for one new check on the sign-up endpoint", "means": "One rule in one place: a small, well-scoped change."}, {"quote": "The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists", "means": "The decider. There is nothing to explore (<b>[[1]]</b>) and no design to agree (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Passwords shorter than twelve characters must be rejected with the team's usual error response", "means": "The requirement is already stated, so an interview (<b>[[3]]</b>) has nothing to ask."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer approach this with Claude?", "means": "Make the change and test it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Small, well-scoped and settled? Direct execution. Plan mode is for big or undecided changes.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Direct execution: simple well-scoped changes (single validation check)."}}}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-2", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Explore subagent for verbose discovery", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer is in plan mode with Claude, designing how to split the large <code>User</code> model into smaller pieces. The design depends on every place in the app that reads or writes user fields. Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context. The design talk that followed lost track of earlier decisions. What should the engineer <b>do</b> for the search?", "options": [{"t": "Have an Explore subagent run the searches and return a summary per field.", "why": "Best. The subagent does the noisy searching in its own context and hands back only the summary. The planning session keeps its room for the design."}, {"t": "Let Claude run the searches in the session, then use <code>/compact</code> before designing.", "why": "Works, but after the damage. Compacting frees space, but the raw output already crowded the session, and the summary it keeps is not chosen for this design.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Leave plan mode and change the model in small steps, searching as each step needs.", "why": "Works, but wrong mode. Splitting a central model is an architectural change. Without a plan, early steps pick a design by default, and later steps redo them.", "trap": "wrong mode"}, {"t": "Save the full usage list in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for every later session to load.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. The list is loaded into every session, for every task, and goes stale as soon as the code changes.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context.", "explanation": "The Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output and returns a summary, so the main session keeps its context for planning and decisions.", "eli5": {"question": "Planning a big change needs a long search through the app, and last time the search output crowded out the planning. The clue: the searches filled the session's context.", "answer": "When you plan a kitchen, you send someone to measure every room. They come back with one sheet of numbers, not a pile of tape-measure notes. The <b>Explore subagent</b> is that helper: it does the messy searching elsewhere and hands back a short summary. <b>Rule: noisy discovery goes to the Explore subagent; the planning session gets the summary.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The helper measures; you get one tidy sheet.", "Letting the notes pile up on the table, then tidying: the mess already got in the way.", "Starting to knock down walls before the plan is agreed.", "Pinning the notes to the fridge for every future job, where they go out of date."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full context can be fixed afterwards</b>. Compacting helps later, but the design talk already ran in a crowded session. The Explore subagent keeps the long output out of the session from the start. Only its summary comes back.", "diagram": "plan session -> Explore subagent: find user-field usages\n               (long output stays in the subagent)\nplan session <- summary per field", "mapTitle": "Where should long output go?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool", "Effect on the main session"], "rows": [["Long discovery before a design", "Explore subagent", "Only the summary comes back"], ["Session already full of old output", "<code>/compact</code>", "Shrinks what is there"], ["Facts every task needs, every time", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loaded into every session"], ["Architectural change, open design", "Plan mode", "Design agreed before edits"], ["One small, settled edit", "Direct execution", "No discovery needed"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer is in plan mode with Claude, designing how to split the large User model", "means": "An architectural change: plan mode is right. Leaving it (<b>[[2]]</b>) trades one problem for another."}, {"quote": "Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context", "means": "The decider: the problem is long discovery output landing in the planning session."}, {"quote": "The design talk that followed lost track of earlier decisions", "means": "Compacting after the search (<b>[[1]]</b>) comes after the damage. Keep the output out from the start."}, {"quote": "What should the engineer do for the search?", "means": "Run it in an Explore subagent and take back a summary: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Long discovery output? Give the search to an Explore subagent and keep only its summary.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output, returns summaries."}}}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-3", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Test-driven iteration", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Claude wrote a function in <code>packages/shared/</code> that prices a booking from its start and end times. Prices come out wrong around midnight and around daylight-saving changes. Each fix, asked for in prose, breaks another case. The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each. How should the engineer <b>get</b> the function right?", "options": [{"t": "Describe the midnight and daylight-saving rules in more detail, and ask for a rewrite.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Better prose helps a little, but prose is what keeps breaking other cases. Nothing checks every case each round.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Paste three of the bookings into the prompt as input and expected-price examples.", "why": "Works, but covers only some cases. Concrete examples beat prose, but three of twenty leaves the rest unchecked, and nothing runs them after each change.", "trap": "covers only some cases"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode and have Claude design a new pricing approach before any edits.", "why": "Works, but different problem. The approach is not in question; the edge cases are. A new design still needs checking against the same bookings.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Turn the list into tests first, have Claude run them, and share the failures.", "why": "Best. Every known case is checked on every round, and each failure tells Claude exactly what is still wrong. Fixes can't quietly break another case."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each.", "explanation": "When the right answers are known, write them as tests first and share the failures with Claude. Test-driven iteration gives each round a clear target and catches fixes that break other cases.", "eli5": {"question": "A pricing function keeps getting edge cases wrong, and each fix breaks another. The clue: the team already knows the right price for twenty tricky bookings.", "answer": "If a student keeps getting sums wrong, you don't explain the rules again in longer words. You give them the answer sheet as a checklist, and after each try you show which sums are still wrong. <b>Tests first, then share the failures</b> is that checklist. <b>Rule: known right answers → tests first, and feed Claude the failures.</b>", "options": ["Explaining the rules again in longer words.", "Showing three worked sums out of twenty, and never checking the rest.", "Redesigning the method when the problem is a handful of tricky sums.", "Correct. Mark every sum after every try, and show what is still wrong."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a clearer description will finally get it right</b>. The description is what keeps failing: each prose fix breaks another case. When the right answers are known, turn them into tests. Every round is then checked against every case, and the failures say exactly what to fix.", "diagram": "write tests from the twenty bookings\n  -> Claude edits -> run tests\n  -> share failures -> repeat until all pass", "mapTitle": "How do you tell Claude what “right” means?", "map": {"head": ["You have", "Technique", "What it gives Claude"], "rows": [["Known right answers for many cases", "Tests first, share failures", "A target checked every round"], ["A few clear before/after examples", "Input and output examples", "The shape of the change"], ["Unclear requirements", "Interview pattern", "Questions answered before code"], ["Several problems that affect each other", "One message with all of them", "Fixes made together"], ["Independent problems", "One at a time, in sequence", "Clean, separate fixes"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each fix, asked for in prose, breaks another case", "means": "More prose (<b>[[0]]</b>) repeats what is failing."}, {"quote": "The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each", "means": "The decider: the right answers are known, so they can become tests. Three examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) leave most of them unchecked."}, {"quote": "Prices come out wrong around midnight and around daylight-saving changes", "means": "Edge cases, not an open design question, so plan mode (<b>[[2]]</b>) aims at the wrong thing."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer get the function right?", "means": "Tests first, then share the failures each round: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Know the right answers? Write them as tests first, then share the failures with Claude.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "Test-driven iteration (write tests first, share failures)"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-04", "was": "m7-s1-04", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 PostToolUse hooks for transformation", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "<code>get_customer</code> returns the full card number on file, and the team that owns that server will not change it. Twice, the agent read a card number to a customer. Security says this must never happen. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a system-prompt rule to mention cards by their last four digits only, with two examples.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear rule with examples cuts the leaks, but prompts have a non-zero failure rate, and this is a must-never.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that masks the card field in results before Claude reads them.", "why": "Best. The hook changes the result before Claude reads it, so the agent can't repeat a number it never saw. The server stays as it is."}, {"t": "Route <code>get_customer</code> through a subagent told to pass on only the name, plan and box IDs.", "why": "Works, but still a model choosing. The subagent sees the full number and decides what to pass on; its instructions are not a filter.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Scan each reply for card-number patterns and block the reply before it reaches the customer.", "why": "Works, but only for the formats it knows. A number read out with spaces or in parts slips past, and Claude still has the number.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Security says this must never happen.", "explanation": "When a tool returns data the model must never use, change the result with a PostToolUse hook before the model sees it. Prompt rules only make misuse less likely.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent sometimes reads a customer's card number out loud. The clue: security says this must <i>never</i> happen.", "answer": "If a receptionist must never read out card numbers, don't hand them the number: black it out on the paper first. A <b>PostToolUse hook</b> is that black marker. It edits each tool result before Claude reads it, and Claude can't repeat what it never saw. <b>Rule: if the model must never use some data, remove it before the model sees it.</b>", "options": ["A written rule usually works, but “usually” isn't enough for a must-never.", "Correct. Black out the number before Claude ever sees it.", "A helper told to pass on only some fields still sees the number, and might pass it on.", "Checking each reply catches the obvious cases, but Claude still knows the number."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a firm instruction stops the model saying something</b>. It lowers the chance, but prompt compliance is probabilistic. For data that must never reach a customer, the reliable place to act is the tool result itself: change it before the model reads it.", "diagram": "get_customer → [PostToolUse: mask card] → Claude\nserver unchanged        model never sees the number", "mapTitle": "Which hook, and where does it act?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Mechanism", "Where it acts"], "rows": [["Model must never see or repeat a field", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook masks it", "On the result, before Claude reads it"], ["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook denies the call", "On the call, before the tool runs"], ["Messy result formats (dates, codes)", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook normalises them", "On the result"], ["A wording or tone preference", "Prompt instruction + examples", "In Claude's instructions"], ["You own the tool and nobody needs the field", "Stop returning it", "In the tool itself"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "get_customer returns the full card number on file, and the team that owns that server will not change it", "means": "The fix has to happen on the agent's side, between the tool and Claude."}, {"quote": "Twice, the agent read a card number to a customer", "means": "The number reaches Claude in ordinary chats. Scanning replies (<b>[[3]]</b>) catches only the patterns it knows."}, {"quote": "Security says this must never happen", "means": "The decider: a must-never. A prompt rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a subagent told what to pass on (<b>[[2]]</b>) both still rely on the model choosing."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Change the result before Claude sees it: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Model must never use some data? Strip it from the tool result with a PostToolUse hook.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "PostToolUse hooks intercept results for transformation before the model sees them."}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-11", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks that block outgoing tool calls", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "Refunds above $150 must go to a human. The system prompt says so, yet a monthly review found a few larger refunds that the agent made itself. Finance wants this to stop completely. What should the team <b>do</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks <code>process_refund</code> over the limit and hands the case to a human.", "why": "Best. The hook checks every refund call before it runs. Code enforces the limit every time, which a prompt can't promise."}, {"t": "Restate the limit first in Claude's instructions, with three examples of declining big refunds.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Clearer prompting cuts the misses but can't remove them, and finance wants zero.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Run a nightly job that finds refunds over $150, reverses them and sends each case to a human.", "why": "Works, but after the money has gone. Reversals catch every case, but customers see a refund taken back, and the rule was still broken first.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Have a second Claude instance review each refund decision before the agent replies to the customer.", "why": "Works, but still a model's judgement. A second instance catches some cases but is probabilistic too, and it adds a model call to every refund.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Finance wants this to stop completely.", "explanation": "Business rules that must hold every time are enforced in code. A PreToolUse hook can inspect each outgoing tool call and block the ones that break the rule.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent was told “no refunds over $150”, and still made a few. The clue: finance wants <i>zero</i>.", "answer": "Telling a cashier “never give more than $150 back” works most days. A till that won't open above $150 without a manager's key works every day. A <b>PreToolUse hook</b> is that till: it checks the refund before it happens. <b>Rule: must hold every time → check it in code, before the action runs.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The till won't open above the limit.", "A bigger sign by the till still relies on the cashier remembering.", "Taking money back the next day is messier than not paying it out.", "A second cashier watching helps, but can also miss things, and costs more."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>clearer instructions will finish the job</b>. They reduce the misses, but a prompt is followed most of the time, not every time. When the requirement is “never”, put a check in code on the tool call itself, before the action runs.", "diagram": "Claude → process_refund($180)\n          → [PreToolUse: over $150? deny + escalate]\n          → tool never runs", "mapTitle": "Before, after, or in the prompt?", "map": {"head": ["Requirement", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook denies the call", "Checks before anything happens"], ["A step must come first (verify, then refund)", "Prerequisite gate in code", "Blocks the later tool until the first succeeds"], ["Data in a result must not reach Claude", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook masks it", "Acts on the result"], ["Behaviour should usually follow a style", "Prompt + examples", "Probabilistic is fine"], ["Output needs a second opinion on quality", "Independent review instance", "Fresh eyes, still probabilistic"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Refunds above $150 must go to a human", "means": "A hard business rule tied to one tool call, which a hook can check."}, {"quote": "The system prompt says so, yet a monthly review found a few larger refunds", "means": "The prompt already tried. Moving or repeating it (<b>[[1]]</b>) only lowers the rate."}, {"quote": "Finance wants this to stop completely", "means": "The decider: zero misses means code, before the action. A reviewer model (<b>[[3]]</b>) is probabilistic, and a nightly reversal (<b>[[2]]</b>) comes after the refund."}, {"quote": "What should the team do?", "means": "Check every refund call before it runs: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Must never happen? Block the tool call in a PreToolUse hook, before the action runs.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "Hooks intercept outgoing tool calls to enforce compliance (e.g., block refunds above a threshold)."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.3 Report access failures with partial results", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent fetches each paper's full text through the journals' MCP server. Some papers sit behind paywalls the hospital does not subscribe to, and the server refuses them. The subagent analyses the rest and returns only those findings. Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists. How should the subagent <b>report this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Return its findings plus a structured error naming each refused paper and the reason.", "why": "Best. The coordinator gets the work that succeeded and knows exactly what is missing and why, so it can ask the library, try another source or publish with the gap stated."}, {"t": "Retry refused papers with backoff, then return the findings and “some papers unavailable”.", "why": "Works, but it hides the detail. A paywall refusal won't change on retry, and “some papers” doesn't say which ones or why.", "trap": "hides the context"}, {"t": "Stop with an error until every paper can be fetched, and return no findings before then.", "why": "Works, but it throws away good work. One refused paper shouldn't end a run whose other analyses are finished and useful.", "trap": "ends the run"}, {"t": "Return its findings, and add a line to each summary that some sources may be missing.", "why": "Works, but vaguely. The committee is warned, yet nobody knows which papers are missing, so nobody can fetch them or judge how much they matter.", "trap": "loses which ones"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists.", "explanation": "Report failures with structured context: what failed, why, what was tried and the partial results. Silently returning only the successes makes a gap look like a finding.", "eli5": {"question": "Some papers couldn't be opened, and the report pretends they don't exist. The clue: the summaries read as if there's no other evidence.", "answer": "If an assistant can't get into three of the files, you want to hear “here are 17, and these 3 were locked”, not just the 17. Then you can ask for a key. <b>Report what worked and exactly what didn't, with the reason.</b> <b>Rule: never let a failure look like an empty result.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Here's what I have, and here's exactly what's locked.", "Knocking on a locked door again won't open it, and “some files” doesn't say which.", "Refusing to hand over anything because three files were locked wastes the 17.", "“Some may be missing” worries people without telling them what to fetch."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>returning only what worked is the clean, safe option</b>. But silence about a failure reads as “nothing else exists”. The coordinator can only recover from a failure it can see: what failed, why, and what is already done.", "diagram": "server refuses paywalled papers\ntoday:  findings only -> gap looks like no evidence\nbetter: findings + refused list + reason\n        -> coordinator decides", "mapTitle": "How the failure reaches the coordinator", "map": {"head": ["Report", "What the coordinator knows", "Outcome"], "rows": [["Findings only", "Thinks the set is complete", "Gap reads as “no evidence”"], ["Findings + “some missing”", "Something is missing, not what", "Can't fetch or weigh the gap"], ["Exception, run ends", "Nothing; finished work lost", "Committee gets nothing"], ["Findings + structured failure", "Which papers, why, no retry", "Ask library, other source, or state the gap"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the server refuses them", "means": "An access failure, not an empty result. A paywall refusal won't change on retry (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The subagent analyses the rest and returns only those findings", "means": "The failure is dropped on the way up. Stopping the run (<b>[[2]]</b>) would swing too far the other way."}, {"quote": "Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists", "means": "The decider: lost information has turned into a false impression. A vague warning (<b>[[3]]</b>) doesn't say what to fetch."}, {"quote": "every claim must cite a paper and section", "means": "The committee needs to know which papers were never read, by name."}], "rule": "Report a failure with its type, what was tried and the partial results. Silence reads as “no evidence”.", "guide": {"obj": "5.3 Error propagation across multi-agent systems", "quote": "Structured error context (failure type, attempted query, partial results, alternatives) enables coordinator recovery."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-2", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Access failure vs valid empty result", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The extraction agent calls a <code>verify_carrier</code> MCP tool to check each carrier against the licensing registry. The registry sometimes goes offline for an hour or more. During those outages the tool returns “Carrier not found”, the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence. Certificates from licensed carriers are then rejected as unlicensed. Which change is <b>best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Retry the registry with backoff inside the tool before it returns “Carrier not found”.", "why": "Works, but only for short blips. After an hour-long outage the retries run out and the tool still says “not found”, a wrong answer that looks like a real one.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Send each “Carrier not found” certificate to a reviewer before it is rejected.", "why": "Works, but it treats the symptom. Reviewers catch the wrong rejections, at the cost of checking every real miss too, while the tool keeps saying something false.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Answer from a cache of earlier registry lookups while the registry is offline.", "why": "Works, but only for carriers seen before, and the cached answer may be out of date. A new carrier still comes back “not found”.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Return a transient, retryable <code>isError</code> result when the registry is offline.", "why": "Best. The agent can now tell “the registry couldn't be reached” from “the registry says no licence”, and hold the certificate until the lookup works."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence", "explanation": "A tool must distinguish an access failure from a valid empty result. Return an isError result with an error category and a retryable flag, and keep “not found” for lookups that actually worked.", "eli5": {"question": "When the registry is down, the tool says “not found”, the same words it uses for a real “no licence”. The clue: two very different events give the same answer.", "answer": "If you phone a shop and nobody picks up, that doesn't mean the shop doesn't sell bread. “No answer” and “we have none” are different news. A tool must say which one happened: an <b>isError</b> result marked <b>transient, retryable</b> for “couldn't reach it”. <b>Rule: never report an access failure as an empty result.</b>", "options": ["Ringing again helps if the line is busy for a minute, not for an hour.", "Asking a person to double-check every “none” works, but the phone is still lying.", "Remembering last week's answer helps only for shops you've rung before.", "Correct. Say “nobody answered”, not “they have none”."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>“not found” is a safe default when a lookup fails</b>. It isn't: it turns “we don't know” into a confident wrong answer. Downstream logic acts on it as fact. The tool has to report the failure as a failure.", "diagram": "offline -> \"Carrier not found\" -> rejected (wrong)\noffline -> isError, transient, retryable\n        -> hold, retry later", "mapTitle": "What the tool returns, and what the agent can do", "map": {"head": ["What happened", "What the tool returns", "What the agent can do"], "rows": [["Registry answered: no licence", "Success, “not found”", "Reject as unlicensed"], ["Registry offline", "<code>isError</code>, transient, retryable", "Hold and retry later"], ["Bad input (malformed carrier ID)", "<code>isError</code>, validation", "Fix the input and call again"], ["Registry offline, reported as “not found”", "A false empty result", "Rejects good certificates"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The registry sometimes goes offline for an hour or more", "means": "Too long for retries inside the tool (<b>[[0]]</b>) to wait out."}, {"quote": "the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence", "means": "The decider: an access failure and a valid empty result look identical. Only the tool can tell them apart."}, {"quote": "Certificates from licensed carriers are then rejected as unlicensed", "means": "Wrong answers acted on as fact. A reviewer (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a cache (<b>[[2]]</b>) works around the false answer instead of removing it."}, {"quote": "Which change is best?", "means": "Report the outage as an error with its category: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Couldn't reach it is not the same as found nothing. Return isError with a category and a retryable flag.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "distinguish access failures from valid empty results."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-3", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.3 Local recovery, propagate only unresolved errors", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The weekly dependency-risk report runs one subagent per module. Each subagent looks up every package in an advisory database over MCP. A few lookups per run time out, and they succeed on a second try seconds later. Today each timeout goes straight to the coordinator, which restarts that module's scan from the start. Runs now take most of the night. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator wait a minute before it restarts a module after a timeout.", "why": "Works, but it still throws away finished lookups. Each restart redoes the whole module for one failed package.", "trap": "redoes finished work"}, {"t": "Have the subagent retry blips itself and pass up only lookups that still fail.", "why": "Best. Short blips are fixed where they happen, finished lookups are kept, and the coordinator hears only about failures it has to decide on."}, {"t": "Raise the advisory client's timeout to give slow lookups longer to finish.", "why": "Works, but it hides the problem. Fewer timeouts, yet a hung call now holds the run for longer, and any timeout that remains still restarts the module.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Have the subagent skip timed-out packages and list them in the report as not checked.", "why": "Works, but it gives up too soon. A lookup that would succeed seconds later is left as a gap in the report every week.", "trap": "gives up too soon"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "they succeed on a second try seconds later", "explanation": "Recover locally from transient errors in the subagent, and propagate only unresolved errors, with the partial results, to the coordinator.", "eli5": {"question": "A few lookups hiccup and work on a second try, but each hiccup restarts a whole module. The clue: a second try seconds later works.", "answer": "If one page of a 40-page print job jams, the printer reprints that page, not the whole document, and only calls you if it keeps jamming. A subagent should do the same: <b>retry the blip itself</b>, keep what's done, and <b>report up only what is still broken</b>. <b>Rule: recover locally, propagate only unresolved errors.</b>", "options": ["Waiting before reprinting the whole document still reprints the whole document.", "Correct. Reprint the jammed page; call for help only if it keeps jamming.", "A longer wait for each page means a stuck page holds everything up longer.", "Leaving the jammed page out works, but the document has a hole every week."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every error should go straight to the coordinator, so nothing is hidden</b>. But a blip that a quick retry fixes isn't a decision for the coordinator. Handle transient errors locally; send up only what is still unresolved, together with the partial results.", "diagram": "timeout -> subagent retries (seconds) -> ok\nstill failing -> error + results so far\n              -> coordinator", "mapTitle": "Who handles which failure", "map": {"head": ["Failure", "Who handles it", "How"], "rows": [["Brief timeout, fine on retry", "The subagent", "Retry locally, keep the results"], ["Still failing after retries", "The coordinator", "Structured error + partial results"], ["Business or permission refusal", "The coordinator", "Not retryable; report the reason"], ["Bad input to the tool", "The subagent", "Fix the input and call again"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "they succeed on a second try seconds later", "means": "The decider: these are transient blips that a local retry fixes. Skipping them (<b>[[3]]</b>) gives up on lookups that would work."}, {"quote": "Today each timeout goes straight to the coordinator, which restarts that module's scan from the start", "means": "Finished lookups are thrown away. Waiting first (<b>[[0]]</b>) still restarts the module."}, {"quote": "Runs now take most of the night", "means": "The cost of handling every blip at the top. A longer timeout (<b>[[2]]</b>) trades restarts for longer waits."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Retry in the subagent, propagate only what is still failing: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Recover from blips inside the subagent; send up only unresolved errors, with partial results.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "local recovery in subagents, propagate only unresolved errors with partial results"}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Descriptions that separate similar tools", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The search subagent has two MCP tools, <code>search_journals</code> and <code>search_trial_registry</code>. Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.” Committees often ask about trials that are still running. The subagent sends these questions to <code>search_journals</code>, which holds only published papers, and reports that nothing was found. What is the most effective <b>first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a routing step that sends questions about ongoing or recruiting trials to the registry tool.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Keyword code catches the phrasings it was written for and misses the rest, and it has to be kept up as questions change.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Rewrite both descriptions: what each source holds, example queries, and when to use the other.", "why": "Best. Descriptions are what Claude reads to choose a tool. Saying what each one covers, with examples and a boundary, fixes the cause in two edits."}, {"t": "Merge the two tools into one <code>search_studies</code> tool that queries both sources at once.", "why": "Works, but more than a first step. Every question now pays for two searches, and the server and its callers have to change.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add five example questions to the subagent's prompt, each showing which search tool to call.", "why": "Works, but misses the cause. Examples help on questions like the ones shown, add tokens to every call, and leave two identical descriptions in place.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the primary way Claude chooses between tools. When two tools share a vague description, the first step is to say what each one covers, with input formats, example queries and boundaries.", "eli5": {"question": "Two drawers carry the same label, “Studies”. One holds published papers; the other holds trials still running. The helper keeps opening the wrong one.", "answer": "Before you hire a sorter or knock the drawers together, <b>write better labels</b>: “Published papers: use for finished studies” and “Trials still running: use for ongoing or recruiting trials”. Claude picks tools by their descriptions the way you pick drawers by their labels. <b>Rule: similar tools, wrong picks → fix the descriptions first.</b>", "options": ["A sorter at the door helps, but only for the words it was told to look for.", "Correct. Clear labels on both drawers.", "One big drawer works, but you search everything every time and have to rebuild the cabinet.", "Showing a few examples helps, but the labels still say the same thing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the model needs extra machinery to choose</b>: a router, a merged tool, or examples. Claude chooses a tool mainly from its description. Two tools with the same one-line description give it nothing to choose by, so the first fix is the descriptions themselves.", "diagram": "question → Claude reads descriptions → picks a tool\n\"Searches for studies on a drug\" x2 → coin toss\nsay what each holds + when to use the other → right pick", "mapTitle": "What fixes a wrong tool choice?", "map": {"head": ["Cause", "Fix", "Size"], "rows": [["Similar tools, vague descriptions", "Formats, examples, edge cases, boundaries in each description", "Small, first step"], ["Descriptions are clear, choices still inconsistent", "A few targeted examples in the prompt", "Small, later step"], ["Too many tools for one agent", "Give the agent only the tools its role needs", "Medium"], ["One generic tool doing several jobs", "Split it into purpose-specific tools", "Medium"], ["Picking by keywords in code", "Routing layer", "Large; usually overbuilt"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.”", "means": "The decider: Claude has nothing to tell the two tools apart. That is the cause, and examples in the prompt (<b>[[3]]</b>) work around it."}, {"quote": "which holds only published papers", "means": "A boundary the description should state: running trials belong in the registry."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "The cheapest change that fixes the cause: <b>[[1]]</b>. A router (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a merged tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) are bigger builds."}], "rule": "Claude picks the wrong one of two similar tools? Fix the descriptions first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Include input formats, example queries, edge cases, boundaries."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Keyword wording that pulls the wrong tool", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The system prompt says: “When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use <code>pause_subscription</code>.” Company policy is that cancellations go to a person through <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Customers who write “stop sending boxes, I want to cancel” now get a paused plan instead. They are billed again when the pause ends. The <code>pause_subscription</code> description says only “Pauses deliveries.” What is the best <b>fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep the line and add three example chats where cancelling customers are sent to a person.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The examples pull one way and the keyword line still pulls the other, so some cancellations keep getting paused.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks a pause when the message mentions cancelling.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Code now guesses intent from words, and it blocks customers who say “don't cancel, just pause”.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a <code>cancel_request</code> tool that hands cancellations to staff, described for that case.", "why": "Works, but a new tool for a job <code>escalate_to_human</code> already does. Now three tools overlap instead of two.", "trap": "new tool"}, {"t": "Drop the keyword line, and say in each tool's description which requests it is for.", "why": "Best. The line ties a word to a tool, which is what causes the wrong pick. Clear descriptions with boundaries let Claude choose by what the customer wants."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use pause_subscription.", "explanation": "Keyword-sensitive system-prompt wording creates unintended tool associations. Remove the association and put each tool's purpose and boundaries in its description.", "eli5": {"question": "A sign says “Anyone who says <i>stop</i>, send to the pause desk.” So people who say “stop, I'm leaving” end up paused, not cancelled.", "answer": "Take the sign down, and label each desk by what it does: “Pause desk: a break from deliveries. Leaving for good? Go to the person at the front.” <b>Rule: a prompt line that ties a word to a tool causes misrouting → remove it and put the boundary in the tool descriptions.</b>", "options": ["Showing people the right desk helps, but the sign still points the wrong way.", "A guard who stops anyone saying “cancel” also stops people who said “don't cancel”.", "A new desk for leavers works, but the front desk already does that job.", "Correct. Remove the misleading sign and label the desks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a misrouting needs new code or a new tool</b>. Here the system prompt itself causes it: a keyword rule sends any mention of stopping to one tool. Remove the cause, and give each tool a description that says what it is for and what it is not for.", "diagram": "\"stop ... cancel\" → prompt rule: stop = pause → paused\nrule gone + clear descriptions → escalate_to_human", "mapTitle": "Where does the wrong pick come from?", "map": {"head": ["Source of the misroute", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["A prompt line ties a keyword to a tool", "Remove or reword it by intent", "The line causes the pull"], ["Tool descriptions are one line", "Add purpose, examples and boundaries", "Descriptions drive the choice"], ["Two tools do the same job", "Keep one, describe it clearly", "Overlap causes misrouting"], ["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook", "Code for must-hold rules"], ["Clear descriptions, still inconsistent", "A few targeted examples", "A later step"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use pause_subscription", "means": "The decider: a keyword rule in the prompt. Examples added beside it (<b>[[0]]</b>) still compete with it."}, {"quote": "cancellations go to a person through escalate_to_human", "means": "A tool for this already exists, so a new one (<b>[[2]]</b>) adds overlap."}, {"quote": "The pause_subscription description says only “Pauses deliveries.”", "means": "No boundary to choose by. A hook guessing intent from words (<b>[[1]]</b>) is code for a wording problem."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Remove the keyword line and describe each tool clearly: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "A prompt line ties a word to a tool? Remove it, and put the boundary in the tool descriptions.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Ambiguous/overlapping descriptions cause misrouting; keyword-sensitive system-prompt wording creates unintended tool associations."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Too many tools for one agent", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An Agent SDK script triages new bug reports in Linear. It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools. Its job needs about five of them. It often calls <code>update_issue</code> when it should call <code>move_issue</code>. Sometimes it queries staging when no data is involved. Every tool already has a detailed description. What is the best <b>fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite all the tool descriptions with sharper boundaries between issue and database tools.", "why": "Works, but misses the cause. The descriptions are already detailed; the number of tools is what lowers the choice quality.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Add a classifier that reads each bug report and picks which tools to offer for it.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The script does one job, so its tool set can be fixed once instead of chosen per report.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Give the triage script only the tools its job needs and leave the rest out.", "why": "Best. Fewer tools make the choice more reliable, and a triage script has no reason to reach the database."}, {"t": "Merge <code>update_issue</code> and <code>move_issue</code> into one <code>change_issue</code> tool with a mode field.", "why": "Works, but fixes one pair. The choice moves into a parameter, and the database calls still happen.", "trap": "fixes one symptom"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools.", "explanation": "Giving an agent many tools degrades selection reliability, and tools outside its role get misused. Scope each agent to the tools its job needs.", "eli5": {"question": "A helper sorting mail has been handed every tool in the building, and keeps grabbing the wrong one.", "answer": "Better labels on 18 tools still leave 18 tools to pick from. Give the mail sorter the five things a mail sorter needs. <b>Rule: wrong picks among many tools, with good descriptions already → cut the agent's tool set to its job.</b>", "options": ["Clearer labels help, but the labels are already good and the pile is still too big.", "A helper who hands out tools for each letter works, but the job never changes.", "Correct. Give the sorter only the tools for sorting.", "Gluing two tools together fixes one mix-up, not the pile."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every wrong pick is a description problem</b>. Descriptions come first when they are vague. Here they are already detailed, and the agent holds far more tools than its job needs. Too many tools degrade the choice, and tools outside the role get misused.", "diagram": "18 tools + built-ins → triage script → wrong picks\n5 triage tools → triage script → right picks", "mapTitle": "Descriptions, or the tool set?", "map": {"head": ["Symptom", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Similar tools, one-line descriptions", "Better descriptions", "Primary selection mechanism"], ["Many tools, good descriptions, wrong picks", "Scope the tool set to the role", "Too many tools degrade selection"], ["Agent uses tools outside its job", "Remove them from its definition", "Off-role tools get misused"], ["One role often needs one outside tool", "Give a limited cross-role tool", "Keeps the common case fast"], ["Tool must run first", "Forced <code>tool_choice</code>", "Fixes order, not choice"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools", "means": "The decider: the tool count is the cause."}, {"quote": "Sometimes it queries staging when no data is involved", "means": "A tool outside its job gets misused. Merging two issue tools (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves this in place."}, {"quote": "Every tool already has a detailed description", "means": "Rewriting them (<b>[[0]]</b>) does not reach the cause."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "A fixed, smaller tool set for this one job: <b>[[2]]</b>. A per-report classifier (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more than a fixed job needs."}], "rule": "Good descriptions and still wrong picks among many tools? Give the agent only the tools its job needs.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Too many tools (18 vs 4-5) degrades selection reliability."}}}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-1", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Calibrating when to escalate", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "The agent's prompt says to “escalate when needed.” Upset customers with simple problems, such as one missing item, are often handed to a person. Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone. In some of those chats it promises refunds that policy does not allow. Which change best improves <b>when the agent escalates</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the agent rate its confidence in each case and escalate when the rating is low.", "why": "Works, but an unreliable signal. The agent is often most sure of itself on exactly the cases it gets wrong, such as the calm policy exceptions.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "Train a separate classifier on past tickets to decide which chats go to a person.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It needs labelled history and retraining, and it learns past habits, including the bad ones described here.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Escalate when a sentiment check finds the customer upset, and let the agent handle the rest.", "why": "Works, but the wrong signal. Mood is not complexity: it sends more simple cases to people and still misses the calm exceptions.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "State escalation criteria in the prompt, with examples of escalate versus resolve.", "why": "Best. The problem is an unclear boundary. Stating the triggers and showing a few cases on each side fixes that directly, at prompt cost."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone.", "explanation": "When escalation is miscalibrated, define explicit escalation criteria and show few-shot examples of escalate versus resolve. Sentiment and self-reported confidence are unreliable proxies for complexity.", "eli5": {"question": "A new shop assistant calls the manager whenever a customer frowns, but quietly bends the rules for polite customers.", "answer": "Don't hand them a mood meter or ask how sure they feel. Tell them <b>when to call the manager</b> (“anything the refund policy doesn't cover; anyone who asks for the manager”) and walk through a few real cases. <b>Rule: wrong escalations → clear criteria plus a few examples, not mood or self-confidence.</b>", "options": ["Asking “how sure are you?” fails: they feel surest when they're bending the rules.", "Studying years of old cases works, but it's a big job and copies old mistakes.", "A mood meter calls the manager for frowns, not for hard cases.", "Correct. Say when to call the manager, and show a few cases."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>escalation needs a signal the model can't fake</b>: a mood score, a confidence number, a trained classifier. The real problem is that nobody told the agent where the line is. Upset is not the same as complex, and the model's own confidence is poorly calibrated.", "diagram": "\"escalate when needed\" → mood decides → wrong cases\ncriteria + examples:\n  policy gap? asked for a person? → escalate\n  simple, even if upset → resolve", "mapTitle": "Which signal tells you to escalate?", "map": {"head": ["Signal", "Reliable?", "Why"], "rows": [["Customer asks for a person", "Yes: escalate at once", "An explicit trigger"], ["Policy exception or gap", "Yes: escalate", "Needs a human decision"], ["Agent cannot make progress", "Yes: escalate", "Stuck is a trigger"], ["Customer sounds upset", "No", "Sentiment is not complexity"], ["Agent says it is unsure", "No", "Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "escalate when needed", "means": "The cause: no criteria. The fix is to write them down and show examples: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Upset customers with simple problems, such as one missing item, are often handed to a person", "means": "Mood is already driving escalation. A sentiment check (<b>[[2]]</b>) makes that worse."}, {"quote": "Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone", "means": "The decider: the hard cases look calm and feel easy, so neither mood nor self-rated confidence (<b>[[0]]</b>) catches them."}, {"quote": "Which change best improves when the agent escalates?", "means": "The proportionate fix is in the prompt. A classifier (<b>[[1]]</b>) is a bigger build."}], "rule": "Escalating the wrong cases? Explicit criteria plus examples, not sentiment or self-rated confidence.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Sentiment-based escalation and self-reported confidence are unreliable proxies for complexity."}}}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Structured handoff summaries", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "When the agent escalates, staff pick up the case from a queue. Each case shows the line “Customer needs help” and a link to the chat. Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act. Some of them ask the customer to explain the problem again. What should the agent <b>send with each escalation</b>?", "options": [{"t": "The full chat transcript, pasted into the case in place of the link.", "why": "Works, but staff still read the whole chat to find the facts. It saves a click, not the minutes.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "A paragraph written by the agent that describes the problem in its own words.", "why": "Works, but free text varies. Some paragraphs leave out the box or the amount, and staff still have to hunt or ask.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "A summary with set fields: customer ID, cause, refund amount, next step.", "why": "Best. Staff see the facts they act on in the same place every time, without reading the chat."}, {"t": "The customer's account details and recent boxes, pulled fresh from <code>get_customer</code>.", "why": "Works, but different problem. It gives staff data, not the diagnosis: what went wrong and what the agent recommends.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act.", "explanation": "Escalations should carry a structured handoff summary (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) so a human can act without reading the transcript.", "eli5": {"question": "A nurse hands a patient to the next shift by saying “they need help, read the file.”", "answer": "Good handovers use a short card with the same boxes every time: who, what's wrong, what's been done, what to do next. The next nurse acts in seconds. <b>Rule: hand over to a human with a structured summary, not the transcript.</b>", "options": ["Handing over the whole file still means reading the whole file.", "A free-written note helps, but each one leaves out something different.", "Correct. The same short card, every time.", "The patient's records are useful, but they don't say what's wrong today."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>more information makes a better handoff</b>. Staff don't need more; they need the few facts they act on, in a fixed place. A structured summary lets them act without reading the transcript.", "diagram": "escalate_to_human(\n  customer_id, cause, refund_amount,\n  recommended_action )\n→ staff act without opening the chat", "mapTitle": "What does a good handoff carry?", "map": {"head": ["Handoff", "What staff do", "Result"], "rows": [["Link only", "Read the whole chat", "Minutes per case"], ["Full transcript", "Read the whole chat", "Minutes per case"], ["Free-text paragraph", "Read, sometimes ask again", "Facts vary"], ["Account data only", "Work out the problem", "No diagnosis"], ["Structured summary with set fields", "Act on it", "Fast and consistent"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each case shows the line “Customer needs help” and a link to the chat", "means": "The handoff carries no facts, only a pointer to them."}, {"quote": "Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act", "means": "The decider: the cost is reading. Pasting the transcript in (<b>[[0]]</b>) keeps that cost."}, {"quote": "Some of them ask the customer to explain the problem again", "means": "Facts are missing. Free text (<b>[[1]]</b>) sometimes misses them too; account data (<b>[[3]]</b>) has no diagnosis."}, {"quote": "What should the agent send with each escalation?", "means": "Fixed fields staff act on: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Handing a case to a human? Send a structured summary they can act on without the transcript.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "compile structured handoff summaries (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) for humans without the transcript."}}}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-3", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Several customer matches", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "A customer writes in from a new email address and gives only their name. <code>get_customer</code> returns two active accounts with that name. The agent picks the account with the most recent order and carries on. Last week this sent a refund to the wrong customer. How should the agent handle a lookup that returns <b>more than one customer</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Escalate each lookup with several matches to a person, who checks which account it is.", "why": "Works, but costs resolution. A person checks what the customer could confirm in one reply, and the queue grows.", "trap": "escalates what it could resolve"}, {"t": "Ask the customer for an identifier, such as a box number or postcode, first.", "why": "Best. The customer knows which account is theirs. One question settles it, and the agent can then resolve the case itself."}, {"t": "Pick the match whose postcode and recent boxes fit the chat best, and note the choice.", "why": "Works, but still a guess. Better clues pick right more often, and a wrong pick still refunds a stranger.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "Have Claude rate how sure it is of the match, and ask the customer when it is unsure.", "why": "Works, but an unreliable signal. Claude was already sure enough to refund the wrong account.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "get_customer returns two active accounts with that name.", "explanation": "When a lookup returns several customers, ask the customer for an identifier rather than choosing by heuristic. A guess, however good, sometimes acts on the wrong account.", "eli5": {"question": "Two people called Sam Lee have parcels at the post office. The clerk hands over the newer parcel to whoever says “Sam Lee”.", "answer": "The clerk should ask “what's your postcode?” One question, and the right Sam gets the right parcel. <b>Rule: several matches → ask for an identifier, don't pick.</b>", "options": ["Calling a supervisor works, but the customer could answer it in one line.", "Correct. Ask for something only the right Sam knows.", "A smarter guess is still a guess.", "The clerk felt sure last time too."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a smarter rule can pick the right account</b>. Any rule is a proxy, and the cost of a wrong pick is a refund to a stranger. The customer has the missing fact, so ask them.", "diagram": "get_customer(\"Priya Shah\") → 2 accounts\n  ask: box number or postcode? → 1 account → resolve", "mapTitle": "Unsure who or what? Ask, guess, or escalate?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Agent's move", "Why"], "rows": [["Several customer matches", "Ask for an identifier", "The customer knows; don't guess"], ["Request is unclear", "Ask a clarifying question", "Cheap and exact"], ["Policy exception or gap", "Escalate", "Needs a human decision"], ["Customer asks for a person", "Escalate at once", "Explicit trigger"], ["Agent feels unsure", "Not a trigger by itself", "Self-rated confidence is unreliable"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "get_customer returns two active accounts with that name", "means": "The decider: the lookup is ambiguous, and both accounts are live."}, {"quote": "The agent picks the account with the most recent order", "means": "A heuristic. Better clues (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a confidence rating (<b>[[3]]</b>) are still guesses."}, {"quote": "Last week this sent a refund to the wrong customer", "means": "A wrong guess costs money, so guessing better is not enough."}, {"quote": "How should the agent handle a lookup that returns more than one customer?", "means": "Ask the customer, then resolve it: <b>[[1]]</b>. Escalating (<b>[[0]]</b>) hands over a case one question would settle."}], "rule": "Several matches? Ask the customer for an identifier; never pick by heuristic.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Multiple customer matches → ask for identifiers (not heuristic selection)."}}}, {"id": "m4p-s1-03", "was": "s1-03", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks & normalization", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 2, "question": "Customers can claim a refund within 30 days of delivery. The agent checks each box against that window. Delivery dates reach it in three shapes, one per warehouse system: Unix seconds, ISO dates, and a plain number such as <code>20260403</code>. The agent misjudges the plain number, mostly near month ends. Another warehouse system joins soon. Which <b>TWO</b> changes make the answer the same for every source?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that turns each delivery date into one ISO form before Claude reads it.", "why": "Best. Code converts every format before Claude sees it, so the plain number never reaches Claude. A new warehouse needs one more rule in the hook."}, {"t": "Add a <code>convert_date</code> tool, and tell the agent to call it before any refund-window question.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The tool converts dates correctly, but calling it is a request, so some chats will skip it.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Describe all three date shapes in the system prompt, with a worked conversion for each one.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples make mistakes rarer, but Claude still converts and counts the days itself, which is where the month-end errors come from.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have <code>lookup_order</code> also return <code>days_since_delivery</code> and a <code>within_window</code> flag.", "why": "Best. The tool does the date maths in code, so Claude reads the answer instead of working it out, whatever the source format."}], "answer": [0, 3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Delivery dates reach it in three shapes, one per warehouse system", "explanation": "Deterministic data handling belongs in code. A hook normalises mixed formats before the model sees them, and a tool can return derived values instead of asking the model to compute them.", "eli5": {"question": "Dates arrive written three different ways, and the agent misreads one of them. Soon there will be a fourth. The clue: the answer must be the same whatever system the date came from.", "answer": "Imagine a post room that gets letters dated in three styles. Instead of training every clerk to read all three, you stamp one standard date on each letter as it arrives (<b>a PostToolUse hook that normalises</b>). Even better, the sender can write “arrived 12 days ago” on the envelope (<b>the tool returns the computed value</b>). <b>Rule: format conversion and date maths belong in code, not in Claude's head.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Every date is stamped in one standard form before Claude reads it.", "A conversion machine on the desk helps, but clerks still have to remember to use it.", "A wall chart of date styles helps, but the clerk still does the sums and still slips at month ends.", "Correct. The answer arrives already worked out."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think Claude needs <b>better instructions about dates</b>. But converting formats and counting days are exact jobs with one right answer. That is work for code. Claude should receive either <b>one clean format</b> or the <b>answer already computed</b>.", "diagram": "lookup_order result\n  1743638400 | 2026-04-03 | 20260403\n        ↓ PostToolUse hook\n  2026-04-03  (one ISO form)\n  + days_since_delivery, within_window from the tool", "mapTitle": "Who should handle exact data work?", "map": {"head": ["Job", "Best place", "Why"], "rows": [["Convert mixed formats to one", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook", "Runs on every result, before Claude reads it"], ["Compute a derived value (days, totals)", "The tool itself", "Claude reads the answer, no maths"], ["A step Claude must take every time", "Code, not a prompt request", "Prompts are followed most of the time, not always"], ["Explain how formats look", "System prompt", "Helps judgement, not exact work"], ["Judging an unclear customer claim", "Claude", "Needs reasoning, not a formula"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Delivery dates reach it in three shapes, one per warehouse system", "means": "The decider. Mixed formats are exactly what a normalising hook (<b>[[0]]</b>) is for."}, {"quote": "The agent misjudges the plain number, mostly near month ends", "means": "The error is in Claude's own conversion and day counting. Prompt examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) leave that work with Claude."}, {"quote": "Another warehouse system joins soon", "means": "The fix must scale. One more hook rule or tool-side calculation (<b>[[3]]</b>) handles it."}, {"quote": "Which TWO changes make the answer the same for every source?", "means": "The same answer every time needs code. A tool Claude must remember to call (<b>[[1]]</b>) is still a request."}], "rule": "Normalise formats in a PostToolUse hook and compute derived values in the tool. Don't ask Claude to do exact conversions.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Agent SDK hooks for interception and normalization", "quote": "Normalize heterogeneous formats (Unix ts, ISO 8601, numeric codes)"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s1-07", "was": "s1-07", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Tool distribution", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Over time the support agent has gained many tools. Next to the five core ones sit back-office tools such as <code>issue_credit_memo</code>, <code>adjust_inventory</code> and <code>override_price</code>. Each is needed in very few chats, and they are sometimes called when they should not be. Since the list grew, the agent picks the wrong core tool more often, spread across all five. Which change <b>best reverses</b> this?", "options": [{"t": "Leave the everyday tools where they are, and move the rarely used ones to a specialist it hands off to.", "why": "Best. A short tool list on the agent that handles most chats restores reliable selection, and the rare tools stay available through the specialist."}, {"t": "Rewrite every tool description with inputs, examples and boundaries that set it apart from its neighbours.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. Sharper descriptions fix confusion between look-alike tools. These mistakes are spread across all the core tools and began as the list grew.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks back-office tools unless a supervisor approved the case.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. The hook stops misuse of the back-office tools, but they stay in the list, so choosing among the core tools is still harder.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Merge all the tools into a few broad ones, each taking an action parameter that names the operation.", "why": "Works, but blurs the tools. Fewer tools shortens the list, but generic action tools hide what each operation does, so selection inside them gets harder.", "trap": "blurs the tools"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Since the list grew, the agent picks the wrong core tool more often, spread across all five.", "explanation": "Too many tools on one agent degrades selection. Keep the high-frequency tools on the front-line agent and scope rare capabilities to a specialist.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent's toolbox got crowded, and now it grabs the wrong everyday tool more often. The clue: the mistakes spread across all five core tools and began as the list grew.", "answer": "Think of a kitchen drawer stuffed with gadgets you use once a year. Finding the everyday knife gets slower and sloppier. You move the rare gadgets to a cupboard in the back room (<b>a specialist agent</b>) and keep the drawer for daily tools. <b>Rule: too many tools → keep the frequent ones on the main agent, move rare ones to a specialist.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Clear the drawer; rare gadgets live with a specialist.", "Better labels help when two tools look alike. Here the drawer is simply too full.", "Locking the rare gadgets stops misuse, but they still clutter the drawer.", "Gluing gadgets into a few multi-tools empties the drawer, but now each tool is harder to tell apart inside."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think wrong tool picks always mean <b>bad descriptions</b>. That is true when two tools look alike. But when errors are <b>spread across every tool</b> and started as the list grew, the cause is the <b>number of tools</b>. Selection gets less reliable as one agent holds more of them.", "mapTitle": "The agent picks the wrong tool. Why?", "map": {"head": ["Pattern of mistakes", "Likely cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Spread across all tools, began as the list grew", "Too many tools on one agent", "Move rare tools to a specialist"], ["Between two look-alike tools", "Vague or overlapping descriptions", "Rewrite descriptions with boundaries"], ["Rare tool used when it shouldn't be", "Tool given outside its role", "Scope it to a role, or gate it"], ["One generic tool used for many jobs", "Blurry action-parameter tool", "Split into purpose-specific tools"], ["Model answers in text instead of calling", "Tool choice left to auto", "Set <code>tool_choice</code> for that step"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Over time the support agent has gained many tools", "means": "The list is long, which points to tool count."}, {"quote": "Each is needed in very few chats, and they are sometimes called when they should not be", "means": "These tools are off the agent's main job, so they belong with a specialist: <b>[[0]]</b>. A hook (<b>[[2]]</b>) stops misuse but leaves them in the list."}, {"quote": "Since the list grew, the agent picks the wrong core tool more often, spread across all five", "means": "The decider. Not look-alike confusion, so better descriptions (<b>[[1]]</b>) miss the cause. Merging into generic tools (<b>[[3]]</b>) blurs them."}], "rule": "Errors spread across all tools as the list grew → too many tools. Keep frequent tools on the main agent; move rare ones to a specialist.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Too many tools (18 vs 4-5) degrades selection reliability."}}}, {"id": "m4p-s2-04", "was": "s2-04", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "An engineer has rewritten a prose spec for a log-redaction function four times. Each run, Claude builds it differently. Sometimes it masks the whole object that holds a secret, and sometimes only the value. Nested arrays and values that are already masked come out differently each time. The engineer has a firm answer for every case. They also want later changes checked against those answers. Which <b>TWO</b> changes fit best?", "options": [{"t": "Show Claude two or three before-and-after log records, with a nested array and a masked value.", "why": "Best. Concrete input and output pairs show the transformation directly and settle the cases that prose leaves open."}, {"t": "Ask Claude to interview the engineer about the unclear cases before it writes any more code.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Interviewing draws out requirements nobody has settled. The engineer already has every answer; the gap is in showing them to Claude.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Write failing tests for the disputed cases first, share the failures, and iterate until they pass.", "why": "Best. Tests state each answer exactly, give Claude concrete feedback, and keep checking later changes."}, {"t": "Expand the prose spec with exact definitions for each disputed case and a glossary of its key terms.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Sharper prose helps a little, but four prose rewrites have already failed to pin the behaviour down.", "trap": "more of the same"}], "answer": [0, 2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The engineer has a firm answer for every case.", "explanation": "Concrete input/output examples communicate a transformation better than prose, and test-driven iteration states the target objectively and keeps checking it. The interview pattern is for requirements that are not yet settled.", "eli5": {"question": "Four rewrites of a written spec, and Claude still builds the function differently each time. The engineer knows every answer. The clue: the answers are <i>known</i> but the <i>prose</i> isn't getting them across, and later changes must be <i>checked</i>.", "answer": "Explaining a haircut in words rarely works; showing a before-and-after photo does. And a checklist you tick at the end tells you whether the cut matches. For code, the photos are <b>input/output examples</b> and the checklist is <b>failing tests</b> that Claude iterates against, which keep checking later changes too. <b>Rule: prose keeps failing → show examples and write tests first.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Before-and-after photos show exactly what you want.", "Asking questions helps when you don't know what you want. Here you already do.", "Correct. A checklist that tells you, now and later, whether the result matches.", "A longer written description is the thing that has already failed four times."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>unclear output means the requirements are unclear</b>, so Claude should ask questions. Here the engineer has every answer; prose is the weak channel. The exam guide's tools for this are concrete input/output examples, which beat prose for transformations, and test-driven iteration, which also keeps checking later changes.", "diagram": "{user:{token:\"abc\"}}  →  {user:{token:\"***\"}}\n[{key:\"x\"},{key:\"y\"}]  →  [{key:\"***\"},{key:\"***\"}]\n\"***\"                  →  \"***\" (not re-masked)", "mapTitle": "Claude keeps building it differently. Which lever?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Lever", "Why"], "rows": [["Answers known, prose fails to convey them", "Input/output examples", "Shows the transformation"], ["Answers known, must stay checked later", "Tests first, share failures", "Objective and repeatable"], ["Answers not yet decided", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks questions first"], ["Prose is vague on terms", "Sharper definitions", "Helps only if wording is the gap"], ["Several problems that interact", "One message with all of them", "Fixes depend on each other"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer has rewritten a prose spec for a log-redaction function four times", "means": "More prose (<b>[[3]]</b>) is the lever that has already failed."}, {"quote": "Nested arrays and values that are already masked come out differently each time", "means": "Specific cases that a before-and-after example settles directly (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The engineer has a firm answer for every case", "means": "The decider. Nothing is left to discover, so an interview (<b>[[1]]</b>) asks for what is already known."}, {"quote": "They also want later changes checked against those answers", "means": "Tests keep checking: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Prose fails to convey a known transformation → input/output examples plus tests written first.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "Concrete input/output examples communicate transformations better than prose"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s2-07", "was": "s2-07", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Large-codebase exploration", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer has spent a long session exploring the payments code in <code>server/</code>. Claude now gives different answers about where requests are authenticated. It talks about “typical middleware patterns” instead of the file it named earlier. The exploration will continue over several more sessions this week. Two other engineers will pick up parts of it. What is the <b>best response</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> now and keep exploring in this session with the condensed history.", "why": "Works, but stays in one session. Compacting frees space today, but it carries nothing to later sessions or to the other engineers, and it can blur exact file names.", "trap": "stays in one session"}, {"t": "Have Claude re-read the authentication code end to end so the exact details are fresh again.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. The details return for a while, but more volume goes into an already degraded context, and nothing reaches later sessions.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Resume this session tomorrow with <code>--resume</code> so the full history carries over.", "why": "Works, but carries the problem. Resuming keeps every earlier finding, but it brings back the same degraded context too.", "trap": "carries the problem"}, {"t": "Write confirmed findings to a scratchpad file, and give broad searches to subagents.", "why": "Best. A scratchpad file keeps findings across sessions and people, and subagents keep verbose search output out of the main context."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The exploration will continue over several more sessions this week.", "explanation": "Inconsistent answers and talk of “typical patterns” are signs of context degradation. Persist findings in a scratchpad file that later sessions and colleagues can read, and delegate verbose searches to subagents.", "eli5": {"question": "After a long session, Claude is getting vague and contradicting itself. The work goes on for days, and other people will join. The clue: the findings must survive <i>beyond this session</i>.", "answer": "After a long day of detective work, your memory of the morning gets fuzzy. The fix isn't to squeeze your memory harder; it's a case file. Write down what you have confirmed, so tomorrow-you and your colleagues start from it. Send helpers to do the messy searching and report back. That is a <b>scratchpad file</b> plus <b>subagents</b>. <b>Rule: degrading and multi-session → write findings down, delegate the noisy searching.</b>", "options": ["Tidying your desk helps today, but your colleagues can't read your desk tomorrow.", "Rereading everything piles more paper onto a desk that is already overflowing.", "Picking up tomorrow exactly where you left off brings back the same fog.", "Correct. A case file everyone can read, and helpers for the noisy searches."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full, confused session just needs compacting</b>. <code>/compact</code> frees space inside one session. Here the signs are <i>degradation</i> (inconsistent answers, “typical patterns” instead of the specific file), and the work spans sessions and people. The guide's answer is a scratchpad file for key findings plus subagents for verbose output.", "mapTitle": "Context trouble: what kind, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Vague answers; work spans sessions or people", "Degradation, no saved state", "Scratchpad file + subagents"], ["Nearly full, answers still specific, ends today", "Space only", "<code>/compact</code>"], ["Continue the same healthy session later", "Nothing wrong", "<code>--resume</code>"], ["Search output floods the context", "Verbose discovery", "Subagent returns a summary"], ["Work must survive a crash", "No saved state", "Manifest of progress"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude now gives different answers about where requests are authenticated", "means": "Inconsistent answers: a sign of degradation, not just a full context."}, {"quote": "It talks about “typical middleware patterns” instead of the file it named earlier", "means": "The textbook sign. Re-reading (<b>[[1]]</b>) adds volume; resuming (<b>[[2]]</b>) keeps the fog."}, {"quote": "The exploration will continue over several more sessions this week", "means": "The decider. <code>/compact</code> (<b>[[0]]</b>) helps only inside this session."}, {"quote": "Two other engineers will pick up parts of it", "means": "Findings must live in a file others can read: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Context degrading and work spans sessions or people → scratchpad file for findings, subagents for verbose search.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Scratchpad files persist key findings across boundaries; subagent delegation isolates verbose output"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s2-10", "was": "s2-10", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.6 Independent review instances", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A session generates a billing module in <code>server/</code>. The engineer then asks the same session to review it, and it says the code looks right. A colleague opens a fresh session with the plain prompt “review this for bugs”. It finds an off-by-one at a proration boundary and a rounding error. Both sessions used the same model and settings. What should <b>change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give review sessions explicit criteria for boundary conditions and the rounding of money amounts.", "why": "Works, but not the difference. Criteria sharpen reviews, but the colleague found both bugs with an equally plain prompt.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Run reviews in a fresh session that has none of the generating session's history.", "why": "Best. A session that wrote the code keeps its own reasoning and tends to accept it. An independent instance reviews with fresh eyes."}, {"t": "Turn on extended thinking for review passes in the generating session before it concludes.", "why": "Works, but same assumptions. Longer reasoning helps a little, but it starts from the same generation reasoning that missed the bugs.", "trap": "same blind spot"}, {"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> in the generating session, then repeat the same review in that session.", "why": "Works, but keeps the bias. Compacting frees space, but the generation reasoning stays in summary form, so the review is still a self-review.", "trap": "same blind spot"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "A colleague opens a fresh session with the plain prompt “review this for bugs”.", "explanation": "A session that generated code retains its reasoning and is less effective at reviewing its own changes. Independent review instances catch subtle issues better than self-review or extended thinking.", "eli5": {"question": "The session that wrote the code says it's fine. A fresh session with a plain prompt finds two bugs. The clue: the only real difference is <i>fresh</i>.", "answer": "Proofreading your own essay is hard: you read what you meant, not what you wrote. A friend who never saw your drafts spots the typos at once. For Claude, the friend is an <b>independent instance</b> with none of the generation history. <b>Rule: review code in a fresh instance, not in the session that wrote it.</b>", "options": ["A better checklist helps, but the friend found the typos without one.", "Correct. A fresh reader with no memory of your drafts.", "Thinking longer about your own essay still reads what you meant.", "Shortening your notes still leaves you reading your own essay."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a missed bug means the review prompt was weak</b>. Here the prompts were equally plain, the model and settings were the same, and only one session found the bugs. The difference is history: the generating session keeps its own reasoning and won't question it. An independent instance has none.", "diagram": "session A: write → review (same reasoning) → \"looks right\"\nsession B: fresh  → review                → 2 bugs", "mapTitle": "Review missed bugs. What changes the result?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Same prompt; fresh session finds bugs", "Self-review bias", "Independent review instance"], ["Fresh session also misses them", "Vague review criteria", "Explicit criteria per category"], ["Self-review with more thinking", "Same assumptions", "Still a self-review"], ["Large change, many files", "Attention dilution", "Per-file pass + integration pass"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The engineer then asks the same session to review it, and it says the code looks right", "means": "A self-review: the reviewer holds the generation reasoning."}, {"quote": "A colleague opens a fresh session with the plain prompt “review this for bugs”", "means": "The decider. A plain prompt worked when the session was fresh, so criteria (<b>[[0]]</b>) weren't the gap."}, {"quote": "Both sessions used the same model and settings", "means": "Only the history differs. More thinking (<b>[[2]]</b>) or compacting (<b>[[3]]</b>) keeps that history. Use a separate instance: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Review generated code in an independent instance; self-review keeps the generation reasoning.", "guide": {"obj": "4.6 Multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures", "quote": "Independent review instances (no prior reasoning) catch subtle issues better than self-review/extended thinking"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s3-04", "was": "s3-04", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Tool distribution & least privilege", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "While writing a summary, the reporting subagent often stops to confirm small details, such as a drug's approved generic name. Each check goes back through the coordinator to a search subagent, and every round trip adds a long wait. Most checks are one-field lookups. A few ask whether two papers report the same trial, which takes real research across several sources. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give the reporting subagent the full toolset that the literature-search subagent uses.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Full search tools would end the round trips, but agents misuse tools outside their speciality, and same-trial research belongs with the coordinator.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Have the reporting subagent collect its open checks and send them as one batch at the end.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. One batch means fewer round trips, but the draft is left full of open details, and simple lookups still go the long way.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Give the reporter a small tool for one-field lookups, and keep harder questions with the coordinator.", "why": "Best. A limited cross-role tool removes the round trip for the frequent, simple case, and the rarer research questions still go through the coordinator."}, {"t": "Have the coordinator look up likely names and details in advance and add them to the Task prompt.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Prefetching helps when you know what will be asked. The details come up as the summary is written, so the coordinator would be guessing.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Most checks are one-field lookups.", "explanation": "Scope each agent's tools to its role, then make a deliberate exception for a frequent, simple need: a narrow cross-role tool. Work that needs coordination keeps flowing through the coordinator.", "eli5": {"question": "The report writer keeps asking the boss to ask the researcher simple questions, and each trip is slow. The clue: most questions are one-field lookups; only a few need real research.", "answer": "A shop assistant has to ask the manager, who asks the stockroom, every time a customer wants a price. Give the assistant a price scanner for prices, and keep the tricky questions going to the manager. That scanner is a <b>narrow cross-role tool</b>: one small capability from another role, for the frequent simple need. <b>Rule: frequent simple lookup → a narrow tool for it; complex work stays with the coordinator.</b>", "options": ["Handing the assistant the stockroom keys works, but now they're doing the stockroom's job, and may get it wrong.", "Saving all questions for the end means fewer trips, but the customer waits with gaps on the receipt.", "Correct. A price scanner for the simple questions; the manager for the hard ones.", "Guessing which prices will be needed works sometimes. You can't know in advance what customers will ask."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>least privilege means no exceptions</b>, so every check must go through the coordinator. The guide allows a <b>limited cross-role tool</b> when one need is frequent and simple. The frequent case gets a direct path; the complex case keeps coordination.", "diagram": "one-field check  → lookup tool (direct)\nsame-trial question → coordinator → search subagent", "mapTitle": "A subagent needs something from another role. How do you give it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Frequent, simple lookups; rare complex ones", "Narrow cross-role tool; complex via coordinator", "Fast path for the common case"], ["Needed facts are known before the task starts", "Pass them in the Task prompt", "No tool needed"], ["Agent given another role's full toolset", "Avoid", "Off-speciality tools get misused"], ["Checks pile up until the end", "Avoid", "Blocks the work in progress"], ["Complex research mid-task", "Route through the coordinator", "It owns delegation"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each check goes back through the coordinator to a search subagent, and every round trip adds a long wait", "means": "The cost is the round trip. A batch at the end (<b>[[1]]</b>) leaves the draft blocked on open details."}, {"quote": "Most checks are one-field lookups", "means": "The decider. A frequent, simple need is exactly what a narrow tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) is for."}, {"quote": "A few ask whether two papers report the same trial, which takes real research across several sources", "means": "That work stays with the coordinator, so giving the full search toolset (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more than needed."}, {"quote": "the reporting subagent often stops to confirm small details", "means": "The details arise while writing, so prefetching them (<b>[[3]]</b>) means guessing."}], "rule": "Frequent simple need → narrow cross-role tool. Complex needs → through the coordinator.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Scoped access; limited cross-role tools for high-frequency needs."}}}, {"id": "m4p-s3-05", "was": "s3-05", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Provenance through synthesis", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Each analysis summary keeps the paper, section and page in separate fields. The synthesis subagent turns the summaries into flowing prose. The reporting subagent then cites each sentence by finding the summary that reads most like it. A committee member found a dosing claim cited to a paper that never mentions dosing. Spot checks find more claims cited to the wrong paper. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the citation-check subagent compare every cited claim with its paper before release.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. A full check would catch wrong citations before they go out, but the pipeline would still guess them, and each one caught means rework.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Have the reporting subagent match more strictly, and leave a sentence uncited when unsure.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Stricter matching swaps some wrong citations for missing ones, and every claim must be cited. It is still a guess made after the link was lost.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have analysis add a short supporting quote to each finding, which makes the matching step easier.", "why": "Works, but still guessing. Quotes give the matcher more to go on, but citations are still rebuilt by resemblance instead of carried through.", "trap": "still guessing"}, {"t": "Have synthesis tag each claim with its source summary IDs, and build citations from them.", "why": "Best. The claim-to-source link is lost when synthesis writes prose. Carrying the link through synthesis removes the guesswork where it starts."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The reporting subagent then cites each sentence by finding the summary that reads most like it.", "explanation": "Source attribution is lost when synthesis compresses content without keeping claim-to-source mappings. Preserve those mappings through every stage instead of rebuilding them downstream by similarity.", "eli5": {"question": "Claims end up cited to the wrong papers. The summaries had the sources right. The clue: citations are added afterwards by guessing which summary a sentence looks like.", "answer": "A cook mixes ingredients from labelled jars into one soup, then tries to guess from the taste which jar each flavour came from. Better to write down which jars go into each spoonful as you cook. In the system, synthesis should keep a <b>claim-to-source mapping</b>: each claim carries the IDs of the summaries behind it. <b>Rule: carry the source link through each step; don't rebuild it afterwards.</b>", "options": ["A taster checking every bowl catches mistakes, but the kitchen is still guessing.", "Guessing more carefully means fewer wrong labels but more missing ones.", "Better tasting notes help the guess, but it's still a guess.", "Correct. Note the jars for each spoonful as you cook."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to <b>add a checker</b> when citations go wrong. But the summaries already hold the right sources. The link breaks at <b>synthesis</b>, which writes prose without saying which summary each claim came from. After that, the reporter can only guess by resemblance.", "diagram": "summaries (paper, section, page) ✓\n  synthesis → prose          ✗ link dropped\n  reporter  → cite by look-alike = guess\nfix: synthesis emits claim + [summary IDs]", "mapTitle": "Citations are wrong. Where does the link break?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Sources right in summaries, wrong in the report", "Synthesis drops the claim-source link", "Claim plus source IDs through synthesis"], ["Summaries mix content with source details", "Analysis output format", "Separate structured fields"], ["Wrong citations reach readers this week", "No check before release", "Independent check before release"], ["Sources disagree", "Not an attribution fault", "Show both, with sources"], ["Numbers or dates blurred in summaries", "Lossy summarising", "Keep exact facts as fields"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each analysis summary keeps the paper, section and page in separate fields", "means": "Analysis already does its part; the link is lost later."}, {"quote": "The synthesis subagent turns the summaries into flowing prose", "means": "Prose with no tags is where the claim-to-source link disappears."}, {"quote": "The reporting subagent then cites each sentence by finding the summary that reads most like it", "means": "The decider. Citations are guesses. Stricter matching (<b>[[1]]</b>) or quotes (<b>[[2]]</b>) are still guesses; <b>[[3]]</b> removes the guess."}, {"quote": "Spot checks find more claims cited to the wrong paper", "means": "A wider problem, not a one-off. A release check (<b>[[0]]</b>) catches errors but leaves the cause."}], "rule": "Keep claim-to-source links through synthesis; never rebuild citations by similarity.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "Preserve structured claim-source mappings through synthesis"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s4-01", "was": "m4-s4-01", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Built-in tool selection", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The engineer wants to turn on a strict TypeScript check but skip the generated GraphQL files. The code generator names every output file <code>*.generated.ts</code> and writes a 'do not edit' comment at the top. An old formatter run rewrote the top lines of many files. Some generated files are leftovers that nothing imports any more. How should Claude <b>build the list</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Use Glob with the pattern <code>**/*.generated.ts</code> and take every matching path.", "why": "Best. Glob matches file paths, and every generated file follows the naming rule, leftovers included."}, {"t": "Use Grep for the 'do not edit' header comment and take every file that has it.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The formatter rewrote the top lines of many files, so some generated files no longer carry the header.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Read the code generator's config file and list every output file it declares.", "why": "Works, but misses old files. The config describes today's outputs; leftovers from removed queries are not in it.", "trap": "misses leftovers"}, {"t": "Use Grep on import lines that mention <code>.generated</code> and collect those paths.", "why": "Works, but looks from the wrong side. Imports show the files somebody uses, so leftovers that nothing imports are missed.", "trap": "misses leftovers"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The code generator names every output file *.generated.ts", "explanation": "When a reliable file-name pattern identifies the files, Glob is the tool. Grep searches contents, which only works when the content marker is reliable.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude must find every generated file. The clue is: <b>every output file is named <code>*.generated.ts</code></b>, while the header comment has been damaged in many files.", "answer": "Think of a library where every cookbook has a red spine, but some title pages were torn out. To find all the cookbooks, you look at the spines, not inside the books. Looking at names is <b>Glob</b>; looking inside is <b>Grep</b>. The name rule holds for every generated file, even unused leftovers. <b>Rule: if the name tells you, use Glob; if only the contents tell you, use Grep.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Every generated file has the same kind of name, so a name pattern finds them all.", "Checking the title page works for most books. Some title pages were torn out by the formatter.", "The catalogue lists only today's books. Old leftovers were never recorded there.", "This lists books that someone borrowed. Books nobody borrows are left off."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Grep is the search tool</b> for everything. Grep searches <i>inside</i> files. Here the reliable signal is the <b>file name</b>, and Glob matches file paths. The header inside the file was damaged, so content search now misses some files.", "diagram": "Glob **/*.generated.ts  → every generated file\nGrep 'do not edit'     → only files whose header survived\nGrep imports           → only files someone still imports", "mapTitle": "What reliably marks the files you want?", "map": {"head": ["Reliable marker", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["A file-name pattern", "Glob", "Matches paths, ignores contents"], ["Text inside the file", "Grep", "Searches contents across many files"], ["One known file to change", "Edit (or Read + Write)", "Targeted change"], ["Understanding a flow", "Grep entry points, then Read", "Follow the calls step by step"], ["A list kept in config", "Read the config", "Only as complete as the config is"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The code generator names every output file *.generated.ts", "means": "The decider. A name pattern covers every generated file, so Glob (<b>[[0]]</b>) finds them all."}, {"quote": "An old formatter run rewrote the top lines of many files", "means": "The header is no longer reliable, so a Grep for it (<b>[[1]]</b>) misses some files."}, {"quote": "Some generated files are leftovers that nothing imports any more", "means": "Leftovers are missing from imports (<b>[[3]]</b>) and from today's generator config (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "How should Claude build the list?", "means": "Match the reliable name pattern: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Reliable name pattern → Glob. Reliable text inside → Grep.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "Grep = content search; Glob = file path patterns"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s4-02", "was": "m4-s4-02", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Edit failure fallback", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A long deployment config has four environment blocks with the same settings. Each block's name sits on its first line, far above the settings. Every block contains <code>timeout: 30</code>, and only the staging block must change it to 60. Claude's <code>Edit</code> call fails because its old text matches four places. What should Claude <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Retry Edit with old text long enough to reach up to the staging name line.", "why": "Works, but clumsy. The old text would have to copy the whole block from its name line down to the value, which is long and easy to get wrong.", "trap": "clumsy"}, {"t": "Run <code>sed</code> through Bash on the line number of the staging timeout.", "why": "Works, but leaves the file tools. A shell edit by line number is fragile and skips the Read and Write route made for this case.", "trap": "wrong tool"}, {"t": "Read the whole file, change the staging value, then Write the file back.", "why": "Best. Edit needs a unique text match. When there is none, the fallback is Read plus Write, where Claude can use the block's position."}, {"t": "Ask the engineer to make this one change by hand in their own editor.", "why": "Works, but hands the job back. Claude can make this change itself with Read and Write.", "trap": "hands it back"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude's Edit call fails because its old text matches four places.", "explanation": "Edit makes a targeted change by unique text match. When the text is not unique, fall back to Read plus Write.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude tried to change one value with Edit, and Edit refused because the same text appears four times. The clue is: <b>the old text matches four places</b>.", "answer": "Edit is like find-and-replace that only works when the word appears once. Here the same line appears in four identical blocks. So Claude opens the whole file (<b>Read</b>), changes the line in the staging block, and saves the whole file (<b>Write</b>). <b>Rule: Edit fails on a non-unique match → Read the file and Write it back.</b>", "options": ["This can work, but Claude would have to copy a long, identical chunk exactly. Easy to get wrong.", "A shell edit by line number works, but it is fragile and skips the file tools.", "Correct. Read the whole file, change the right block, Write it back.", "The engineer could do it, but Claude can do it safely itself."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a longer match always fixes a failed Edit</b>. That works when the text near the change is different. Here the blocks are identical all the way up to their name lines. The documented fallback is <b>Read the whole file, then Write it back</b>.", "diagram": "production: ... timeout: 30\nqa:         ... timeout: 30\nstaging:    ... timeout: 30   ← change this one\ndev:        ... timeout: 30", "mapTitle": "Which file tool fits this change?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["Change text that appears once", "Edit", "Targeted change by unique match"], ["Change text that appears in several identical places", "Read + Write", "Edit cannot pick one; position can"], ["Change every copy of the text", "Edit with replace_all", "All matches are meant"], ["Create a new file", "Write", "Nothing to match"], ["Find files by name", "Glob", "Path patterns, not contents"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A long deployment config has four environment blocks with the same settings", "means": "Identical blocks mean any nearby text repeats too."}, {"quote": "Each block's name sits on its first line, far above the settings", "means": "A unique match would need a long copy of the block (<b>[[0]]</b>). Possible, but clumsy."}, {"quote": "Claude's Edit call fails because its old text matches four places", "means": "The decider. Edit needs a unique match, so the fallback is Read plus Write: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "What should Claude do next?", "means": "Claude can finish the job itself with its file tools. No need for sed (<b>[[1]]</b>) or the engineer (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}], "rule": "Edit fails because the text is not unique → Read the whole file and Write it back.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "When Edit fails on non-unique match → Read + Write fallback"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s5-01", "was": "s5-01", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Non-interactive CI invocation", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The review job runs <code>claude \"Check this diff for injection risks\"</code> in a workflow step. The engineer tested it in a terminal, where she read the answer and typed <code>/exit</code>. On the runner, the step sits idle until the job's time limit cancels it. The next step, which turns the reply into comments, then has nothing to read. What should the review step <b>run instead</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Wrap the command in an <code>expect</code> script that sends <code>/exit</code> once the answer appears.", "why": "Works, but a workaround. It fakes the person at the keyboard, and it must guess when the answer is finished. The CLI already has a mode that needs no person.", "trap": "workaround"}, {"t": "Set a short time limit on the step so a stuck run fails within minutes, not hours.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. The job fails sooner and wastes fewer runner hours, but it still produces no review.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Run <code>claude -p</code> with the same prompt, and have the next step read its standard output.", "why": "Best. <code>-p</code> (<code>--print</code>) is non-interactive mode: Claude answers once, prints the reply and exits, so nothing waits for a person."}, {"t": "Replace the step with a script that sends the diff to the Messages API and prints the reply.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A direct API call does finish, but it rebuilds the job by hand and gives up the Claude Code setup the repository already commits.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The engineer tested it in a terminal, where she read the answer and typed /exit.", "explanation": "In CI, Claude Code runs in print mode (-p / --print): one prompt in, one answer out, then exit. Without it the CLI waits for a person to type the next turn.", "eli5": {"question": "A command that worked in a terminal hangs forever in CI. The clue: in the terminal, a person <i>typed /exit</i> to finish it. On the runner, nobody is there to type.", "answer": "Think of a phone call that only ends when someone hangs up. In a terminal, the engineer hung up by typing <code>/exit</code>. In CI nobody is on the line, so the call stays open. Claude Code has a “leave a message” mode instead: <code>claude -p</code> (print mode) answers once, prints the reply and hangs up by itself. <b>Rule: Claude Code in a script or CI → always use -p.</b>", "options": ["A robot that hangs up for you works, but it has to guess when the other side is done talking.", "Cutting the call off sooner saves time, but you still get no message.", "Correct. Leave-a-message mode: answer once, then hang up by itself.", "Writing your own phone works, but you already own one with the right button."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a hang in CI needs a timeout or a trick to end the session</b>. The real cause is simpler: plain <code>claude \"...\"</code> starts an <i>interactive</i> session that waits for the next turn. The supported answer is <b>print mode</b>, <code>-p</code>, which is built for scripts.", "diagram": "claude \"...\"     → answers, then waits for input (hang)\nclaude -p \"...\"  → answers, prints, exits (CI-safe)", "mapTitle": "Claude Code in CI misbehaves. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Step hangs until the job is cancelled", "Interactive session waiting for input", "<code>claude -p</code>"], ["Step finishes, script can't parse the reply", "Free-text output", "<code>--output-format json</code> + <code>--json-schema</code>"], ["CI ignores team conventions", "Context not in the repository", "Project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>"], ["Review misses problems in its own code", "Same session wrote and reviews", "Independent review instance"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The engineer tested it in a terminal, where she read the answer and typed /exit", "means": "The decider. The command is interactive: it only ends when a person ends it. An <code>expect</code> script (<b>[[0]]</b>) fakes that person instead of not needing one."}, {"quote": "On the runner, the step sits idle until the job's time limit cancels it", "means": "Nobody types the next turn on the runner. A shorter timeout (<b>[[1]]</b>) only makes the failure quicker."}, {"quote": "The next step, which turns the reply into comments, then has nothing to read", "means": "The empty input is a symptom of the hang, not a separate problem."}, {"quote": "What should the review step run instead?", "means": "Keep Claude Code and its committed setup; switch it to print mode: <b>[[2]]</b>. Rebuilding on the raw API (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more work than the fix needs."}], "rule": "Claude Code in a pipeline → claude -p. It answers once and exits.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "-p / --print = non-interactive mode (no input hang)"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s5-04", "was": "s5-04", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Session context isolation", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "To save a runner start-up, one <code>claude -p</code> call does two jobs. It writes tests for the new endpoints, then reviews the whole pull request, new tests included. Human reviewers keep finding generated tests that assert on the wrong thing. The automated review has not flagged one of them. Its prompt lists only general quality checks. What would <b>most improve</b> the review of generated tests?", "options": [{"t": "Run the review as a separate <code>claude -p</code> call that gets the diff but no generation turns.", "why": "Best. The combined session still holds the reasoning that wrote the tests, so it reads them as settled. A fresh instance checks them the way the human reviewers do."}, {"t": "Add a review check that each assertion tests the behaviour that the test's name describes.", "why": "Works, but leaves the cause. Explicit criteria help any reviewer, but this reviewer is checking tests it has just decided were right.", "trap": "leaves the cause"}, {"t": "Turn on extended thinking for the review half, with room to re-derive each test's intent.", "why": "Works, but same blind spot. More reasoning in the same context still starts from the generation reasoning.", "trap": "same blind spot"}, {"t": "Split the review into per-file passes plus one cross-file pass over source and new tests.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Multi-pass review fixes thin attention on large diffs. The problem here is that the reviewer wrote what it reviews.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "one claude -p call does two jobs", "explanation": "A session is a weak reviewer of its own output, because it keeps the reasoning that produced it. An independent instance with no generation history catches more than self-review, even with extended thinking.", "eli5": {"question": "The same Claude call writes tests and then reviews them, and its review never finds the bad tests humans find. The clue: <i>one call does two jobs</i>.", "answer": "Marking your own homework rarely finds your mistakes: you read what you meant, not what you wrote. A different marker catches them. Here that means a separate <code>claude -p</code> call that sees the diff but not the reasoning that wrote the tests (an <b>independent review instance</b>). <b>Rule: never let the writer review its own work in the same session.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A fresh marker who never saw the working.", "A sharper marking scheme helps, but the student is still marking their own homework.", "Thinking longer about your own answers mostly convinces you they're right.", "Marking page by page helps with long homework, but it's still your own homework."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a review that misses problems needs better criteria or more thinking</b>. Those help an independent reviewer. Here the reviewer is the same session that wrote the tests, so it keeps the reasoning behind them and tends to agree with itself. The fix is <b>who reviews</b>.", "diagram": "now:  one session: write tests → review PR → approve\nfix:  session A writes → fresh claude -p reviews diff", "mapTitle": "A review step misses problems. What's wrong with it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Reviewer is the session that wrote the code", "Separate review instance", "No shared reasoning"], ["Independent reviewer lacks a specific check", "Explicit criteria for that check", "Says what counts"], ["Large diff reviewed in one pass", "Per-file passes + cross-file pass", "Avoids thin attention"], ["Hard reasoning, independent reviewer", "Extended thinking", "More room to reason"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "one claude -p call does two jobs", "means": "The decider. The writer and the reviewer share one context."}, {"quote": "Human reviewers keep finding generated tests that assert on the wrong thing", "means": "Independent readers catch what the session misses."}, {"quote": "Its prompt lists only general quality checks", "means": "Criteria (<b>[[1]]</b>) would help, but the same session would still apply them to its own tests."}, {"quote": "What would most improve the review of generated tests?", "means": "Change the reviewer, not its effort: <b>[[0]]</b>. Extended thinking (<b>[[2]]</b>) and more passes (<b>[[3]]</b>) keep the same reviewer."}], "rule": "Generated code is reviewed by a separate instance, not by the session that wrote it.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "the session that generated code is less effective at reviewing its own changes than an independent instance"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s5-05", "was": "s5-05", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria & precision", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Developers act on few of the review's findings. The biggest group says a doc comment is out of date, and most of those comments are fine. That group comes from one prompt line: “keep code documentation trustworthy”. Developers now skim past the whole review, including its accurate security findings. What change <b>most improves precision</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add three sample findings of stale doc comments, each with its file, line and suggested fix.", "why": "Works, but too early. Examples steady the shape of findings, but they rest on a goal with no boundary, so Claude still guesses what counts.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Have a second instance score each finding, and post only the ones it rates high.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A grader cuts some noise, but it judges against the same vague “trustworthy” and doubles the cost of every review.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Reword that line: flag a doc comment only when what it claims differs from what the code does.", "why": "Best. “Trustworthy” has no boundary, so almost any comment can be flagged. A condition comparing claim and code can be checked, and explicit criteria are what raise precision."}, {"t": "Post doc-comment findings in a folded summary at the end, not as inline comments.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. Security findings stand out again, but the doc-comment findings are as wrong as before.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "That group comes from one prompt line: “keep code documentation trustworthy”.", "explanation": "Vague goals produce vague findings. Replace a goal like “trustworthy” with a condition checkable against the code, because a noisy category erodes trust in the accurate findings too.", "eli5": {"question": "One vague prompt line creates lots of wrong findings, and people now ignore the whole review. The clue: the line says <i>trustworthy</i>, which has no clear edge.", "answer": "Telling a proofreader “make sure the captions are good” gets every caption flagged. Telling them “flag a caption only when it describes something not in the picture” gets the real errors. That is an <b>explicit criterion</b>: a test Claude can check against the code. <b>Rule: replace vague goals with conditions you can check.</b>", "options": ["Showing sample corrections helps, but the proofreader still doesn't know which captions count.", "A second proofreader with the same vague brief is just as unsure, and twice the cost.", "Correct. Give a test: flag it only when the words don't match the picture.", "Moving the corrections to the back hides them, but they are still wrong."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a noisy category needs examples, a filter or a quieter place</b>. Those treat its output. The cause is the instruction: “trustworthy” has no boundary. The exam guide's fix is an <b>explicit criterion</b>, for example flag a comment only when its claim contradicts the code.", "mapTitle": "Too many false findings. What sharpens them?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["One category is mostly false flags", "Vague goal in the prompt", "Explicit, checkable criterion"], ["Criterion clear, findings' shape varies", "Prose underspecifies output", "A few worked examples"], ["Prompt says “only if highly confident”", "Confidence wording", "Swap for categories"], ["Category can't be made precise yet", "No good test exists", "Turn it off for now"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The biggest group says a doc comment is out of date, and most of those comments are fine", "means": "One category is mostly false positives."}, {"quote": "That group comes from one prompt line: “keep code documentation trustworthy”", "means": "The decider. The cause is a vague goal. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a grader (<b>[[1]]</b>) both inherit it."}, {"quote": "Developers now skim past the whole review, including its accurate security findings", "means": "The noise costs trust in good findings. Folding it away (<b>[[3]]</b>) hides it without fixing it."}, {"quote": "What change most improves precision?", "means": "Precision comes from what counts as a finding: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "A vague goal makes false positives; rewrite it as a condition Claude can check against the code.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "Explicit criteria beat vague instructions (\"flag comments only when claimed behavior contradicts actual code\" vs \"check comments are accurate\")"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s5-06", "was": "s5-06", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for consistency", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The review prompt defines each severity level with a clear test, such as “reachable from an unauthenticated request”. Most findings get the same level on every run. A possible null dereference on a guarded path was marked critical on one pull request and minor on another. The swings happen where two of the tests seem to apply at once. What <b>most improves</b> consistency?", "options": [{"t": "Add more conditions to each level's definition to cover guarded and partly guarded paths.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Sharper wording helps when levels are vague. These are already clear tests, and more conditions add more to weigh at the boundary.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Add a few borderline findings, each with the level chosen and why the other level lost.", "why": "Best. The written tests are fine; the inconsistency sits where two of them overlap. Examples that show the reasoning on borderline cases make those calls consistent."}, {"t": "Run the review three times, and post each finding at the level most runs gave it.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Voting smooths some swings, but it triples the cost of a check developers wait on, and it hides the boundary rather than teaching it.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Ask Claude to reason step by step through each test before it picks a level.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Reasoning helps a little, but at the boundary two tests still fit, and Claude has nothing showing which one wins.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The swings happen where two of the tests seem to apply at once.", "explanation": "When instructions are already explicit but output still varies on borderline cases, few-shot examples are the most effective fix. Choose boundary cases and show why one level beat the other.", "eli5": {"question": "Severity rules are clear and work most of the time, but borderline findings flip between levels. The clue: the swings happen where <i>two tests apply at once</i>.", "answer": "A referee who knows the rulebook still calls borderline fouls differently. What helps is watching replays of borderline calls with the reason for each decision. For Claude, those replays are <b>few-shot examples</b>: a few boundary findings, each with the level chosen and why the other lost. <b>Rule: clear rules but inconsistent borderline calls → show worked examples.</b>", "options": ["A thicker rulebook gives the referee more rules to weigh against each other.", "Correct. Replays of borderline calls, with the reason for each.", "Three referees voting smooths some calls, at three times the cost, and none of them learns the boundary.", "Thinking harder helps a little, but two rules still fit and nothing shows which wins."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>inconsistent output means the rules need more words</b>. Here the rules are clear tests and work on most findings. The swings sit at the <b>overlap</b> between two tests. The exam guide's fix for that is <b>few-shot examples</b> that show the reasoning on ambiguous cases.", "mapTitle": "Output varies. Is it the rule or the boundary?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Clear tests, swings on borderline cases", "Overlap between tests", "2–4 boundary examples with reasons"], ["Levels described in vague words", "No real test to apply", "Explicit severity criteria"], ["One category mostly false flags", "Vague goal", "Checkable condition"], ["Review misses its own mistakes", "Same session reviews", "Independent instance"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The review prompt defines each severity level with a clear test", "means": "The criteria are already explicit, so adding more conditions (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more of the same."}, {"quote": "Most findings get the same level on every run", "means": "The rules work in clear cases."}, {"quote": "The swings happen where two of the tests seem to apply at once", "means": "The decider. A boundary problem, which examples teach: <b>[[1]]</b>. Voting (<b>[[2]]</b>) and step-by-step reasoning (<b>[[3]]</b>) don't show which test wins."}], "rule": "Clear criteria, inconsistent borderline calls → a few worked boundary examples with the reason for each.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "2-4 targeted examples showing reasoning for the chosen action"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s6-05", "was": "s6-05", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot across varied structures", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The prompt holds eight worked certificates, all picked from the six largest carriers because they were to hand. Accuracy is high for those carriers and much lower for the rest. Smaller carriers put limits in footnotes, in tables split across pages, or under labels like “Each Claim”. New carriers join often, and their first certificates arrive before anyone has seen the layout. What <b>most improves</b> accuracy on the smaller carriers?", "options": [{"t": "Swap most of the eight for small-carrier certificates with unusual layouts, each noting how fields were found.", "why": "Best. Examples teach generalisation when they show varied, awkward structures and the reasoning. Varied layouts carry over to carriers nobody has seen yet."}, {"t": "Add written layout rules: limits may sit in footnotes, tables may split, and Each Claim means per claim.", "why": "Works, but only for what's been seen. Rules cover the variations already listed. They don't show how to reason about a new layout, where examples do better.", "trap": "only what's seen"}, {"t": "Keep a prompt per carrier with that carrier's own samples, picked by the sender when a certificate arrives.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It lifts accuracy on known carriers, but a new carrier has no prompt yet, and there are many prompts to maintain.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Extend the prompt to one sample from every current carrier, ordered from the highest volume to the lowest.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It adds a large token cost to every document and teaches recall of known layouts, not how to handle the next one.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "New carriers join often, and their first certificates arrive before anyone has seen the layout.", "explanation": "Few-shot examples should show varied and ambiguous structures so the model can generalise to new patterns. Choose them where the model fails, not where it already succeeds.", "eli5": {"question": "All the worked examples come from the big carriers, and accuracy is poor on everyone else. The clue: new carriers arrive <i>before anyone has seen their layout</i>, so the model must cope with the unknown.", "answer": "If you train a new clerk only on the neatest forms, they freeze on the first messy one. Show them a few awkward forms and talk through how you found each box, and they learn to cope with forms they've never seen. Those are <b>varied few-shot examples with reasoning</b>. <b>Rule: pick examples from where the model struggles, and show how the answer was found.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A few awkward forms, with the reasoning, teach the clerk to handle new ones.", "A list of known quirks helps with those quirks, not with the next new one.", "A binder per carrier works for carriers you know; a new carrier has no binder yet.", "One example of every form is a huge binder, and still says nothing about the next new one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>more examples, or more specific examples, means better accuracy</b>. What matters is which examples. Eight variations of layouts the model already reads well teach nothing new. A few <b>varied, awkward examples with the reasoning shown</b> help the model generalise to layouts it has never seen.", "mapTitle": "Accuracy is low on some documents. What do examples need to show?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["New layouts keep arriving unseen", "Varied, awkward examples with reasoning", "Generalises to novel layouts"], ["Fixed set of known senders", "Per-sender prompts or examples", "Each layout covered directly"], ["Known quirks, few in number", "Written rules can do", "Nothing new to generalise"], ["Instructions alone give inconsistent output", "Add 2–4 targeted examples", "Most effective for consistency"], ["Examples all from easy cases", "Swap for hard cases", "Easy ones teach nothing new"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "all picked from the six largest carriers because they were to hand", "means": "The examples were chosen by convenience, not by where the model fails."}, {"quote": "Smaller carriers put limits in footnotes, in tables split across pages, or under labels like “Each Claim”", "means": "Varied structures, the case few-shot handles. Listing them as rules (<b>[[1]]</b>) covers only these."}, {"quote": "New carriers join often, and their first certificates arrive before anyone has seen the layout", "means": "The decider. Per-carrier prompts (<b>[[2]]</b>) and one sample per carrier (<b>[[3]]</b>) can't cover an unseen carrier."}, {"quote": "What most improves accuracy on the smaller carriers?", "means": "Varied examples that generalise: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Choose few-shot examples that show varied, awkward layouts with reasoning, so the model generalises to new ones.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Demonstrate ambiguous-case handling; enable generalization to novel patterns"}}}, {"id": "m4p-s6-06", "was": "s6-06", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Field-level calibration", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "The team measured 97% field accuracy on 200 certificates labelled during a quiet month. Now they want to post results without review. That month held mostly standard certificates. Long policy schedules arrive mainly at renewal time, so only a handful were in the set. Insured names were almost never wrong, and most errors were in limits on those schedules. What should happen <b>before review is removed</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep a random sample of posted results under review each week, and stop once a quarter passes cleanly.", "why": "Works, but too late. Ongoing sampling is useful, but a random sample has the same blind spot, and it checks after posting rather than deciding what may post.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Calibrate a confidence score per field on the labelled set, and route fields under a threshold to review.", "why": "Works, but too early. Calibrated field confidence is the right routing tool once the labelled set covers each type. A handful of schedules can't calibrate schedules.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Label a new sample with a fixed quota per document type, and report accuracy for every type and field.", "why": "Best. The first sample barely covers schedules and averages easy fields with hard ones. A quota per type gives each type and field its own figure, which is what decides what can skip review."}, {"t": "Run a second extraction on long schedules, and post only the limits on which both runs agree.", "why": "Works, but measures the wrong thing. Agreement shows repeatability, not accuracy, and it changes the extraction instead of measuring it.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Long policy schedules arrive mainly at renewal time, so only a handful were in the set.", "explanation": "Aggregate accuracy can hide poor performance on specific document types and fields. Measure by type and field, with stratified sampling so rare types are covered, before automating; calibrated confidence comes after.", "eli5": {"question": "A test said 97% accurate, but it was taken in a quiet month with almost no long schedules, and those are where the errors are. The clue: <i>only a handful</i> of schedules were in the set.", "answer": "If you test a new driver only on quiet Sunday roads, a great score says little about rush hour. Test them on a fixed number of trips of each kind, and score each kind separately. That is <b>stratified sampling</b>, with accuracy reported <b>per document type and field</b>. <b>Rule: one big average can hide a weak spot. Measure each type and field before trusting automation.</b>", "options": ["Spot-checking some trips later helps, but uses the same quiet roads, and the driver is already alone.", "Asking the driver how confident they feel needs rush-hour practice first to mean anything.", "Correct. Test each kind of road a set number of times, and score each kind separately.", "Two drivers agreeing doesn't prove either one drove well."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>97% accuracy means the system is ready</b>. An average over a sample that barely contains the hard documents hides where errors live. Before removing review, measure accuracy <b>by document type and by field</b>, using a sample with a quota per type. Calibrated confidence routing comes after, once there is enough labelled data for each type.", "diagram": "random sample:  standard ████████████  schedules █\nstratified:     standard ██████  schedules ██████", "mapTitle": "Ready to remove review? What evidence do you need?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Next step", "Why"], "rows": [["High average, rare types barely sampled", "Stratified sample, report per type and field", "Average hides weak spots"], ["Each type well covered by labels", "Calibrate field confidence, route low ones", "Threshold now means something"], ["Automation already live", "Ongoing sampled review", "Catches drift over time"], ["Model rates its own confidence", "Don't route on it uncalibrated", "Self-confidence is unreliable"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team measured 97% field accuracy on 200 certificates labelled during a quiet month", "means": "One average from one kind of month."}, {"quote": "Long policy schedules arrive mainly at renewal time, so only a handful were in the set", "means": "The decider. Schedules are barely measured, so calibrating on this set (<b>[[1]]</b>) or random checks (<b>[[0]]</b>) inherit the gap."}, {"quote": "Insured names were almost never wrong, and most errors were in limits on those schedules", "means": "Errors cluster by type and field, so they must be measured that way. Two runs agreeing (<b>[[3]]</b>) is not a measure of accuracy."}, {"quote": "What should happen before review is removed?", "means": "Measure each type and field first: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Before automating, validate accuracy per document type and field with a stratified sample. An overall figure can hide the weak spot.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Validate accuracy by document type and field before automating"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s1-01", "was": "s5q-s1-01", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Loop termination", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The agent's loop stops when Claude's reply contains a closing line, such as “Anything else I can help with?”. It also stops after fifteen steps. Some refunds are left half-done: the reply opened with a polite wrap-up line and also asked to run <code>process_refund</code>. Some long claims are cut off at the step limit while every step is still making progress. Which change <b>fixes both</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Raise the step limit to forty and set it per claim type from the step counts seen in the logs.", "why": "Works, but only half the problem. Long claims get room to finish, but the loop still ends on wording, so a wrap-up line beside a refund request still cuts the refund off.", "trap": "only half the problem"}, {"t": "Loop while <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>, and keep a high cap as a backstop.", "why": "Best. The stop reason says whether Claude asked for a tool, whatever the text says. Long claims run until Claude is done, and a high cap only guards against runaway loops."}, {"t": "Skip the closing-line check on any reply that also asks to run a tool, and keep the step limit.", "why": "Works, but treats one symptom. Half-done refunds stop, but the loop still guesses from text, and long claims are still cut off at the step limit.", "trap": "treats one symptom"}, {"t": "Have a small classifier read each reply and judge whether the customer's case is finished.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A classifier may judge endings better than a phrase list, but it is still guessing from text. The API already reports this in <code>stop_reason</code>.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Some refunds are left half-done: the reply opened with a polite wrap-up line and also asked to run process_refund.", "explanation": "The loop should continue when stop_reason is tool_use and stop on end_turn. Reading the reply's wording and using an iteration cap as the main stop are both anti-patterns.", "eli5": {"question": "The loop decides it is done by spotting a goodbye phrase or by counting steps. Both go wrong. The clue: a reply said goodbye <i>and</i> asked for a refund in the same breath.", "answer": "Think of a waiter who leaves the table as soon as a guest says “thanks”, even when the guest is still ordering. Better to look at the order pad: is there an order on it or not? Claude's order pad is <b><code>stop_reason</code></b>. <code>tool_use</code> means “run this for me and come back”. <code>end_turn</code> means “I'm finished”. <b>Rule: decide when to stop from stop_reason, not from words or a step count.</b>", "options": ["A longer shift helps the slow tables, but the waiter still walks off when someone says “thanks”.", "Correct. Look at the order pad: keep serving while there is an order, stop when there isn't. A high cap is only a safety net.", "Ignoring “thanks” when an order is on the pad fixes one case, but the shift still ends too early for long tables.", "Hiring someone to judge the mood of each table still guesses. The order pad already tells you."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to see <b>two separate bugs</b>: a phrase check that misfires and a step limit that is too low. They share one cause. The loop is guessing when Claude is done, when <b>Claude already says so</b> in <code>stop_reason</code> on every response.", "diagram": "reply → stop_reason?\n  tool_use → run tool, add result, call again\n  end_turn → done, answer the customer\ncap (high) → only for runaway loops", "mapTitle": "How should the loop decide it is finished?", "map": {"head": ["Signal used", "What goes wrong", "Better"], "rows": [["Closing phrase in the text", "Text and a tool request can come together", "<code>stop_reason</code>"], ["Fixed step count as the main stop", "Long, healthy tasks are cut off", "<code>stop_reason</code>, cap as backstop"], ["Phrase check skipped when a tool is asked for", "Patches one case, still reads text", "<code>stop_reason</code>"], ["Model judging its own text", "Still a guess, extra machinery", "<code>stop_reason</code>"], ["<code>stop_reason</code> = <code>tool_use</code> / <code>end_turn</code>", "Nothing: this is the contract", "Keep it"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The agent's loop stops when Claude's reply contains a closing line", "means": "Completion is read from text, an anti-pattern in the guide."}, {"quote": "It also stops after fifteen steps", "means": "A step cap is the main stop. Raising it (<b>[[0]]</b>) moves the cut-off, it doesn't remove it."}, {"quote": "the reply opened with a polite wrap-up line and also asked to run process_refund", "means": "The decider. Text and a tool request arrive together, so any text check can misfire. Skipping it only here (<b>[[2]]</b>) fixes one case."}, {"quote": "while every step is still making progress", "means": "The work is healthy; only the stopping rule is wrong."}, {"quote": "Which change fixes both?", "means": "One change for both symptoms: branch on <code>stop_reason</code>, <b>[[1]]</b>. A classifier (<b>[[3]]</b>) still guesses from text."}], "rule": "Continue on tool_use, stop on end_turn. A cap is a safety net, never the main stop.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "Anti-patterns: parsing natural-language signals to determine termination; arbitrary iteration caps as the primary stop"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s1-05", "was": "s5q-s1-05", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Ambiguity resolution", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "<code>get_customer</code> searches by the name the customer gives. For common names it often finds several accounts. It then returns the one with the latest box. Sometimes that is the wrong person, and a refund goes to a stranger's card. Most chats start in the help widget before the customer signs in. What should the agent do when a search <b>finds several accounts</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Use the account tied to the signed-in session, and fall back to the name search when there is none.", "why": "Works, but only some chats. A signed-in account beats any name match, but most chats start before sign-in, so most still fall back to the same guess.", "trap": "only some chats"}, {"t": "Take the top match only when its match score is high, and escalate the rest with the candidate list.", "why": "Works, but still guesses. Fewer wrong picks get through, but high-scoring guesses can still be wrong, and many easy chats go to a person.", "trap": "still guesses"}, {"t": "Show the agent every match and have it ask the customer for a box ID or postcode before acting.", "why": "Best. With several matches, the deciding fact is one only the customer knows. One question replaces a guess with a lookup."}, {"t": "Rank matches by recent boxes and city, then read the chosen name and city back to confirm.", "why": "Works, but can confirm the wrong one. Reading back catches some mistakes, but two people with a common name in the same city will both say yes.", "trap": "can confirm the wrong one"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Most chats start in the help widget before the customer signs in.", "explanation": "When a lookup returns several customer matches, ask the customer for an identifier instead of choosing one by heuristic.", "eli5": {"question": "A name search finds several people, and the agent picks one by guessing. Sometimes it picks the wrong one and refunds a stranger. The clue: most customers aren't signed in yet, so the agent has nothing but the name.", "answer": "At a busy pharmacy, three people called Priya Shah are waiting. The pharmacist doesn't hand the bag to whoever came most recently. They ask for a date of birth. That is <b>asking for an identifier</b> instead of picking by a rule of thumb. <b>Rule: several matches → ask the customer for something only they know.</b>", "options": ["Using the name on a loyalty card works for card holders, but most people at the counter don't show one.", "Handing over only when you're quite sure still guesses, and sends many easy cases to the manager.", "Correct. Ask for the date of birth before handing over the bag.", "Reading the name back doesn't help when two Priyas live on the same street."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better way to pick</b> will fix it: a score, a ranking, or a read-back. Each is still a guess. When several accounts match, the fact that decides it is one <b>only the customer knows</b>, and asking costs one turn.", "mapTitle": "A lookup is ambiguous. How do you pick the account?", "map": {"head": ["What you have", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Several matches, customer not signed in", "Ask for a box ID or postcode", "Replaces a guess with a lookup"], ["Customer signed in to a verified account", "Use the session's account", "Identity already proven"], ["One clear match", "Use it", "Nothing to resolve"], ["Match score or ranking rule", "Don't pick by it", "Heuristic selection"], ["Refund before identity is verified", "Prerequisite gate", "Blocks money moves until verified"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "For common names it often finds several accounts", "means": "An ambiguous lookup: the classic case for asking."}, {"quote": "It then returns the one with the latest box", "means": "Heuristic selection. Scores (<b>[[1]]</b>) and rankings (<b>[[3]]</b>) are more of the same."}, {"quote": "a refund goes to a stranger's card", "means": "The cost of a wrong pick is high."}, {"quote": "Most chats start in the help widget before the customer signs in", "means": "The decider. A session account (<b>[[0]]</b>) helps only the few signed-in chats. Asking works for all: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Several matches → ask for an identifier. Never pick by heuristic.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Multiple customer matches → ask for identifiers (not heuristic selection)"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s1-07", "was": "s5q-s1-07", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 tool_choice", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A new <code>get_loyalty_tier</code> tool returns the customer's tier, and the tier decides which remedies they may get. Product wants the agent's first reply in every chat to greet the customer with their tier perks. Each chat request already carries the signed-in customer ID. An engineer proposes setting <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> on the first request. Which setup <b>best meets the need</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep <code>any</code> on the first request; at the start of a chat the tier lookup is the likely pick.", "why": "Works, but the wrong tool may run. <code>any</code> forces some tool call, but Claude still chooses which, and <code>get_customer</code> is just as likely a first move.", "trap": "wrong tool may run"}, {"t": "Force <code>get_loyalty_tier</code> by name on the first request, then go back to <code>auto</code>.", "why": "Best. A forced tool choice makes that exact tool run first, so the greeting can use the tier. Going back to <code>auto</code> lets the rest of the chat flow freely."}, {"t": "Keep <code>auto</code> and add a gate that blocks remedy tools until the tier lookup has returned.", "why": "Works, but different problem. A gate orders remedies behind the tier, but nothing makes the lookup happen before the first reply.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Keep <code>auto</code> and open the system prompt with an instruction to look up the tier first.", "why": "Works, but only usually works. The instruction makes the lookup likely, not certain, and the need is every chat.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Product wants the agent's first reply in every chat to greet the customer with their tier perks.", "explanation": "tool_choice any forces some tool call but lets Claude pick which. To make one specific tool run first, force it by name, then return to auto.", "eli5": {"question": "One particular tool must run before the first reply in every chat. Someone suggests a setting that forces <i>a</i> tool. The clue: it has to be <i>this</i> tool, every time.", "answer": "A host greeting guests is told “check the guest list before you say hello”. Saying “check something first” lets them check the coat rack instead. Saying “check <i>the guest list</i> first” leaves no room. That is forcing a tool by name: <code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": ...}</code>. <code>any</code> only means “use some tool”. <b>Rule: one specific tool must run first → force it by name, then go back to auto.</b>", "options": ["“Check something first” might mean the coat rack, not the guest list.", "Correct. “Check the guest list first”, then carry on as normal.", "Locking the gift table until the list is checked is useful, but the host can still greet without it.", "A note asking the host to check the list works most nights, not every night."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b><code>any</code> means “run the right tool”</b>. It only means “call some tool rather than reply in text”. Claude still picks which. When one specific tool must run, <b>force it by name</b> for that request.", "mapTitle": "Which tool_choice setting fits?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Setting", "Effect"], "rows": [["This exact tool must run now", "Forced <code>{\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":...}</code>", "That tool runs, no choice"], ["Some tool must run, Claude picks which", "<code>any</code>", "A tool call, never plain text"], ["Normal conversation", "<code>auto</code>", "Claude may call a tool or reply"], ["Tool B only after tool A has run", "Prerequisite gate", "Orders calls, doesn't start them"], ["Encourage a habit, misses are fine", "Prompt instruction", "Likely, not certain"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Product wants the agent's first reply in every chat to greet the customer with their tier perks", "means": "The decider. The tier must be fetched before the first reply, every chat. A prompt line (<b>[[3]]</b>) is only likely."}, {"quote": "Each chat request already carries the signed-in customer ID", "means": "The tool has its input ready on the first request, so forcing it works."}, {"quote": "An engineer proposes setting tool_choice to any on the first request", "means": "<code>any</code> forces a tool, not this tool (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Which setup best meets the need?", "means": "Force the tool by name, then return to <code>auto</code>: <b>[[1]]</b>. A gate (<b>[[2]]</b>) orders remedies, not the greeting."}], "rule": "any = some tool. Forced = this tool. Force by name when one tool must run first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Force a tool to run first (e.g., extract_metadata before enrichment); \"any\" guarantees a tool call rather than text"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s2-05", "was": "s5q-s2-05", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Interview pattern", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer wrote a prose spec for a codemod that moves <code>mobile/</code> to a new date library. Three runs each made different guesses about cases the spec never mentions: commented-out calls, calls in test fixtures, and import aliases. Each time, the engineer had not thought of the case until a run tripped over it. They want the gaps found before the next run. What is the <b>most effective next step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add before-and-after file pairs for the three known cases to the spec, then rerun the codemod.", "why": "Works, but only for known cases. Examples beat prose for the three cases already found, but each run uncovers a new case nobody had thought of.", "trap": "only known cases"}, {"t": "Have Claude ask the engineer about edge cases and open choices before it writes any code.", "why": "Best. The interview pattern has Claude surface the questions the author has not thought of, before any code is written."}, {"t": "Run the next attempt in plan mode and review the proposed change for each file before approving.", "why": "Works, but slow. Plan mode would show some odd cases inside per-file plans, but reading many plans is a slow way to find missing requirements.", "trap": "indirect"}, {"t": "Send an Explore subagent to list unusual call patterns across <code>mobile/</code> before the next run.", "why": "Works, but half the job. It finds odd code patterns, but not what the engineer wants done with each one.", "trap": "half the job"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each time, the engineer had not thought of the case until a run tripped over it.", "explanation": "When each attempt exposes a requirement the author had not considered, use the interview pattern: Claude asks questions before implementing. Concrete examples or tests come once the cases are known.", "eli5": {"question": "Every run of a code change trips over a case nobody planned for. The clue: the engineer had not thought of those cases at all. The missing piece is in the engineer's head, not in the code.", "answer": "Before a builder starts, a good one sits down and asks: “What about the back door? What about the old pipes?” Questions find the gaps before the work begins. That is the <b>interview pattern</b>: Claude asks the engineer questions first. <b>Rule: requirements you haven't thought of yet → have Claude interview you before it builds.</b>", "options": ["Showing examples helps for the three cases you know. The fourth case is still unknown.", "Correct. The builder asks questions first and finds the gaps.", "Reading the builder's plan for every room might show a gap, but it is a slow way to find them.", "A survey of the house finds odd corners, but not what you want done in each."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a clearer spec fixes inconsistent runs</b>. Clearer examples help only for cases the author already knows. Here every run reveals a case the author had <i>not thought of</i>. The interview pattern turns that around: Claude asks about edge cases and open choices before coding.", "mapTitle": "Runs keep going wrong: what is missing?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "What is missing", "Technique"], "rows": [["Each run hits a case the author never considered", "Unknown requirements", "Interview pattern"], ["Known cases described in prose are misread", "A clear picture of the change", "Before-and-after examples"], ["Known behaviour must stay correct", "A check that can fail", "Tests written first"], ["Large change with several valid designs", "An agreed approach", "Plan mode"], ["Unfamiliar code to explore", "A map of the code", "Explore subagent"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Three runs each made different guesses about cases the spec never mentions", "means": "The spec has gaps, and Claude fills them with guesses."}, {"quote": "Each time, the engineer had not thought of the case until a run tripped over it", "means": "The decider. The gaps are unknown to the author, so examples of known cases (<b>[[0]]</b>) cannot close them."}, {"quote": "They want the gaps found before the next run", "means": "Find gaps before coding. A code survey (<b>[[3]]</b>) finds patterns but not decisions; plan review (<b>[[2]]</b>) is slow."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective next step?", "means": "Claude asks first: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Requirements the author hasn't thought of → interview pattern before implementation.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "the interview pattern (Claude asks questions first)"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s2-06", "was": "s5q-s2-06", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Message batching strategy", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Four small tasks remain. Rename the <code>Ledger</code> type in <code>packages/shared/</code> to <code>Journal</code>. Update the 40 files that import it. Add a CHANGELOG line, whose wording the release template fixes. Bump a version constant in an unrelated package. The import paths depend on whether the type moves to a new module during the rename. The reviewer wants all four in one pull request. How should the engineer <b>send these to Claude</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Send the rename, the import updates and the CHANGELOG line in one message, and the version bump next.", "why": "Works, but groups by theme. The CHANGELOG wording is fixed by the template, so it does not interact with the rename and only adds noise to that message.", "trap": "groups by theme"}, {"t": "Send all four tasks in one message, let Claude pick the order, and review the whole diff at the end.", "why": "Works, but dilutes attention. One pull request is a review grouping, not a reasoning one, and the two tasks that interact get less focus.", "trap": "dilutes attention"}, {"t": "Send the rename and the import updates together, then the CHANGELOG line and the bump one at a time.", "why": "Best. The imports depend on how the rename is done, so those two go in one message. The other two depend on nothing and go one at a time."}, {"t": "Send each of the four tasks as its own message, in order, and review the diff after each one.", "why": "Works, but splits a pair. Sent alone, the rename settles where the type lives without weighing the imports that depend on it.", "trap": "splits a pair"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The import paths depend on whether the type moves to a new module during the rename.", "explanation": "Send interacting problems in one message and independent ones in sequence. Group by dependency, not by theme or by which pull request they land in.", "eli5": {"question": "Four jobs, one pull request. The clue: only the rename and the imports depend on each other. The CHANGELOG wording is fixed in advance, and the version bump is in another package.", "answer": "When you move a cupboard, you tell the movers about the cupboard and the things that hang on it in the same breath. The shopping list and the bin can wait for their own turns. Tell Claude about <b>interacting problems in one message</b> and the independent ones in sequence. <b>Rule: group by what depends on what, not by topic or pull request.</b>", "options": ["Mentioning the CHANGELOG with the rename feels natural, but its wording is already fixed, so it only adds noise.", "One big message works, but the two tasks that matter together get less attention.", "Correct. The pair that depends on each other goes together; the rest go one by one.", "One at a time works, but the rename gets decided without thinking about its imports."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>tasks that ship together should be sent together</b>. A pull request groups work for review. Claude reasons best when <b>tasks that interact share a message</b> and independent ones come one at a time. Only the rename and its imports interact here.", "mapTitle": "Which tasks share a message?", "map": {"head": ["Relationship", "How to send", "Example here"], "rows": [["One task's result shapes another", "Same message", "Rename + imports"], ["Fully independent", "Its own message, in sequence", "Version bump"], ["Same topic, but output fixed in advance", "Its own message", "CHANGELOG line from the template"], ["Same pull request only", "Not a reason to group", "All four"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Add a CHANGELOG line, whose wording the release template fixes", "means": "The note does not depend on the rename, so grouping it with the rename (<b>[[0]]</b>) is grouping by theme."}, {"quote": "Bump a version constant in an unrelated package", "means": "Independent: its own message."}, {"quote": "The import paths depend on whether the type moves to a new module during the rename", "means": "The decider. The rename and the imports interact, so splitting them (<b>[[3]]</b>) loses that link."}, {"quote": "The reviewer wants all four in one pull request", "means": "A review grouping, not a reasoning one. One big message (<b>[[1]]</b>) dilutes the pair that matters."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer send these to Claude?", "means": "Pair together, the rest in sequence: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Interacting problems in one message; independent ones in sequence.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "single message for interacting problems, sequential for independent ones"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s2-07", "was": "s5q-s2-07", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Scratchpads & manifests", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "A codemod changes about 70 files and runs for over an hour. It has been cut off twice, once by a crash and once when the engineer needed the machine. Before restarting, the engineer hand-fixed some converted files and reverted one. Both times the run started over, as Claude could not tell which files were done. Which change makes the work resumable with the <b>least added complexity</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude keep a progress file of each target file and its status, read on every restart.", "why": "Best. A manifest on disk survives crashes and records progress outside the conversation. The engineer can note their own changes in it too."}, {"t": "Resume the interrupted session by name with <code>--resume</code> and have Claude carry on.", "why": "Works, but stale. Resuming suits a session where nothing changed since. The engineer edited and reverted files, so the old tool results describe a state that is gone.", "trap": "stale view"}, {"t": "Have Claude pause after every few files and print a summary for the engineer to keep by hand.", "why": "Works, but manual. The engineer becomes the progress record and must paste it back after each crash.", "trap": "manual record"}, {"t": "Split the work into runs of about ten files, each started fresh with its own list of files.", "why": "Works, but only shrinks the loss. A crash still loses part of a batch, and nothing records which files inside it were done.", "trap": "shrinks the loss"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Before restarting, the engineer hand-fixed some converted files and reverted one.", "explanation": "Long mechanical work needs durable state outside the conversation. A manifest file survives crashes and context limits; a resumed session restores what was said, not what is now on disk.", "eli5": {"question": "A long job keeps getting cut off and starts again from zero. The clue: between runs, the engineer changed some files by hand, so the old conversation no longer matches the disk.", "answer": "Painters tick off each room on a sheet taped to the door. If they go home, the sheet tells tomorrow's crew where to start, even if the owner repainted one room overnight. That sheet is a <b>manifest</b> (a progress file on disk). <b>Rule: long work that must survive crashes → keep a progress file on disk, not just a conversation.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The sheet on the door survives anything and can be updated.", "Replaying yesterday's chat works if nothing changed. Here the owner changed rooms overnight.", "Asking the painter to phone you after every few rooms works, but you become the sheet.", "Smaller jobs lose less, but you still don't know which rooms in a batch were done."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>resuming the old session restores progress</b>. It restores the conversation, including tool results from before the hand edits. A <b>manifest file on disk</b> records progress outside the conversation, survives a crash, and can be updated when files change.", "mapTitle": "Long work keeps getting cut off: how do you keep its place?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Long mechanical work across many files", "Manifest file on disk", "Survives crashes and edits"], ["Short pause, nothing changed since", "<code>--resume</code> the named session", "Context is still true"], ["Files changed since the last session", "Fresh session with a structured summary", "Avoids stale tool results"], ["Context getting full mid-task", "<code>/compact</code>", "Space, not progress"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It has been cut off twice, once by a crash and once when the engineer needed the machine", "means": "Progress must live somewhere that survives a crash."}, {"quote": "Before restarting, the engineer hand-fixed some converted files and reverted one", "means": "The decider. The old session's tool results are stale, so <code>--resume</code> (<b>[[1]]</b>) carries a wrong picture."}, {"quote": "Claude could not tell which files were done", "means": "The missing piece is a record of status per file. Smaller batches (<b>[[3]]</b>) don't add one."}, {"quote": "Which change makes the work resumable with the least added complexity?", "means": "One file Claude updates and reads: <b>[[0]]</b>. Manual summaries (<b>[[2]]</b>) put the job on the engineer."}], "rule": "Long work that must survive interruptions → a manifest on disk that the next run reads.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Structured state persistence (manifests) for crash recovery"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s2-10", "was": "s5q-s2-10", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.6 Multi-pass review architecture", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A CI job reviews the finished codemod by running <code>claude -p</code> on a clean checkout of the branch. Its report on about 70 changed files is thorough for the first few and thin in the middle. It missed a moved module whose caller still uses the old import. The team wants the review to catch problems in every file and between files. Which review design <b>best fits</b> this change?", "options": [{"t": "Have a separate Claude instance, with no access to the codemod's session, review the full diff.", "why": "Works, but already done. An independent reviewer beats self-review, but the CI job already starts fresh on a clean checkout. It is still one pass over everything.", "trap": "already in place"}, {"t": "Run the review with <code>--output-format json</code> and a schema, and post findings as PR comments.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Structured findings improve how the review is delivered, not what a single pass notices.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add the team's review criteria and the new API's contract to <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for the CI reviewer.", "why": "Works, but more context, same spread. CLAUDE.md is the right home for review criteria, but one pass is still spread thin across every file.", "trap": "still one pass"}, {"t": "Review each file on its own for local issues, then do a second pass over calls between files.", "why": "Best. Per-file passes give each file full attention, and a separate integration pass checks contracts between files, where the missed caller sits."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Its report on about 70 changed files is thorough for the first few and thin in the middle.", "explanation": "Large multi-file changes need per-file passes for local correctness and a cross-file integration pass for contracts between files. One pass over everything dilutes attention.", "eli5": {"question": "One reviewer reads 70 files in one go. They are careful at the start, skim the middle, and miss a problem that sits between two files. The clue: attention thins as the pile grows.", "answer": "An exam marker with a huge stack marks the first scripts carefully and rushes the rest. Better: mark each script on its own, then do one separate check that the answers fit together. That is a <b>multi-pass review</b>: per-file passes plus a cross-file integration pass. <b>Rule: big multi-file change → review each file alone, then review the links between them.</b>", "options": ["A fresh marker helps when the author marks their own work. This marker is already fresh; the stack is the problem.", "A neater report form changes how marks are written down, not how carefully scripts are read.", "A better marking guide helps, but the marker still rushes through a huge stack.", "Correct. Each script gets full attention, and a separate check catches answers that don't fit together."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a weak review means the reviewer is not independent</b>. This CI reviewer already starts fresh on a clean checkout. The real problem is <b>attention spread across too many files in one pass</b>. Split it: one pass per file, then an integration pass for the links between files.", "diagram": "file 1 -> pass   file 2 -> pass   ...   file 70 -> pass\n          \\________ integration pass ________/\n             calls and imports between files", "mapTitle": "A code review misses things: what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Thin in the middle, cross-file bugs missed", "Attention spread across one pass", "Per-file passes + integration pass"], ["Reviewer agrees with the author's reasoning", "Self-review in the same session", "Independent instance"], ["Findings hard to post or parse", "Free-text output", "<code>--output-format json</code> + schema"], ["Reviewer ignores team standards", "Missing project context", "Criteria in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "running claude -p on a clean checkout of the branch", "means": "Already independent of the session that wrote the code, so a separate instance (<b>[[0]]</b>) changes little."}, {"quote": "Its report on about 70 changed files is thorough for the first few and thin in the middle", "means": "The decider. Attention dilution in one pass. More context (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a new format (<b>[[1]]</b>) keeps one pass."}, {"quote": "It missed a moved module whose caller still uses the old import", "means": "A bug between files: needs an integration pass."}, {"quote": "Which review design best fits this change?", "means": "Per-file plus cross-file passes: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Big multi-file change → per-file passes plus a cross-file integration pass.", "guide": {"obj": "4.6 Multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures", "quote": "Multi-pass: per-file local analysis + cross-file integration passes (avoid attention dilution, contradictions)"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s3-07", "was": "s5q-s3-07", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Temporal & conflicting sources", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "Synthesis flagged a conflict about surgical infections. One paper puts the infection rate at 4%, and another puts it at 9%. Both are reputable, and the committee could not tell which to trust. A reviewer later found the two papers studied patients a decade apart. Each source record holds a title, a DOI and a section, and nothing else. Which change <b>best handles</b> this kind of conflict in future summaries?", "options": [{"t": "Have synthesis show both figures side by side, each with its source, and mark the disagreement.", "why": "Works, but only half. Showing both with attribution is right for a real conflict, but readers still can't see that the figures cover different years.", "trap": "only half"}, {"t": "Have citation checking confirm each figure in its paper, and drop any it can't find word for word.", "why": "Works, but different problem. It catches figures the papers don't contain. Both figures here are real, so both pass.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Tell synthesis that when two reputable figures disagree, the more recently published paper wins.", "why": "Works, but picks blindly. Newer is often better, but the rule throws away a valid figure and hides the time difference.", "trap": "picks arbitrarily"}, {"t": "Record each source's publication date and study period, and have synthesis compare periods first.", "why": "Best. With dates on every source, synthesis can tell a time difference from a real conflict, and attribute both when periods match."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each source record holds a title, a DOI and a section, and nothing else.", "explanation": "Require publication and data-collection dates on sources so temporal differences are not read as contradictions. Genuine conflicts over the same period are shown with attribution, not resolved arbitrarily.", "eli5": {"question": "Two good papers give different infection rates. They looked at patients ten years apart, but nothing in the system records dates. The clue: the source record holds <i>nothing else</i> but title, DOI and section.", "answer": "Two weather reports disagree: one says snow, one says sun. If you see one is from January and one from July, there's no conflict. Without dates you can't tell. So add <b>publication date and study period</b> to every source record, and have synthesis compare periods before calling it a conflict. Real conflicts over the same period are then shown side by side. <b>Rule: date every source so a time gap isn't mistaken for a contradiction.</b>", "options": ["Showing both reports with their names is fair, but readers still don't see one is from winter.", "Checking each report says what it claims is good, but both do.", "“Trust the newest report” sometimes picks right, but it hides why they differ.", "Correct. Put a date on every report, then compare."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to treat two different figures as a <b>contradiction to resolve</b>: show both, verify both, or pick one. Often they measure <i>different periods</i>. The exam guide says to require publication and collection dates so time differences aren't misread as contradictions. Showing both with attribution is the rule for a real conflict over the same period.", "mapTitle": "Two sources disagree. What should synthesis do?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Figures differ; studies cover different years", "Time gap, not conflict", "Record dates, compare periods first"], ["Figures differ for the same period", "Real conflict", "Show both with attribution"], ["A figure isn't in the cited paper", "Bad extraction or citation", "Verify against the source"], ["Pressure to give one number", "Arbitrary choice", "Don't pick one; annotate the conflict"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "One paper puts the infection rate at 4%, and another puts it at 9%", "means": "Two real figures that look like a conflict."}, {"quote": "Both are reputable, and the committee could not tell which to trust", "means": "Both pass a check (<b>[[1]]</b>), and choosing one by rule (<b>[[2]]</b>) is arbitrary."}, {"quote": "A reviewer later found the two papers studied patients a decade apart", "means": "It is a time difference. Side-by-side figures alone (<b>[[0]]</b>) don't show that."}, {"quote": "Each source record holds a title, a DOI and a section, and nothing else", "means": "The decider. No dates, so synthesis can't compare periods. Add them: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Require publication and study-period dates on sources; compare periods before calling figures a conflict.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "Require publication/collection dates so temporal differences aren't misread as contradictions"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s4-05", "was": "s5q-s4-05", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Adaptive investigation", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "For error spikes, the engineer gives Claude a fixed plan: read the stack trace, the failing method and its callers, check the deploy diff, then propose a fix. Last time the method was fine. The bad value came from an upstream API client after another team's change. Claude still finished the plan and proposed a guard in the method. How should such investigations be <b>run</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Hand each step of the plan to its own subagent, and have a coordinator merge the results.", "why": "Works, but the steps are still fixed. Subagents split the work, but none of them can follow a lead the plan didn't foresee.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Begin in plan mode, and have Claude write the full step list before it reads any code.", "why": "Works, but the list is fixed before any evidence. Plan mode suits designing a change, not an investigation whose next step depends on findings.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Keep the fixed plan, and add steps for upstream API clients and other teams' recent changes.", "why": "Works, but only for causes you foresaw. The next surprise will sit somewhere the longer list doesn't cover.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Give Claude the goal and the evidence so far, and let it pick each next step as it goes.", "why": "Best. When the method proves fine, Claude can turn upstream at once instead of finishing steps that no longer fit."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Last time the method was fine.", "explanation": "Fixed sequential pipelines suit predictable work. Open-ended investigations need adaptive decomposition, where each next step comes from what the last one found.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude followed a checklist even after the evidence pointed elsewhere. The clue: <i>Last time the method was fine</i>, yet the plan carried on as if it weren't.", "answer": "Think of a doctor with a fixed checklist: check the knee, x-ray the knee, operate on the knee. If the knee is fine and the pain comes from the back, the checklist still ends in knee surgery. A good doctor picks each next test from the last result. For Claude, give the goal and the evidence, and let it choose each next step (<b>adaptive investigation</b>). <b>Rule: steps known in advance → fixed plan. Next step depends on findings → let Claude adapt.</b>", "options": ["Four doctors each doing one knee step still all look at the knee.", "Writing a fuller checklist before seeing the patient is still a checklist written blind.", "Adding a back check helps this time, but the next pain may come from somewhere else again.", "Correct. Each test is chosen from the last result."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the plan just missed a step</b>, so a longer plan will fix it. The real fault is that the steps were <b>fixed before anything was found</b>. An investigation is open-ended: each next step depends on what the last one showed. That calls for adaptive decomposition, not a better fixed list.", "diagram": "fixed:    trace -> method -> callers -> diff -> fix\n          (method fine, plan carries on)\nadaptive: trace -> method fine -> look upstream -> ...", "mapTitle": "Fixed plan or adaptive investigation?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of work", "Structure", "Why"], "rows": [["Open-ended investigation", "Goal plus evidence; Claude picks each next step", "Next step depends on findings"], ["Same steps every time, such as per-file review", "Fixed sequence (prompt chaining)", "Predictable and easy to check"], ["Known extra causes to check every time", "Longer fixed checklist", "Covers only what was foreseen"], ["Independent parts to cover in parallel", "Subagents with a coordinator", "Splits the work, not the choice of steps"], ["Designing a large code change", "Plan mode", "Explore and decide before editing"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the engineer gives Claude a fixed plan", "means": "The steps are set before anything is read."}, {"quote": "Last time the method was fine", "means": "The decider. The findings changed direction, but the plan could not."}, {"quote": "The bad value came from an upstream API client after another team's change", "means": "The cause sat outside every step. Adding this step (<b>[[2]]</b>) covers this case, not the next one."}, {"quote": "Claude still finished the plan and proposed a guard in the method", "means": "The plan overrode the evidence. Only <b>[[3]]</b> lets the evidence choose the next step; <b>[[0]]</b> and <b>[[1]]</b> still fix the steps up front."}], "rule": "Open-ended investigation → give the goal and let Claude adapt each step. Predictable work → fixed sequence.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Adaptive investigation plans generate subtasks from what's discovered"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s4-06", "was": "s5q-s4-06", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Explicit subagent context", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 2, "question": "It is late in a long incident session, and the context window is nearly full. The engineer spawns an Explore subagent. Its prompt says: 'Investigate the issue we've been discussing and report the call paths involved.' It returns a broad tour of the serializer code. It never mentions the failing frame, the changed field or the other team's change. Which <b>TWO</b> changes would most improve the next attempt?", "options": [{"t": "Write the failing frame, the changed field and the suspect serializer into its prompt.", "why": "Best. A subagent starts with none of the session's history, so the findings must be in its prompt."}, {"t": "Fork the session and do the mapping in the branch, which already holds the incident history.", "why": "Works, but the branch starts nearly full. It has the history, but little room is left for the mapping work.", "trap": "carries the clutter"}, {"t": "Give the subagent the main session's full tool list, including the Sentry and Linear tools.", "why": "Works, but for a different problem. More tools don't tell the subagent what to look for.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Say what to send back: each path from entry point to serializer, with its files and lines.", "why": "Best. A stated deliverable tells the subagent what done looks like, instead of leaving it to tour."}], "answer": [0, 3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Its prompt says: 'Investigate the issue we've been discussing and report the call paths involved.'", "explanation": "Subagents start with isolated context: pass the relevant findings explicitly and state the deliverable and its quality criteria. Forking carries the history along, which helps only when there is room for it.", "eli5": {"question": "A helper was sent off with 'look into what we discussed', but the helper wasn't in the discussion. The clue: <i>Investigate the issue we've been discussing</i>.", "answer": "Think of sending a courier with a note that says 'fetch the thing we talked about'. The courier wasn't there, so they bring back a bit of everything. Fix the note two ways: say what you already know (the frame, the field, the suspect), and say exactly what to bring back (each path, with files and lines). A <b>subagent</b> starts with an empty memory (<b>isolated context</b>); only its prompt tells it anything. <b>Rule: a subagent knows only its prompt, so give it the findings and the deliverable.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Tell the courier what you already know.", "Cloning yourself works, but your clone arrives with a full desk and no room to work.", "A bigger toolbox doesn't tell the courier what to fetch.", "Correct. Tell the courier exactly what to bring back."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a subagent knows what the session knows</b>. It doesn't. A subagent starts with isolated context: no history, only its prompt. 'The issue we've been discussing' points at nothing. The fix is to put the findings in the prompt and to state the deliverable.", "diagram": "main session (nearly full): frame, field, suspect\n   | prompt: 'the issue we've been discussing'\n   v\nsubagent: empty context -> broad tour", "mapTitle": "What does the subagent need from you?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Subagent needs earlier findings", "Write them into its prompt", "No automatic inheritance"], ["Subagent returns a vague tour", "State the deliverable and when it is complete", "Goal plus quality criteria"], ["Try another approach from a shared baseline, with room to spare", "Fork the session", "The branch keeps the shared history"], ["Main context nearly full", "Delegate with a short, explicit prompt", "Keeps verbose work out of the main session"], ["Subagent lacks a needed capability", "Add that tool to its list", "Tools fix ability, not direction"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the context window is nearly full", "means": "A fork (<b>[[1]]</b>) would start with that same nearly full history."}, {"quote": "Investigate the issue we've been discussing and report the call paths involved", "means": "The decider. The subagent wasn't there, and 'report the call paths' doesn't say what complete looks like."}, {"quote": "It returns a broad tour of the serializer code", "means": "No stated deliverable, so it toured. Name the output: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "It never mentions the failing frame, the changed field or the other team's change", "means": "It lacked the findings, not the tools (<b>[[2]]</b>). Give it the findings: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "A subagent knows only its prompt: pass the findings and state the deliverable.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt (no automatic inheritance / shared memory)"}}}, {"id": "m5p-s5-03", "was": "s5q-s5-03", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 --json-schema", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A security dashboard reads the review job's findings in a fixed shape: severity, file, line and description. The job runs <code>claude -p</code> with <code>--output-format json</code>. A small adapter maps whatever keys come back onto the dashboard's shape. Every few weeks a key comes back renamed, such as <code>location</code> instead of <code>file</code>. Then the adapter breaks. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Pass the dashboard's schema with <code>--json-schema</code> and remove the key-mapping adapter.", "why": "Best. The keys drift because the output shape is not constrained. A schema on the CLI run makes the output match the dashboard's shape."}, {"t": "Check each result against the schema and rerun the review with the errors added on failure.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. Retry with error feedback usually fixes a bad run, but each failure costs a second review and the shape is still unconstrained.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "List the exact field names and severity values with the review criteria in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Naming the fields makes drift rarer, but an instruction is a tendency, not a constraint.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a contract test that fails the build when the keys in the review output change.", "why": "Works, but too late. The test turns a silent break into a loud one, but it detects drift after it happens instead of removing it.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "A small adapter maps whatever keys come back onto the dashboard's shape.", "explanation": "--output-format json gives valid JSON; --json-schema gives your JSON. When a consumer needs a fixed shape, constrain the output at generation instead of repairing it afterwards.", "eli5": {"question": "The JSON is valid, but its key names sometimes change, and the code that reads it breaks. The clue: an adapter maps <i>whatever keys come back</i>. Nothing fixes the shape at the source.", "answer": "Asking someone to “fill in a form” without giving them the form means each one looks a bit different. Hand them the printed form and every answer lands in the same box. <code>--output-format json</code> says “answer in JSON”; <code>--json-schema</code> hands over the form. <b>Rule: a consumer needs a fixed shape → give the CLI the schema.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Hand out the printed form, and stop translating answers afterwards.", "Sending back a messy answer to redo usually works, but you pay twice and the form is still missing.", "Describing the form in a note helps, but people still drift now and then.", "An alarm when a box name changes tells you fast, but doesn't stop it changing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>JSON output is already structured</b>, so the fix belongs in the code that reads it. <code>--output-format json</code> makes the reply valid JSON, but it doesn't fix the <b>keys</b>. <code>--json-schema</code> does, at the source.", "diagram": "claude -p --output-format json   → valid JSON, keys may vary\n  + --json-schema dashboard.json  → valid JSON, your keys", "mapTitle": "CI output breaks the consumer. Where does the fix go?", "map": {"head": ["What breaks", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Reply is prose, not JSON", "No output format", "<code>--output-format json</code>"], ["JSON is valid but keys or enums drift", "No schema", "<code>--json-schema</code>"], ["Shape is right but a value is wrong", "Semantic error", "Validate and retry with the error"], ["Job hangs waiting for input", "Interactive mode", "<code>-p</code> / <code>--print</code>"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A security dashboard reads the review job's findings in a fixed shape", "means": "The consumer has a contract."}, {"quote": "The job runs claude -p with --output-format json", "means": "Valid JSON is already there; the shape is not."}, {"quote": "A small adapter maps whatever keys come back onto the dashboard's shape", "means": "The decider. The shape is repaired afterwards instead of fixed at the source: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Every few weeks a key comes back renamed", "means": "Rare drift: a prompt note (<b>[[2]]</b>) makes it rarer; a retry (<b>[[1]]</b>) or contract test (<b>[[3]]</b>) reacts to it."}], "rule": "Valid JSON is not your JSON. Pin the shape with --json-schema.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "--output-format json + --json-schema for machine-parseable findings → inline PR comments."}}}, {"id": "m5p-s6-04", "was": "s5q-s6-04", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks for normalization", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The agent calls a <code>get_policy</code> tool to confirm dates in each carrier's system. Carriers return dates in different forms: Unix timestamps, ISO strings, or day-first text. The model sometimes reads a day-first date as month-first, and confirms the wrong expiry. Each carrier always uses the same form. Where should the dates be <b>made consistent</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In the system prompt, with a note on each carrier's date form and how to read it.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The notes help the model read the dates, but it still parses them itself, and the slips continue at a lower rate.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "In a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that turns every date into one form before the model reads it.", "why": "Best. The forms are fixed, so code can convert them exactly. A hook on the tool result does it before the model ever sees a day-first date."}, {"t": "In the validator, which rejects confirmed dates that differ from extracted ones and retries.", "why": "Works, but too late. It catches some misreadings after they happen, and spends a retry on each, instead of removing the confusion.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "In a separate wrapper service for each carrier, which converts its dates before returning.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It gives the same result as one hook, with one service to build and run per carrier.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each carrier always uses the same form.", "explanation": "Fixed formats can be converted exactly in code. A PostToolUse hook normalises tool results before the model reads them, so the model never meets the ambiguity.", "eli5": {"question": "Carriers send dates in different styles, and the model sometimes reads them the wrong way round. The clue is: <i>each carrier always uses the same form</i>. Fixed styles can be converted exactly by code.", "answer": "Think of a post room that rewrites every date on incoming letters into one style before the letters reach your desk. You never see the confusing ones. That post room is a <b><code>PostToolUse</code> hook</b>: code that changes a tool's result before the model reads it. <b>Rule: if code can convert it exactly, convert it before the model sees it.</b>", "options": ["A note on how to read each style helps, but the model still does the reading.", "Correct. Code rewrites every date into one style on the way in.", "Rejecting wrong dates afterwards catches some, late, and costs a retry each.", "A service per carrier does the same job as one hook, with far more to run."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a model that misreads formats needs better instructions about them</b>. That fits when formats are open-ended and unpredictable. These are a few fixed forms, one per carrier. Converting them is <b>deterministic work with one right answer</b>, and a hook can do it on every tool result.", "diagram": "get_policy → \"04/03/2027\" (day-first)\n  PostToolUse hook → \"2027-03-04\"\n  model reads one form only", "mapTitle": "Model misreads data from tools: who should fix it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["A few fixed formats in tool results", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook normalises", "Exact, before the model reads"], ["Formats vary unpredictably by layout", "Examples in the prompt", "Code cannot list every case"], ["Wrong value already produced", "Validator plus retry", "Catches after the fact"], ["Action must be blocked above a limit", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook on that tool", "Deterministic control"], ["Value needs outside data", "A tool against a real source", "Correct and traceable"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Carriers return dates in different forms: Unix timestamps, ISO strings, or day-first text", "means": "Heterogeneous formats in tool results: the textbook case for normalising."}, {"quote": "The model sometimes reads a day-first date as month-first, and confirms the wrong expiry", "means": "The model is doing a parsing job. A prompt note (<b>[[0]]</b>) leaves it doing that job."}, {"quote": "Each carrier always uses the same form", "means": "The decider. Fixed forms convert exactly in code. One hook (<b>[[1]]</b>) covers all carriers; a service each (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more machinery; the validator (<b>[[2]]</b>) acts too late."}], "rule": "Normalise fixed formats in a PostToolUse hook, before the model reads them.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "PostToolUse hooks intercept results for transformation before the model sees them."}}}, {"id": "m6p-s1-09", "was": "m6-s1-09", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Validating by category before automating", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Today a person approves every redelivery the agent proposes for a damaged box. Leadership wants the agent to approve redeliveries on its own. They point to last month's audit, where reviewers agreed with the agent in 96% of randomly sampled chats. Only about 20 of the sampled chats were redelivery cases. What is the <b>most appropriate</b> step before making the change?", "options": [{"t": "Add confidence scores for box ID and delivery date, calibrated on labelled chats, and route low ones.", "why": "Works, but too early. Calibrated confidence is a good way to route review once redelivery accuracy is known. It hasn't been measured yet.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Write clear criteria for when a redelivery needs a person, with an example chat for each.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Criteria improve the agent's judgment, but they don't show whether its redelivery decisions are accurate enough.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Sample more redelivery chats until their own agreement rate is measured, then decide.", "why": "Best. The 96% rests on about twenty redelivery chats and says little about them. Measure the category being automated before automating it."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that sends redeliveries above a set value to <code>escalate_to_human</code>.", "why": "Works, but guards only the big ones. A hook enforces a limit well, but it says nothing about the quality of decisions below it.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Only about 20 of the sampled chats were redelivery cases.", "explanation": "Overall accuracy can hide poor performance on one type of case. Measure accuracy for the category being automated, with enough samples, before automating it.", "eli5": {"question": "Leadership wants to let the agent approve redeliveries alone, citing a 96% overall score. The clue: only about <i>20</i> of the audited chats were redeliveries.", "answer": "A student who scores 96% overall might have got most of the few geometry questions wrong. Before you let them teach geometry, you test them on geometry. That is <b>validating accuracy by category</b>: sample enough redelivery chats to measure that type on its own. <b>Rule: before automating a type of case, measure accuracy for that type.</b>", "options": ["Grading how sure the student feels is useful later, once you know their geometry score.", "A clearer marking guide helps the student, but it doesn't tell you how good they are now.", "Correct. Test geometry on its own before trusting them with it.", "Checking only the hardest problems leaves the rest of their geometry untested."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to trust <b>a high overall accuracy</b>. An overall figure can hide poor results on one type of case, and here redeliveries were a small slice of the sample. The exam guide says to <b>validate by type before automating</b>, then use calibrated confidence to route review.", "mapTitle": "Ready to automate a type of case?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Next step", "Why"], "rows": [["Overall score high, this type barely sampled", "Sample this type until its accuracy is measured", "Overall figures hide weak types"], ["This type measured and accurate", "Calibrated confidence routes the doubtful ones", "Review where it is needed"], ["Agent's judgment on edge cases is poor", "Explicit criteria with examples", "Sharpens decisions"], ["A hard money limit must hold", "Hook in code", "Deterministic"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Leadership wants the agent to approve redeliveries on its own", "means": "A decision to automate one type of case."}, {"quote": "reviewers agreed with the agent in 96% of randomly sampled chats", "means": "An overall figure, across all types."}, {"quote": "Only about 20 of the sampled chats were redelivery cases", "means": "The decider. Redelivery accuracy is unknown, so routing by confidence (<b>[[0]]</b>), criteria (<b>[[1]]</b>) and a value hook (<b>[[3]]</b>) all come before it is measured. Measure first: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Before automating a type of case, measure accuracy for that type with enough samples.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Validate accuracy by document type and field before automating"}}}, {"id": "m6p-s2-07", "was": "m6-s2-07", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria for precision", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The <code>/review</code> command asks Claude to “flag anything that could cause problems, but be conservative”. Engineers ignore most of its findings. Over half are style or naming points the linter already enforces. Tenant-scoping findings, by contrast, are nearly always acted on. The team wants engineers to trust that a raised finding is worth reading. Which change <b>helps most</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add three example findings that fix the layout and severity scale, one showing a borderline case.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Examples steady the format and severity, but they don't say that style points shouldn't be raised.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have an independent Claude instance check each finding without the review context, keeping agreed ones.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A second reviewer drops some noise, but it works from the same vague brief and accepts style points too.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a <code>detected_pattern</code> field to findings and track which patterns engineers dismiss this month.", "why": "Works, but delays the fix. Tracking dismissals finds noisy patterns, and this one is already found.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Rewrite the command to name the issue classes to raise and those to skip, with a code sample for each.", "why": "Best. Explicit categories with a code sample each replace “anything” and “be conservative”, and cut the noisy class at the source."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Over half are style or naming points the linter already enforces.", "explanation": "Vague instructions and “be conservative” don't improve precision. Name the categories to report and to skip, with concrete code examples, to keep trust in real findings.", "eli5": {"question": "A review command flags lots of things, and people ignore it. The clue: over half the noise is <i>one known kind</i> (style points the linter already checks).", "answer": "A smoke alarm that also beeps at toast gets its battery pulled, and then nobody hears the real fire. You fix it by telling it exactly what to react to: smoke, not toast. For Claude, that is <b>explicit criteria</b>: which issue classes to raise, which to skip, with a small code sample for each. “Be conservative” is like saying “beep less”. <b>Rule: you can name the noise → name it in the criteria.</b>", "options": ["Showing what a good beep sounds like doesn't stop it beeping at toast.", "A second alarm with the same settings beeps at the same toast.", "Logging every false alarm finds the cause, but you already know it is toast.", "Correct. Tell the alarm exactly what counts and what to ignore."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>noisy output needs better examples, a second check or more data</b>. Each of those has a place. Here the noise has a name: style and naming points that the linter covers. The exam guide says “be conservative” doesn't improve precision; <b>explicit categories with code examples</b> do.", "mapTitle": "A reviewer raises too much noise. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["One known class makes most of the noise", "Vague “flag anything” brief", "Name classes to raise and skip"], ["Format or severity varies run to run", "No shared model of output", "Few-shot example findings"], ["Noise source unknown", "Not yet measured", "Track <code>detected_pattern</code> dismissals"], ["Subtle misses in generated code", "Self-review bias", "Independent review instance"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "flag anything that could cause problems, but be conservative", "means": "The cause: a vague brief, with a confidence word that doesn't sharpen it."}, {"quote": "Over half are style or naming points the linter already enforces", "means": "The decider. The noise is a named class, already found, so tracking (<b>[[2]]</b>) isn't needed. Skip it explicitly."}, {"quote": "Tenant-scoping findings, by contrast, are nearly always acted on", "means": "Precision is the issue, not format (<b>[[0]]</b>). A second checker with the same brief (<b>[[1]]</b>) keeps the same line."}, {"quote": "Which change helps most?", "means": "Name what to raise and what to skip: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Noise from a nameable class → explicit raise/skip categories with code examples, not “be conservative”.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "High false-positive categories erode developer trust in accurate ones; define explicit severity criteria with concrete code examples"}}}, {"id": "m6p-s3-05", "was": "m6-s3-05", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Resume vs fresh session with summary", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "A research session on a heart-failure drug was paused on Friday. By then the analysis subagent had read dozens of papers. Over the weekend, the journal replaced most of those papers with corrected versions. Several tables the coordinator had quoted have changed. The session history still holds the original tool results. On Monday the team wants to continue with two new questions. What is the <b>most reliable</b> way to continue?", "options": [{"t": "Resume with <code>--resume hf-drug</code>, tell the agent which papers were corrected, then add the two new questions.", "why": "Works, but stale history. Resuming and naming the changes is right when a few files changed. Here most results are out of date, and the old tables still fill the history.", "trap": "stale history"}, {"t": "Resume with <code>--resume hf-drug</code>, run <code>/compact</code> to shrink the history, then re-read the corrected papers.", "why": "Works, but keeps stale facts. Compacting shrinks the history, but it summarises the old tables instead of removing them.", "trap": "keeps stale facts"}, {"t": "Open a new session from a structured summary of what still holds, and re-read the corrected papers.", "why": "Best. When much of the earlier tool output is stale, a new session with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming."}, {"t": "Use <code>fork_session</code> to branch Friday's session: one branch for old questions, one for new.", "why": "Works, but copies the problem. Forking suits trying different approaches from a good baseline. Both branches inherit the stale results.", "trap": "copies the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Over the weekend, the journal replaced most of those papers with corrected versions.", "explanation": "Resume when earlier context is still mostly valid and tell the agent what changed. Start fresh with a structured summary when much of the history is stale.", "eli5": {"question": "Work paused on Friday, and over the weekend most of the papers it read were corrected. The clue: <i>most</i> of the papers changed, and the history still holds the old versions.", "answer": "If one page of your notes is wrong, you fix that page. If most of the notebook copied from an old edition, you start a clean notebook with just what still holds, and re-read the new edition. That is a <b>new session with a structured summary</b>. Resuming keeps the old tables in Claude's history, and it may keep reasoning from them. <b>Rule: a little changed → resume and say what changed. Much is stale → start fresh from a summary.</b>", "options": ["Saying \"page 4 changed\" works for a few pages. Here most of the notebook is out of date.", "Shrinking an old notebook keeps a short version of the old facts.", "Correct. A clean notebook with only what still holds, plus a list of papers to re-read.", "Photocopying the old notebook twice gives you two notebooks with the same old facts."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>resuming is always best because it keeps the work</b>. It keeps the stale work too. Tool results in the history are what Claude reasons from. When most of them are out of date, a new session from a <b>structured summary</b> of what still holds is more reliable.", "mapTitle": "Continue an old session, or start fresh?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Little changed since the pause", "<code>--resume</code> and say what changed", "History mostly valid"], ["Much of the tool output is stale", "New session from a structured summary", "Old results would mislead"], ["Try two approaches from a good baseline", "<code>fork_session</code>", "Independent branches"], ["Session healthy but nearly full", "<code>/compact</code>", "Frees space, keeps the work"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "By then the analysis subagent had read dozens of papers", "means": "The history is full of tool results from those papers."}, {"quote": "Over the weekend, the journal replaced most of those papers with corrected versions", "means": "The decider. <i>Most</i>, not a few, so resuming and naming changes (<b>[[0]]</b>) leaves mostly stale history."}, {"quote": "The session history still holds the original tool results", "means": "Compacting (<b>[[1]]</b>) summarises them and forking (<b>[[3]]</b>) copies them."}, {"quote": "What is the most reliable way to continue?", "means": "Start fresh from what still holds: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Most tool results stale → new session from a structured summary, not --resume.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "Starting a new session with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming with stale tool results"}}}, {"id": "m6p-s3-06", "was": "m6-s3-06", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 PostToolUse normalization", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Three vendor-run MCP servers feed the analysis subagent. Each reports publication dates in its own format: Unix timestamps, quarter strings like 'Q3 2024', or day-first dates. The synthesis subagent sometimes puts trials in the wrong order. The vendors run these servers, and the team cannot change their responses. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that rewrites those dates to one format before Claude reads the results.", "why": "Best. A PostToolUse hook rewrites tool results in code before the model sees them, on the side the team controls."}, {"t": "Run a small MCP proxy server of the team's own in front of the vendors that converts every date.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A proxy fixes the dates, but it is a new server to run and maintain. A hook does the same inside the agent.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add few-shot examples to the analysis prompt that convert each vendor's date format to ISO 8601.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples improve consistency, but the conversion stays probabilistic, and day-first dates are easy to misread.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Show each source's publication date beside every claim drawn from it in the summary the committee gets.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Readers can spot an odd order, but the dates are still misread upstream and the order is still wrong.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The vendors run these servers, and the team cannot change their responses.", "explanation": "PostToolUse hooks intercept tool results and normalise them before the model sees them, giving deterministic behaviour where prompts give only probabilistic compliance.", "eli5": {"question": "Three outside data feeds write dates three different ways, and trials end up in the wrong order. The clue: the team <i>can't change</i> the feeds.", "answer": "If three suppliers send invoices with dates written differently, you put one clerk at the door who rewrites every date the same way before anyone files them. That clerk is a <b>PostToolUse hook</b>: code that runs on each tool result before Claude reads it. It works every time, not most times. <b>Rule: messy formats you can't fix at the source → normalise them in a hook.</b>", "options": ["Correct. One clerk at the door rewrites every date before anyone reads it.", "Opening a separate office to rewrite the dates works, but it's a whole office to run.", "Showing staff examples helps most of the time, but some dates still get misread.", "Writing the date on the final report doesn't fix the order things were filed in."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the fix belongs in the prompt or at the source</b>. The source is not the team's to change, and a prompt only usually converts correctly. A <b>PostToolUse hook</b> sits in the team's own agent and rewrites results in code before the model reasons over them.", "diagram": "vendor MCP → raw date → PostToolUse hook → ISO date → Claude", "mapTitle": "Tool results arrive in mixed formats. Where do you fix them?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["You run the server", "Fix the format at the source", "Cleanest, one place"], ["Someone else runs the server", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook in your agent", "Deterministic, on your side"], ["Need a shared fix for many agents and teams", "Your own proxy server", "Worth running only at that scale"], ["Wording or style varies, not data", "Few-shot examples", "Usually right is enough"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each reports publication dates in its own format", "means": "Mixed formats reach the model as they are."}, {"quote": "The synthesis subagent sometimes puts trials in the wrong order", "means": "The misread dates cause real errors. Showing dates to readers (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the order wrong."}, {"quote": "The vendors run these servers, and the team cannot change their responses", "means": "The decider. Fix on the team's side. A hook does it in code (<b>[[0]]</b>); examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) only usually; a proxy (<b>[[1]]</b>) adds a server."}], "rule": "Normalise heterogeneous tool results in a PostToolUse hook before the model sees them.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "PostToolUse hooks intercept results for transformation before the model sees them"}}}, {"id": "m6p-s3-09", "was": "m6-s3-09", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Presenting conflicting sources", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Two recent sources disagree on the rate of major bleeding with a new drug. A large trial gives one figure, and a national registry report gives a higher one. The synthesis subagent kept only the trial figure. The committee needs to see both figures to judge them. Summaries go straight from the report subagent to the committee. Which change to the synthesis output <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a section for conflicting results that lists each estimate with its source, date and method.", "why": "Best. Conflicting statistics are kept with source attribution in a clear section, not settled by picking one."}, {"t": "Keep the figure from the stronger study design, and mention the other source's figure in a footnote.", "why": "Works, but still picks one. The other figure survives, but the summary has already chosen, and a footnote is easy to miss.", "trap": "still picks one"}, {"t": "Show every source's publication and data-collection dates next to the claims drawn from it.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Dates stop time gaps being read as conflicts. These sources are both recent and still disagree.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Present numeric findings as tables with a source column, and keep narrative findings as prose.", "why": "Works, but only the format. Tables suit numbers, but a table can still hold only the one figure synthesis chose.", "trap": "only the format"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The committee needs to see both figures to judge them.", "explanation": "When credible sources conflict, keep both values with attribution in a structured section rather than picking one.", "eli5": {"question": "Two good sources give different bleeding rates, and the summary kept only one. The clue: the committee <i>needs to see both figures</i> to judge them.", "answer": "If two thermometers give different readings, you don't throw one away. You write down both, which thermometer, when, and how it was used, and let the doctor decide. In a summary, that is a <b>conflicts section with source attribution</b>. <b>Rule: credible sources disagree → show every figure with its source. Don't pick one.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Both readings, each labelled with where, when and how it was taken.", "Keeping the \"better\" thermometer's reading up front still makes the choice for the doctor.", "Writing the time on each reading helps when the readings were taken at different times. Here both are recent.", "A neat table still only shows the one reading someone chose to keep."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a good synthesis resolves disagreements</b> by choosing the stronger source. When credible sources conflict and the reader must judge, picking one hides the disagreement. The exam guide says to <b>annotate conflicting statistics with source attribution</b>, not to pick one.", "mapTitle": "Sources differ. What should the synthesis do?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best output", "Why"], "rows": [["Credible sources give different figures", "Conflicts section, each figure with source and method", "Reader judges; nothing hidden"], ["Figures differ because of different dates", "Publication and collection dates beside claims", "Time gap, not a conflict"], ["Numbers mixed into long prose", "Tables for numbers, prose for narrative", "Right format per content"], ["Claims lose their sources in summaries", "Claim-source mappings kept through synthesis", "Every claim stays cited"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Two recent sources disagree on the rate of major bleeding with a new drug", "means": "Both are recent, so dates (<b>[[2]]</b>) don't explain the gap."}, {"quote": "The synthesis subagent kept only the trial figure", "means": "The fault: it picked one. A footnote approach (<b>[[1]]</b>) still picks one."}, {"quote": "The committee needs to see both figures to judge them", "means": "The decider. Both figures, with source and method: <b>[[0]]</b>. Table format (<b>[[3]]</b>) doesn't bring the lost figure back."}, {"quote": "Summaries go straight from the report subagent to the committee", "means": "Nobody adds the second figure later, so the synthesis output must carry it."}], "rule": "Conflicting credible figures → keep each one with its source in a conflicts section; don't pick one.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "annotate conflicting statistics with source attribution (don't arbitrarily pick one)"}}}, {"id": "m6p-s4-05", "was": "m6-s4-05", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Forking from a shared baseline", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer spent an hour with Claude mapping how the lesson-sync code uses a blocking Redis client. They now want to try two migrations: one to an async client and one to a connection pool. Each should go far enough to show test failures, and the better one is kept. No files have changed since the mapping, and the context is under half full. How should they <b>go on</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Ask Claude for a structured summary of the mapping, then seed two new sessions with it, one per migration.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. A fresh session with a summary is right when tool results are stale or context is nearly full. Neither is true, and a summary drops detail.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Branch the session twice from the end of the mapping with <code>fork_session</code>, one migration per branch.", "why": "Best. Forking makes independent branches from a shared, still-valid baseline, which is what comparing two approaches needs."}, {"t": "Spawn two subagents with the Task tool, and give each the mapping findings and one migration to build.", "why": "Works, but the edits clash. Both subagents change the same working tree, and a prompt carries less than the full mapping session.", "trap": "clashing edits"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode and have Claude design both migrations side by side before picking one to build.", "why": "Works, but skips the trial. Plan mode compares designs on paper, while the engineer wants each migration far enough to see real test failures.", "trap": "skips the trial"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "No files have changed since the mapping, and the context is under half full.", "explanation": "fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline. When that baseline is still valid, forking keeps all of it for each approach.", "eli5": {"question": "After an hour of good groundwork, someone wants to try two different paths from the same point. The clues: <i>nothing has changed</i> and the session still has room, so the groundwork is still good.", "answer": "Picture a save point in a game. You want to try the left door and the right door, both from the same save. You don't write notes and start a new game. You load the save twice (<b>fork_session</b>). <b>Rule: two approaches from one still-valid baseline → fork. Baseline stale or context full → new session with a summary.</b>", "options": ["Writing notes and starting new games: right when the save is broken. Here it isn't, and notes miss detail.", "Correct. Load the same save twice and try each door.", "Two helpers in the same room, both rearranging the same furniture at once.", "Planning both routes on a map. The engineer wants to actually walk them and see what breaks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a fresh start with a summary is the clean way</b> to begin new work. That is right when the old session is stale or full. Here it is neither, so the session itself is the best baseline, and forking copies all of it into two independent branches.", "diagram": "mapping session (1 hour, still valid)\n   |-- fork A: async client   -> run tests\n   |-- fork B: connection pool -> run tests\nkeep the better branch", "mapTitle": "Resume, fork or start fresh?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Two approaches from one valid baseline", "<code>fork_session</code>", "Independent branches, full context"], ["Baseline stale or context nearly full", "New session + structured summary", "Old results are unreliable"], ["Continue one named past session", "<code>--resume &lt;name&gt;</code>", "Same conversation, same work"], ["Compare designs before writing code", "Plan mode", "Explore safely before changes"], ["Hand a focused subtask to another agent", "Task subagent with findings in its prompt", "No automatic context sharing"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "They now want to try two migrations", "means": "Two divergent approaches from one starting point."}, {"quote": "Each should go far enough to show test failures", "means": "They must be built, not only designed, so plan mode (<b>[[3]]</b>) stops short."}, {"quote": "No files have changed since the mapping, and the context is under half full", "means": "The decider. The baseline is still good, so a summary (<b>[[0]]</b>) only loses detail. Fork it: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Two paths from a still-valid session → fork_session. Stale or full → new session with a summary.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline"}}}, {"id": "m6p-s4-06", "was": "m6-s4-06", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Per-item passes plus integration", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Before a reliability review, a staff engineer asks Claude how each of the app's 25 API clients retries failed calls. The reviewers set the questions in advance: retry count, backoff, timeout and idempotency key. Claude did it all in one long session. Early rows were detailed, and later rows were thin. It also contradicted itself about a shared retry module. What is the <b>best way</b> to restructure the work?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude draft an investigation plan from the first clients and add subtasks as new patterns turn up.", "why": "Works, but fits unknown work. Adaptive plans suit work whose shape comes from discoveries. Here the clients and the questions were set in advance.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Hand the job to an Explore subagent that reads every client in its own context and returns one table.", "why": "Works, but moves the problem. The main session stays clean, but one long pass inside the subagent still thins out.", "trap": "moves the problem"}, {"t": "Have Claude note each client's findings in a scratchpad file and check it before writing later rows.", "why": "Works, but only half the problem. The file keeps findings across context limits, but it is still one long pass with uneven attention.", "trap": "only half the problem"}, {"t": "Give each client its own focused pass, then run one comparison pass that settles the shared module question.", "why": "Best. A focused pass per client gives even depth, and a separate integration pass reconciles what they say about the shared module."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Early rows were detailed, and later rows were thin.", "explanation": "Prompt chaining: analyze each item on its own, then run a cross-item integration pass. This avoids attention dilution and resolves contradictions.", "eli5": {"question": "One long job got worse as it went on and contradicted itself. The clue: <i>early rows detailed, later rows thin</i>. Attention ran out over one long pass.", "answer": "A teacher marking 25 essays in one sitting gives the first ones care and the last ones a glance. Better: mark each essay on its own, fresh (<b>a focused pass per item</b>). Then read all the marks side by side to settle disagreements (<b>an integration pass</b>). <b>Rule: many items, same questions → one pass per item, then one pass across all of them.</b>", "options": ["Making up the plan as you go suits a job you don't understand yet. Here the questions were fixed in advance.", "Another teacher marking all 25 in one sitting gets just as tired.", "Notes help you remember, but you are still marking all 25 in one tired sitting.", "Correct. Each essay fresh, then one side-by-side check."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the problem is memory</b>, so a notes file or a fresh context fixes it. The pattern here is attention dilution: one pass over many items gives later items less care, and nothing compares them at the end. Splitting into one pass per item plus a cross-item pass fixes both symptoms.", "mapTitle": "How should a big analysis be split?", "map": {"head": ["Shape of the work", "Strategy", "Why"], "rows": [["Known items, fixed questions", "Per-item passes + one integration pass", "Even depth, contradictions settled"], ["Shape unknown, comes from findings", "Adaptive investigation plan", "Subtasks follow discoveries"], ["Findings must survive context limits", "Scratchpad file", "Persists key facts"], ["Verbose discovery crowds the main session", "Explore subagent", "Isolates output"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The reviewers set the questions in advance", "means": "The work is known up front, so an adaptive plan (<b>[[0]]</b>) solves a problem they don't have."}, {"quote": "Claude did it all in one long session", "means": "One pass over every client is the cause."}, {"quote": "Early rows were detailed, and later rows were thin", "means": "The decider. Attention dilution, which a scratchpad (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a subagent (<b>[[1]]</b>) doing the same long pass keeps."}, {"quote": "It also contradicted itself about a shared retry module", "means": "A cross-client pass is needed to reconcile it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Many items with fixed questions → one focused pass per item, then one integration pass.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Prompt chaining: analyze each file individually, then a cross-file integration pass"}}}, {"id": "m6p-s5-07", "was": "m6-s5-07", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for actionable output", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The review job returns findings through <code>--json-schema</code>. Each finding has file, line, severity, issue and <code>suggested_fix</code>. Parsing has not failed in a month. The trouble is what <code>suggested_fix</code> says. Some entries show replacement code, but many say things like “consider refactoring”. The prompt already describes the wanted fix in a paragraph. What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Make <code>suggested_fix</code> an object with required <code>replacement_code</code> and <code>explanation</code> strings.", "why": "Works, but shape, not content. A required string can still say “consider refactoring”; the schema already parses.", "trap": "shape, not content"}, {"t": "Add three complete sample findings to the prompt, each with a code fix, one spanning several lines.", "why": "Best. When written instructions give uneven output, a few targeted examples are the most effective way to get consistent, usable content."}, {"t": "Check each fix for a code block before posting, and re-prompt with that finding when one is missing.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. A retry catches vague fixes after the fact and pays a second call, instead of fixing the first answer.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Send each finding to a second Claude instance that rewrites its fix as replacement code.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It doubles calls on every run to repair output the first pass can be shown how to write.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The prompt already describes the wanted fix in a paragraph.", "explanation": "Schemas govern shape, not whether a field's content is useful. Few-shot examples are the strongest first lever for consistent, actionable content once instructions exist.", "eli5": {"question": "The findings are well-formed, but the suggested fixes are often vague. The clue: <i>a paragraph already describes what's wanted</i>, and it isn't working.", "answer": "Think of telling a new cook “make it look nice”. They try, but results vary. Show them three finished plates and they copy the standard. Examples in the prompt (<b>few-shot</b>) do that for Claude. <b>Rule: when instructions alone give uneven output, add 2–4 examples.</b>", "options": ["A stricter plate shape still lets the cook serve a vague dish.", "Correct. Show three finished plates.", "Sending back vague dishes works, but costs a second cook each time.", "A second cook re-plates every dish: double the work."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a schema can force good content</b>. A schema controls shape: fields, types, required. It cannot tell a useful fix from “consider refactoring”. When a written description already exists and output is still uneven, <b>examples</b> are the next lever.", "mapTitle": "Output is wrong. Which lever?", "map": {"head": ["What is wrong", "Lever", "Why"], "rows": [["Fields missing or JSON broken", "Schema / <code>--json-schema</code>", "Fixes shape"], ["Shape fine, content vague, instructions exist", "Few-shot examples", "Shows the standard"], ["Specific check fails after output", "Validate and retry with the error", "Safety net"], ["Judgement errors on hard cases", "Independent review", "Fresh eye"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Parsing has not failed in a month", "means": "Shape is fine, so a tighter schema (<b>[[0]]</b>) targets the wrong thing."}, {"quote": "Some entries show replacement code, but many say things like “consider refactoring”", "means": "The content is uneven."}, {"quote": "The prompt already describes the wanted fix in a paragraph", "means": "The decider. Instructions alone aren't enough. Examples: <b>[[1]]</b>. Retries (<b>[[2]]</b>) and rewriters (<b>[[3]]</b>) come later, if at all."}], "rule": "Instructions exist but output is uneven → add a few targeted examples before adding checks.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Few-shot is the most effective technique for consistent, actionable output when instructions alone are inconsistent."}}}, {"id": "m6p-s6-06", "was": "m6-s6-06", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 tool_choice any vs forced", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "Each document goes alone to a request with three tools: <code>extract_certificate</code>, <code>extract_endorsement</code> and <code>extract_schedule</code>. Uploads carry no type label. With <code>tool_choice</code> on auto, some replies are a prose summary with no tool call, and the parser drops them. When the model does call a tool, it picks the right one. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Expand each extractor's description with the headings and features of its document type.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Richer descriptions help the model pick a tool, but it already picks well; the gap is replies with no tool at all.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add a first call that labels the document type, then force the matching extractor by name.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Classify-then-force is right when the model picks the wrong tool. Here it picks well, so the extra call adds cost for nothing.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Keep auto, and when <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>end_turn</code>, resend saying a tool call is required.", "why": "Works, but patches after. Detecting and resending recovers the dropped replies, but each costs a second call when the API can require a tool call on the first.", "trap": "patches after"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> on every extraction call, with the schemas unchanged.", "why": "Best. <code>any</code> requires a tool call and leaves the choice of tool to the model, which already chooses well."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "When the model does call a tool, it picks the right one.", "explanation": "<code>auto</code> may return text, <code>any</code> requires some tool call, and a forced tool requires one named tool. Use <code>any</code> when a call is needed but the right tool is not known in advance.", "eli5": {"question": "Some replies come back as plain text instead of a tool call. The clue is: <i>when the model does call a tool, it picks the right one</i>. Choosing is fine; the only problem is not calling at all.", "answer": "Think of a post room with three trays. The clerk always picks the right tray, but sometimes writes a note instead of filing the letter. You don't need to tell the clerk which tray; you just say \"every letter goes in a tray\". That is <code>tool_choice: any</code>: <b>a tool call is required, and the model still picks which</b>. Forcing one named tool is for when you already know which tray. <b>Rule: must call a tool, model picks well → any.</b>", "options": ["Better labels on the trays help picking, but picking is already right.", "Close. Labelling first and forcing a tool fits when the model picks the wrong tool. It doesn't.", "Catching text replies and asking again works, but every miss costs an extra call.", "Correct. Every reply is a tool call, and the model keeps choosing the right one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a missing tool call means the model is confused about which tool</b>. But when it calls one, it chooses correctly. The only failure is <b>no call at all</b>, which <code>auto</code> allows. The API setting <code>any</code> removes that option on the first try.", "mapTitle": "What should tool_choice be?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Setting", "Why"], "rows": [["Text replies are acceptable", "<code>auto</code>", "Model may answer without a tool"], ["A tool call is required, model picks well", "<code>any</code>", "Must call some tool"], ["A tool call is required, model picks the wrong one", "Classify, then force the named tool", "You decide the tool"], ["One step must run first, such as metadata", "Forced tool on that call", "Fixed order"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Uploads carry no type label", "means": "The code can't know the right tool in advance, so forcing a named one needs an extra classify call (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "With tool_choice on auto, some replies are a prose summary with no tool call, and the parser drops them", "means": "The failure is text instead of a call. Resending after <code>end_turn</code> (<b>[[2]]</b>) repairs it a call late."}, {"quote": "When the model does call a tool, it picks the right one", "means": "The decider. Selection is fine, so better descriptions (<b>[[0]]</b>) aim at the wrong thing. Require a call: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Model picks well but sometimes replies in text → tool_choice any.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "tool_choice: \"auto\" (may return text), \"any\" (must call a tool)"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-01", "was": "m7-s1-01", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Agentic loop and stop_reason", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The agent's loop ends the turn whenever Claude's response contains a text block. Claude often writes a short line, such as “Let me check that box,” in the same response where it asks to call <code>lookup_order</code>. In those chats the lookup never runs. The customer waits for an answer that never comes. Which change to the loop <b>fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Tell Claude in the system prompt not to write any text in a response that calls a tool.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Claude would write fewer of those lines, but the loop still ends on text, so any line that slips through stops the lookup again.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> on the first request after each customer message.", "why": "Works, but wrong lever. A forced tool call carries no text, so this case stops, but Claude must now call a tool even when it should just answer.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Branch on <code>stop_reason</code>: run the tools and loop on <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>.", "why": "Best. A response can hold text and a tool request together. stop_reason says what comes next: tool_use means run the tools and continue, end_turn means the turn is over."}, {"t": "When a turn ends without an answer, send Claude a short “please continue” message.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. The nudge restarts the stalled chat, but the loop still ends early, and every stall costs an extra request.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The agent's loop ends the turn whenever Claude's response contains a text block.", "explanation": "The loop should be driven by stop_reason: continue on tool_use, stop on end_turn. Checking for text in the response is an anti-pattern, because text and tool calls can arrive together.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude says “Let me check that box” and asks for a lookup in the same breath. The loop hears the words and hangs up before the lookup runs. The clue: the loop decides by looking for text.", "answer": "Imagine a waiter who walks away as soon as you start talking, even when you were in the middle of ordering. The fix is to wait for a clear signal: “that's all”. Each Claude response carries that signal, called <b>stop_reason</b>. <b>Rule: <code>tool_use</code> → run the tool and keep going. <code>end_turn</code> → stop.</b>", "options": ["Asking Claude to stay quiet helps, but the waiter still walks off at the first word.", "Forcing a tool call stops the chatter, but now Claude must call a tool even when it should just reply.", "Correct. Listen for the real “that's all” signal.", "Calling the waiter back each time works, but he keeps walking off."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude talks too much</b> and should be told to be quiet. But the text is harmless. The bug is in the loop: it treats any text as the end of the turn. The API already says whether the turn is over, in <code>stop_reason</code>.", "diagram": "response = text + tool_use\n  stop_reason = tool_use → run tool, send result, loop\n  stop_reason = end_turn → turn over, reply to customer", "mapTitle": "What should end an agent's turn?", "map": {"head": ["Signal", "Use it to stop?", "Why"], "rows": [["<code>stop_reason == \"end_turn\"</code>", "Yes", "The API's own signal that Claude is done"], ["<code>stop_reason == \"tool_use\"</code>", "No: run tools and continue", "Claude is waiting for results"], ["Response contains text", "No", "Text and tool calls can come together"], ["Fixed number of rounds", "Only as a safety cap", "It cuts off work that is not finished"], ["Phrases like “done” in the reply", "No", "Parsing words is fragile"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The agent's loop ends the turn whenever Claude's response contains a text block", "means": "The decider. The stop rule looks at content, not at the signal. A prompt rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a nudge (<b>[[3]]</b>) leave that rule in place."}, {"quote": "Claude often writes a short line, such as “Let me check that box,” in the same response where it asks to call lookup_order", "means": "Text and a tool request arrive together. That is normal, so the loop must handle it."}, {"quote": "In those chats the lookup never runs", "means": "Claude did ask for the tool. Forcing tool calls (<b>[[1]]</b>) answers a different problem: Claude not asking."}, {"quote": "Which change to the loop fixes this?", "means": "Change the stop rule to stop_reason: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Loop on stop_reason: tool_use → run tools and continue; end_turn → stop. Never stop because text appeared.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops", "quote": "Continue the loop when stop_reason == \"tool_use\"; terminate when \"end_turn\"."}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-08", "was": "m7-s1-08", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Explicit request for a human", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A customer writes: “My salmon was missing again. I want to speak to a real person.” The refund is well inside the agent's limit, so the agent could settle the case alone. The customer sounds calm, and the request for a person is clear. How should the agent <b>respond</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Say sorry, offer the refund it can issue now, and escalate if the customer asks for a person again.", "why": "Works, but wrong action. Many customers would take the refund, but this one asked for a person and now has to ask twice.", "trap": "wrong action"}, {"t": "Run <code>lookup_order</code> to confirm the missing salmon, then escalate with what it found.", "why": "Works, but delays the request. The check adds a detail to the handoff, but the customer waits for a decision that is already made.", "trap": "delays the request"}, {"t": "Call <code>escalate_to_human</code> at once, with a note that the customer asked for a person.", "why": "Works, but thin handoff. The customer reaches a person quickly, but the person must ask again which box and item it was about.", "trap": "thin handoff"}, {"t": "Call <code>escalate_to_human</code> at once, passing the customer ID, the box and the missing item.", "why": "Best. An explicit request for a person is a reason to escalate on its own. Passing the facts lets the person pick up without asking again."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "I want to speak to a real person.", "explanation": "When a customer explicitly asks for a human, escalate immediately, even if the agent could resolve the case. Hand over the key facts in a structured way so the human does not need to re-ask.", "eli5": {"question": "A customer says “I want a real person.” The agent could fix the problem itself. The clue: the customer asked directly.", "answer": "If you ask a shop assistant for the manager, you don't want to hear “but I can help you!” first. A good assistant fetches the manager and says “this customer's salmon was missing from box 3.” That's <b>escalate at once, with a handoff that has the facts</b>. <b>Rule: explicit request for a human → escalate now, and pass the case details.</b>", "options": ["Offering a fix first is kind, but the customer already said what they want.", "Checking the order first makes them wait for something they didn't ask for.", "Fetching the manager is right, but the manager has to ask everything again.", "Correct. Fetch the manager now and tell them what happened."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent should resolve what it can</b>, because the refund is within its limit. That is right when a customer is unhappy but hasn't asked for a person. An explicit request for a human is its own trigger: honour it straight away.", "diagram": "\"I want a real person\" → escalate now\n  handoff: customer ID, box, missing item\n  person picks up without asking again", "mapTitle": "When should the agent hand over to a person?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Agent's move", "Why"], "rows": [["Customer explicitly asks for a person", "Escalate at once, with the facts", "The request is the trigger"], ["Frustrated, simple case, no request for a person", "Offer the fix it can make", "Resolve when it's straightforward"], ["Refund above the agent's limit", "Escalate", "Policy limit"], ["Policy doesn't cover the case", "Escalate", "A gap needs a human call"], ["Request is unclear", "Ask a clarifying question", "Don't guess"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "I want to speak to a real person", "means": "The decider: an explicit request. Offering a refund first (<b>[[0]]</b>) makes the customer ask twice."}, {"quote": "The refund is well inside the agent's limit, so the agent could settle the case alone", "means": "Being able to resolve it is not a reason to override the request, or to stop for a lookup (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "How should the agent respond?", "means": "Escalate now, with the facts so the person doesn't re-ask (unlike <b>[[2]]</b>): <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Customer asks for a human? Escalate immediately and pass the case facts in the handoff.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Escalate immediately on explicit demand; offer resolution when straightforward."}}}, {"id": "m7p-s2-09", "was": "m7-s2-09", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 /compact for context pressure", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer is midway through generating a set of screens in one long session. The context is nearly full. Most of it is long test output from earlier runs. Claude's recent answers are still accurate and refer to the right files. Several screens are still left to build. What should the engineer <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude write its decisions to a notes file, then start a new session that reads it first.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt for now. A notes file and a fresh session are the fix when answers have turned vague or work spans sessions. This session is still healthy.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Route future test runs through a subagent that returns only the failing tests and their causes.", "why": "Works, but for later. It keeps future test output small, but it doesn't free the space that is already full.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> to condense the conversation so far, then carry on with the screens.", "why": "Best. The session is accurate; only space is short. Compacting frees context and keeps the work in the same session."}, {"t": "Run <code>/clear</code>, then give Claude a short list of the screens still to build and continue.", "why": "Works, but throws away good work. Space is freed, but the decisions and file details Claude is using correctly are gone.", "trap": "loses the work"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude's recent answers are still accurate and refer to the right files.", "explanation": "/compact reduces context use within a healthy session. Scratchpad files and fresh sessions are for degraded sessions or work across sessions; subagents prevent future bloat.", "eli5": {"question": "The session is almost out of space, mostly from old test output. Claude is still giving good, specific answers. The clue: the session is <i>healthy</i>; it is only <i>full</i>.", "answer": "Your desk is covered in old printouts, but you are working well. You don't move to a new desk; you clear the clutter into a short pile of notes and keep going. That is <code>/compact</code>: it condenses the conversation so far and keeps the session. <b>Rule: healthy but full → /compact. Confused and drifting → notes file and a fresh session.</b>", "options": ["Moving to a new desk with a notes file is the fix when you've lost the thread. You haven't.", "A rule to print less from now on is good, but the desk is already covered.", "Correct. Tidy the desk and keep working.", "Sweeping the desk clean frees space, but throws away work you still need."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full context means starting again</b>. A fresh session with a notes file is the fix for <i>degradation</i>: vague answers, “typical patterns” instead of specific files. Here Claude's answers are still specific, so only space is short. <code>/compact</code> reduces context use inside the same session.", "mapTitle": "Context trouble: what kind, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Nearly full, answers still specific", "Space only", "<code>/compact</code>"], ["Answers vague or inconsistent", "Context degradation", "Notes file, then a fresh session"], ["Verbose output will keep piling up", "Discovery in the main context", "Subagent returns summaries"], ["Work must survive a crash or a new day", "No saved state", "Scratchpad or manifest file"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Most of it is long test output from earlier runs", "means": "Old output already fills the space. Routing future runs (<b>[[1]]</b>) doesn't remove it."}, {"quote": "Claude's recent answers are still accurate and refer to the right files", "means": "The decider. No degradation, so a fresh session (<b>[[0]]</b>) or a full clear (<b>[[3]]</b>) gives up working context for nothing."}, {"quote": "What should the engineer do next?", "means": "Free space, keep the session: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Session still accurate but full → /compact. Session degraded → scratchpad notes and a fresh session.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "/compact to reduce context usage"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s3-02", "was": "m7-s3-02", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Per-request context in the Task prompt", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Each committee sets its own inclusion rules when it sends a request, such as a minimum trial size or drug classes to leave out. The coordinator agrees these rules with the committee before it delegates. Later, the analysis subagent includes studies the committee excluded. Its Task prompt names only the drug and the outcome. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "List each committee's usual rules in the analysis subagent's system prompt, by committee.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. A fixed system prompt can hold rules that rarely change, but these are agreed per request, so the list goes stale and misses one-off rules.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Add a check step after analysis that removes studies the committee's rules exclude.", "why": "Works, but too late. A filter afterwards can drop the wrong studies, but the analysis has already been done on them, and the filter still needs the same rules passed to it.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Save the agreed rules to a file for each request and tell the subagent to read it.", "why": "Works, but more moving parts. The subagent can read the file, but its Task prompt must still name the file, so you may as well put the rules in the prompt.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Write the agreed rules into each analysis Task prompt, beside the drug and the outcome.", "why": "Best. Subagents do not see the coordinator's conversation. Anything agreed for one request must be written into that request's Task prompt."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Its Task prompt names only the drug and the outcome.", "explanation": "Subagents have isolated context and do not inherit what the coordinator discussed. Per-request details must be passed explicitly in the Task prompt; fixed role guidance belongs in the AgentDefinition.", "eli5": {"question": "The coordinator agrees the rules with the committee, but the analysis helper never hears them. The clue: the helper's instructions name only the drug and the outcome.", "answer": "A manager agrees special requests with a client on the phone, then hands a junior a sticky note that says only “Report on drug X”. The junior wasn't on the call. Put the special requests on the note. For a subagent, the note is the <b>Task prompt</b>; it does not hear the coordinator's conversation. <b>Rule: if a subagent needs it for this request, write it into the Task prompt.</b>", "options": ["A standing list on the junior's desk helps with usual requests, but not with what was agreed on today's call.", "Checking the work afterwards catches some mistakes, but the junior still worked without the rules.", "A separate file works, but the note must point to it anyway. Just write the rules on the note.", "Correct. Write today's rules on today's note."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the subagent knows what the coordinator knows</b>. It doesn't. A subagent starts with its own system prompt and the Task prompt, nothing else. Rules that change with each request can only reach it through that Task prompt.", "diagram": "coordinator ↔ committee: agree rules\n  Task prompt: drug + outcome        → rules lost\n  Task prompt: drug + outcome + rules → applied", "mapTitle": "Where should information for a subagent go?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of information", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Details for this one request", "The Task prompt", "Subagents don't inherit the coordinator's context"], ["Role guidance the same on every run", "The subagent's AgentDefinition system prompt", "Loaded every time"], ["Findings from an earlier subagent", "Passed in full in the next Task prompt", "No shared memory"], ["Wrong studies already included", "A later filter", "Cleans up, but too late"], ["Facts that must survive a long chat", "Case-facts block", "Protects the coordinator's own context"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each committee sets its own inclusion rules when it sends a request", "means": "The rules change per request, so a fixed system prompt (<b>[[0]]</b>) is the wrong home."}, {"quote": "The coordinator agrees these rules with the committee before it delegates", "means": "The rules live only in the coordinator's context."}, {"quote": "Later, the analysis subagent includes studies the committee excluded", "means": "The subagent never got the rules. A filter afterwards (<b>[[1]]</b>) treats the result, not the cause."}, {"quote": "Its Task prompt names only the drug and the outcome", "means": "The decider. The delegation is missing the rules. Put them there: <b>[[3]]</b>. A file (<b>[[2]]</b>) still has to be named in this prompt."}], "rule": "Subagents don't inherit context. Per-request details go in the Task prompt.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s3-03", "was": "m7-s3-03", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Parallel subagent spawning", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "For each request, the coordinator runs three searches: journals, the trial registry and regulatory notices. No search needs another's results. Today the coordinator sends one Task call, waits for its result, then sends the next. Committees say summaries take too long, and most of the time goes on waiting for these searches. What is the <b>best way</b> to cut the wait?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator skip any search that a request does not seem to need.", "why": "Works, but only on some requests. Skipping a search saves time when it is not needed, but most requests still wait for three searches one after another.", "trap": "only helps some requests"}, {"t": "Have the coordinator send all three Task calls in one response and run them together.", "why": "Best. The searches don't depend on each other, so they can run at the same time. The coordinator does this by sending several Task calls in one response."}, {"t": "Give each search subagent a shorter time limit, returning partial results when it runs out.", "why": "Works, but trades away quality. Each search ends sooner, but some return less evidence, and they still run one after another.", "trap": "cuts quality"}, {"t": "Have each search subagent return only the fields the coordinator needs from its results.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. Smaller results save context and a little time, but the wait comes from running the searches in series.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "No search needs another's results.", "explanation": "Independent subtasks should run in parallel: the coordinator emits several Task calls in one response. Skipping or trimming searches helps only around the edges.", "eli5": {"question": "Three searches run one after another, and nothing links them. The clue: no search needs another's results.", "answer": "Three friends each need to buy one thing in three different shops. Sending them one at a time, each waiting for the last to come back, is slow. Send all three at once. For the coordinator, “send all at once” means putting <b>several Task calls in one response</b>. <b>Rule: independent subagents run in parallel; send their Task calls together.</b>", "options": ["Skipping a shop you don't need helps sometimes, but the usual trip still has three shops, one after another.", "Correct. Send all three friends at once.", "Telling each friend to hurry gets them back sooner, with less shopping done.", "Smaller shopping bags are lighter, but the friends still go one at a time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the searches themselves are too slow</b>. But each search waits for the one before it to finish, even though none of them needs the others. The quickest gain is to run them at the same time, not to make each one smaller or shorter.", "diagram": "today:  journals ──▶ registry ──▶ notices\nbetter: journals ─┐\n        registry ─┼─▶ coordinator\n        notices ──┘", "mapTitle": "A multi-agent run is slow. Where does the time go?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Independent subagents run one after another", "Serial delegation", "Several Task calls in one response"], ["Requests often run subagents they don't need", "Full pipeline every time", "Select subagents by request"], ["Coordinator context fills with long results", "Verbose outputs", "Trim results to needed fields"], ["Many round trips for simple lookups", "Every check routed through the coordinator", "A scoped tool for the common case"], ["One step needs another's result", "Real dependency", "Keep that step in order"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "For each request, the coordinator runs three searches", "means": "All three searches are part of the normal run, so skipping some (<b>[[0]]</b>) helps only now and then."}, {"quote": "No search needs another's results", "means": "The decider. Nothing forces them to run in order, so they can run in parallel."}, {"quote": "Today the coordinator sends one Task call, waits for its result, then sends the next", "means": "The cause: serial delegation."}, {"quote": "most of the time goes on waiting for these searches", "means": "The wait is the searches running in a row. Time limits (<b>[[2]]</b>) and smaller results (<b>[[3]]</b>) trim the edges."}, {"quote": "What is the best way to cut the wait?", "means": "Run them together: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Independent subagents? Emit all their Task calls in one response so they run in parallel.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "spawn parallel subagents by emitting multiple Task calls in one response"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s3-05", "was": "m7-s3-05", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Fixed pipeline vs adaptive decomposition", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Every evidence summary follows the committee template. It has four sections: efficacy, safety, cost and guideline status. Each section is researched the same way every time, and no section depends on what another finds. Auditors must be able to replay each run step by step. The team is redesigning how the coordinator splits up the work. Which approach <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Chain a fixed set of steps for each part, then run one pass that merges the four outputs.", "why": "Best. The steps are known in advance and don't depend on findings, so a fixed pipeline (prompt chaining) fits. Every run has the same shape, which auditors can replay."}, {"t": "Let the coordinator plan from early findings and add new subtasks as results come back.", "why": "Works, but less predictable. Adaptive plans suit open questions where findings decide the next step. Here the steps never change, and each run would take a different path.", "trap": "hard to audit"}, {"t": "Draft all four sections, check the draft for gaps, and send any thin section back for more work.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. A refinement loop fills coverage gaps, but it makes run length vary, which works against step-by-step replay.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Let the coordinator judge each request and call only the subagents its sections need.", "why": "Works, but there is little to skip. Choosing subagents per request helps when requests vary. Every request here has the same four sections.", "trap": "only helps varied requests"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Each section is researched the same way every time, and no section depends on what another finds.", "explanation": "Fixed sequential pipelines suit work whose steps are known in advance; adaptive decomposition suits investigations whose subtasks depend on intermediate findings. Predictability and auditability favour the fixed pipeline.", "eli5": {"question": "Every summary has the same four sections, researched the same way, and auditors must replay each run. The clue: the steps never change and don't depend on each other.", "answer": "A bakery that makes the same four cakes every day uses a fixed recipe for each, then boxes them together. An inventor's lab, where each test decides the next, plans as it goes. This job is the bakery. In agent terms that's a <b>fixed pipeline</b> (prompt chaining) with one combining pass at the end. <b>Rule: steps known in advance → fixed pipeline. Next step depends on findings → adaptive plan.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Same recipes every day, then box them together.", "Planning as you go suits the inventor's lab. Here every day is the same, and inspectors want the same steps each time.", "Checking for gaps helps when you don't know what's missing. It makes every day's work a different length.", "Choosing which cakes to bake helps when orders vary. Here every order is the same four cakes."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the smarter, adaptive plan is always better</b>. Adaptive decomposition is for open-ended work, where what you find decides what you do next. When the steps are known in advance and audit needs the same shape every run, a fixed pipeline is simpler and easier to replay.", "diagram": "efficacy ─┐\nsafety   ─┤\ncost     ─┼─▶ combine pass ─▶ summary\nstatus   ─┘", "mapTitle": "How should the coordinator split up the work?", "map": {"head": ["Shape of the work", "Strategy", "Why"], "rows": [["Steps known in advance, independent", "Fixed pipeline (prompt chaining) + integration pass", "Predictable, easy to audit"], ["Next step depends on what was found", "Adaptive decomposition", "Subtasks come from findings"], ["Drafts often miss parts of the topic", "Refinement loop: check gaps, re-delegate", "Fills coverage gaps"], ["Requests vary a lot in size", "Select subagents per request", "Skips what isn't needed"], ["One big input dilutes attention", "Per-part passes, then a combining pass", "Each part gets full attention"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Every evidence summary follows the committee template", "means": "The output shape is the same every time."}, {"quote": "Each section is researched the same way every time, and no section depends on what another finds", "means": "The decider. The steps are fixed and independent, so nothing needs to adapt (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Auditors must be able to replay each run step by step", "means": "Runs must have the same shape. A gap-filling loop (<b>[[2]]</b>) varies in length."}, {"quote": "Which approach fits best?", "means": "A fixed pipeline with a combining pass: <b>[[0]]</b>. Choosing subagents (<b>[[3]]</b>) has nothing to skip."}], "rule": "Steps known in advance → fixed pipeline. Steps depend on findings → adaptive decomposition.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Fixed sequential pipelines (prompt chaining) vs dynamic adaptive decomposition based on intermediate findings"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s3-07", "was": "m7-s3-07", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Forcing a tool to run first", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent has two tools: <code>extract_citation_metadata</code> and <code>analyze_findings</code>. Analysis needs the page and DOI fields that extraction produces, so extraction must come first for each paper. The subagent calls a tool on every turn. On some papers it calls <code>analyze_findings</code> first, and those findings have no page numbers. Which change <b>fixes this most directly</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite both tool descriptions to say extraction comes first and which fields analysis needs.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Clearer descriptions help Claude pick the right tool, but the order is still left to the model's judgement.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "On the first request for each paper, force <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>extract_citation_metadata</code>.", "why": "Best. A forced <code>tool_choice</code> makes Claude call that exact tool, so extraction runs first on every paper. Later requests go back to <code>auto</code>."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that looks up page and DOI fields and adds them to each analysis.", "why": "Works, but too late. The hook fills the fields afterwards, but the analysis already ran without them, and the skipped step is hidden.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Merge the two tools into one that extracts the metadata and then analyses the paper.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. One tool fixes the order, but it means rewriting both tools when a single setting does the job.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "On some papers it calls analyze_findings first, and those findings have no page numbers.", "explanation": "tool_choice auto lets Claude choose, any forces some tool call, and a forced named tool makes Claude call that exact tool. To make a specific tool run first, force it on that request.", "eli5": {"question": "The helper sometimes analyses a paper before pulling out its page and DOI details. The clue: it does call a tool every time; it just picks the wrong one first.", "answer": "A lab says “label the sample, then test it”. A sign on the wall mostly works. Handing the technician the label printer first, and nothing else, works every time. For Claude, that is a <b>forced <code>tool_choice</code></b>: on the first request, it must call <code>extract_citation_metadata</code>. <b>Rule: a specific tool must run first → force it by name on that request.</b>", "options": ["A better sign on the wall helps, but the technician can still grab the wrong tool.", "Correct. Hand over the label printer first.", "Labelling samples after the test fills the gap, but the test already ran without a label.", "Building one machine that labels and tests works, but it's a big job when handing over the printer first does it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>better descriptions</b> will fix the order. Descriptions help Claude pick between tools, but the choice is still the model's. When one tool must run first, the API can make that call happen: set <code>tool_choice</code> to that named tool on the first request.", "diagram": "request 1: tool_choice = extract_citation_metadata\nrequest 2+: tool_choice = auto → analyze_findings", "mapTitle": "Which tool_choice setting fits?", "map": {"head": ["What you need", "Setting or fix", "Effect"], "rows": [["One specific tool must run first", "Forced <code>{\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":…}</code>", "That exact tool is called"], ["Claude replies in text when it should call a tool", "<code>tool_choice</code>: <code>any</code>", "Some tool must be called"], ["Claude may answer or call a tool", "<code>tool_choice</code>: <code>auto</code>", "Claude decides"], ["Claude mixes up tools with thin descriptions", "Expand the descriptions", "Better selection, still probabilistic"], ["Results need reshaping before Claude reads them", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook", "Changes results, not order"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Analysis needs the page and DOI fields that extraction produces, so extraction must come first for each paper", "means": "The order matters every time, so a description (<b>[[0]]</b>) that only suggests it is weaker."}, {"quote": "The subagent calls a tool on every turn", "means": "It is not replying in text. The problem is which tool comes first."}, {"quote": "On some papers it calls analyze_findings first, and those findings have no page numbers", "means": "The decider. The wrong tool runs first. Fixing fields afterwards (<b>[[2]]</b>) hides the skipped step."}, {"quote": "Which change fixes this most directly?", "means": "One setting on the first request: <b>[[1]]</b>. Merging tools (<b>[[3]]</b>) is a rebuild."}], "rule": "To make a specific tool run first, force tool_choice to that tool on that request.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Force a tool to run first (e.g., extract_metadata before enrichment)"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s3-08", "was": "m7-s3-08", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Scoped tool access per subagent", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "All subagents are built from one AgentDefinition template that holds the full tool set. The synthesis subagent only needs to read analysis results and save drafts. It often runs journal and registry searches in the middle of a draft. Sometimes it saves drafts with the wrong tool. Every tool already has a clear, detailed description. What is the <b>best fix</b> for its tool use?", "options": [{"t": "List in the synthesis system prompt which tools it may use and which belong to other roles.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Claude will mostly follow the list, but it still holds every tool, and prompt guidance is probabilistic.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks the synthesis subagent from calling search tools.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The hook stops the searches, but it adds code to undo a tool list that is too wide, and the wrong draft tool is still there.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Give synthesis its own AgentDefinition whose tool list holds only its read and draft tools.", "why": "Best. Too many tools make selection less reliable, and agents misuse tools outside their role. Scoping the list removes both problems at the source."}, {"t": "Add sample calls and a note on when synthesis should not use each tool to every description.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. Richer descriptions help when descriptions are thin. These are already detailed; the trouble is how many off-role tools it holds.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "All subagents are built from one AgentDefinition template that holds the full tool set.", "explanation": "Selection reliability drops as the tool count grows, and agents misuse tools outside their specialty. Give each subagent only the tools its role needs.", "eli5": {"question": "The writing helper has every tool in the system, and it keeps using ones that aren't its job. The clue: everyone is built from one template with all the tools.", "answer": "Give a new writer a desk with only a pen and a filing tray, and they write. Give them a desk piled with forty gadgets, and they fiddle with the gadgets. For a subagent, the desk is its <b>tool list in the AgentDefinition</b>. <b>Rule: give each subagent only the tools its role needs.</b>", "options": ["A note saying “don't touch the gadgets” mostly works, but the gadgets are still on the desk.", "A lock on some gadgets works, but it's easier to not put them on the desk.", "Correct. Clear the desk down to a pen and a tray.", "Better labels on the gadgets help when labels are poor. These labels are already good."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>misused tools mean unclear descriptions</b>. That is the first fix when descriptions are thin. Here they are already detailed. The real cause is that the synthesis subagent holds every role's tools, and agents misuse tools outside their specialty.", "diagram": "template: 15 tools → synthesis picks searches\nsynthesis AgentDefinition: read_results, save_draft", "mapTitle": "A subagent uses the wrong tools. What is the cause?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Uses other roles' tools; descriptions are good", "Too many off-role tools", "Scope its tool list in its AgentDefinition"], ["Mixes up similar tools; descriptions are one line", "Thin descriptions", "Expand the descriptions"], ["Needs one cross-role lookup very often", "Round trips through the coordinator", "Add one limited cross-role tool"], ["A call must be blocked for policy reasons", "Compliance rule", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook"], ["Prompt wording pulls Claude towards a tool", "Keyword association", "Reword the system prompt"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "All subagents are built from one AgentDefinition template that holds the full tool set", "means": "The decider. Synthesis carries every role's tools. Telling it which to avoid (<b>[[0]]</b>) leaves them all in place."}, {"quote": "The synthesis subagent only needs to read analysis results and save drafts", "means": "Its role needs very few tools."}, {"quote": "It often runs journal and registry searches in the middle of a draft", "means": "Off-role tools are being used. A hook (<b>[[1]]</b>) blocks the searches but leaves the wide list."}, {"quote": "Every tool already has a clear, detailed description", "means": "Descriptions are not the cause, so improving them (<b>[[3]]</b>) misses."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix for its tool use?", "means": "Give synthesis its own scoped tool list: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Each subagent gets only the tools its role needs, set in its own AgentDefinition.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Agents with off-specialization tools misuse them"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s4-01", "was": "m7-s4-01", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Grep for content search", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer wants to remove an old helper method called <code>legacy_price_round</code>. First they need to find every place that calls it. The calls could be in models, background jobs, views or library code. The file names don't say which files use the helper. How should Claude <b>find the calls</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Glob for every Ruby and view file in the app, then Read each one and note any calls.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Reading every file would find the calls, but it fills the context with thousands of files that never mention the helper.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Grep for the helper's name across the repository, then Read the lines around each match.", "why": "Best. Grep searches inside files, so it finds every call wherever it lives, and Claude reads only the places that matter."}, {"t": "Make the helper raise an error, run the whole test suite with Bash, and list the failures.", "why": "Works, but only for tested code. Failing tests point to real calls, but any call the tests don't reach stays hidden.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Ask an Explore subagent to search the codebase for callers and send back a short list.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The subagent would run the same name search; a separate agent is for long, noisy discovery, not one search.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The file names don't say which files use the helper.", "explanation": "Finding where a name is used is a question about file contents, so Grep is the direct tool. Glob matches file names and paths, and reading everything up front wastes context.", "eli5": {"question": "Someone needs a list of every place one helper is used. The clue: <i>the file names don't say</i> which files use it, so you have to look inside the files.", "answer": "If you want every page in a library that mentions one word, you use the index, not the shelf labels and not by reading every book. <b>Grep</b> is that index: it searches the text inside files. <b>Glob</b> is the shelf label: it matches file names. Here the names give no hint, so search the text and then read only the hits. <b>Rule: looking for words inside files → Grep. Looking for files by name → Glob.</b>", "options": ["Reading every book finds the word, but takes ages and buries you in pages you didn't need.", "Correct. Search the text for the name, then look only at the matches.", "Breaking the helper and running tests finds calls the tests touch. Untested calls stay hidden.", "Sending a helper off to search works, but they would just run the same search for you."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the safest way to find every call is to read everything</b>, or to let the tests tell you. Both find calls, but neither is direct. Finding where a name appears is a question about <b>file contents</b>, and that is exactly what Grep answers. Glob only looks at file names.", "diagram": "Grep \"legacy_price_round\"  → 7 matches, file + line\n  Read those 7 spots            → confirm each call\nGlob **/*.rb → 2,000 files     → names say nothing", "mapTitle": "Which built-in tool answers this question?", "map": {"head": ["What you need", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["Every place a name or text appears", "Grep", "Searches inside files"], ["Files whose name or path matches a pattern", "Glob", "Matches paths, not contents"], ["Understand one file or trace a flow", "Read, following imports", "Build understanding step by step"], ["Long, noisy discovery across a big area", "Explore subagent", "Keeps the noise out of the main context"], ["Behaviour at run time", "Bash running tests", "Shows only what tests reach"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "First they need to find every place that calls it", "means": "The task is a full list of uses: a search inside file contents."}, {"quote": "The calls could be in models, background jobs, views or library code", "means": "The calls are spread everywhere, so the search must cover the whole repository. Tests (<b>[[2]]</b>) only reach covered code."}, {"quote": "The file names don't say which files use the helper", "means": "The decider. A file-name search can't narrow it, so reading every file (<b>[[0]]</b>) is the only Glob route, and it is heavy."}, {"quote": "How should Claude find the calls?", "means": "The direct answer is one content search: <b>[[1]]</b>. A subagent (<b>[[3]]</b>) would run the same search with extra machinery."}], "rule": "Words inside files → Grep, then Read only the matches. File names or paths → Glob.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "Grep = content search; Glob = file path patterns"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s4-08", "was": "m7-s4-08", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Resuming a named session", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer keeps several named Claude Code sessions. Last week, a session called <code>billing-audit</code> traced how invoices are rounded. Today they want to carry on that same investigation. Nobody has changed the billing code since then. Their most recent session was about something else. How should they <b>pick up the work</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Start a new session and paste a structured summary of the billing-audit findings.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. A summary is the safer start when files have changed; here nothing changed, so it only drops what Claude already saw.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Start a new session and have Claude trace the invoice rounding code again from the start.", "why": "Works, but redoes the work. Claude would reach the same findings, after repeating last week's reads.", "trap": "redoes the work"}, {"t": "Start Claude Code with <code>--resume billing-audit</code> and carry on where it stopped.", "why": "Best. Resuming by name reopens that exact conversation, and its earlier tool results are still accurate."}, {"t": "Resume billing-audit, then branch it with <code>fork_session</code> before going further.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A fork is for trying a different approach from a shared starting point; this is one line of work.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Nobody has changed the billing code since then.", "explanation": "--resume with a session name continues a specific prior conversation. When files have changed since, a new session with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming with stale results.", "eli5": {"question": "Someone wants to continue last week's investigation. The clues: they know its name, and <i>nobody has changed the billing code</i>, so last week's notes are still true.", "answer": "If you left a puzzle half done and nobody touched the table, you sit back down and carry on. You don't start again, or copy it onto a new table. <b>--resume &lt;name&gt;</b> is sitting back down at that table. If someone had moved pieces, a fresh table with a clear note would be safer. <b>Rule: nothing changed → resume by name. Files changed → new session with a structured summary.</b>", "options": ["Copying notes to a new table works, but you lose detail you could have kept.", "Starting the puzzle over gets there, but wastes last week's work.", "Correct. Sit back down at the same table.", "Making a copy of the table is for trying two ways at once. There's only one way here."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a fresh start with a summary is always safer</b>. It is safer when the files have changed and old tool results are stale. Here the code is unchanged, so the old results are still accurate, and resuming keeps all of them. The name matters too: the most recent session is a different one.", "mapTitle": "Resume, fork or start fresh?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Continue a named past session, files unchanged", "<code>--resume &lt;name&gt;</code>", "Keeps every earlier finding"], ["Continue, but files have changed since", "New session + structured summary", "Old tool results are stale"], ["Resume after a few known changes", "Resume and tell Claude what changed", "Keeps context, corrects it"], ["Try two approaches from one point", "<code>fork_session</code>", "Independent branches"], ["Continue the most recent session", "<code>--continue</code>", "Opens the latest one"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "a session called billing-audit traced how invoices are rounded", "means": "There is a named session holding the findings. Tracing it again (<b>[[1]]</b>) repeats them."}, {"quote": "Nobody has changed the billing code since then", "means": "The decider. The old tool results are still true, so resuming is safe and a summary (<b>[[0]]</b>) only loses detail."}, {"quote": "Their most recent session was about something else", "means": "The session must be picked by name, not by recency."}, {"quote": "How should they pick up the work?", "means": "One line of work, continued as is: <b>[[2]]</b>. A fork (<b>[[3]]</b>) is for two approaches."}], "rule": "Files unchanged → --resume by name. Files changed → new session with a structured summary.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "--resume &lt;session-name&gt; continues a specific prior conversation"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s5-06", "was": "m7-s5-06", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria over confidence wording", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Developers dismiss most of the reviewer's performance findings. The prompt's only guidance for this category is “flag performance problems”. Last month the team added “report only findings you are highly confident about”, and the dismissal rate did not change. The team wants to keep the category, since real performance bugs have reached production. What should they change <b>next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Replace it with concrete conditions, such as a database query run inside a loop.", "why": "Best. Explicit criteria tell the reviewer exactly what counts as a performance problem. Vague wording plus a plea for confidence does not."}, {"t": "Add three sample findings to the prompt, each with flagged code and why.", "why": "Works, but too early. Examples sharpen criteria that are applied unevenly. Here there are no criteria yet, so the examples would have to carry the whole definition.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Switch the performance category off until its precision improves, then bring it back.", "why": "Works, but wrong goal. Pausing a noisy category protects developers' trust, but the team wants to keep catching real performance bugs.", "trap": "wrong goal"}, {"t": "Tag each finding with the pattern it matched, and study which patterns get dismissed.", "why": "Works, but delays the fix. Tracking patterns shows where the noise comes from, but it measures the problem while the vague guidance stays in place.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The prompt's only guidance for this category is “flag performance problems”.", "explanation": "Vague instructions plus “only high-confidence” wording do not raise precision. Define the category with concrete, checkable conditions; add examples once criteria exist.", "eli5": {"question": "The reviewer's performance comments are mostly ignored. Asking it to be “highly confident” didn't help. The clue: the only guidance is “flag performance problems”.", "answer": "Telling a new referee “call bad fouls, but only when you're sure” changes nothing. Giving them the rulebook does: “a push from behind is a foul”. Concrete conditions are the <b>explicit criteria</b> for the category. <b>Rule: too many false alarms → define exactly what counts, don't ask for more confidence.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Spell out what counts as a performance problem.", "Showing past calls helps a referee who has a rulebook. Without one, they are guessing what the examples have in common.", "Benching the referee stops bad calls, and good ones too.", "Keeping a tally of bad calls is useful later, but the referee still has no rulebook."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>false positives mean the model is not careful enough</b>, so you ask it to be careful. Precision comes from knowing <b>what counts</b>. “Flag performance problems” leaves that open, and “only if highly confident” doesn't close it.", "mapTitle": "The reviewer raises too many false alarms. What's missing?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Category is vague; no criteria", "Concrete, checkable conditions", "Says exactly what counts"], ["Criteria exist but are applied unevenly", "2–4 examples with reasoning", "Shows how to apply them"], ["Prompt asks for “high confidence”", "Replace with criteria", "Confidence wording doesn't raise precision"], ["Not sure which findings are noise", "Track <code>detected_pattern</code> on findings", "Shows dismissal patterns"], ["Category is noisy and not needed", "Pause it", "Protects trust in the rest"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Developers dismiss most of the reviewer's performance findings", "means": "A precision problem in one category."}, {"quote": "The prompt's only guidance for this category is “flag performance problems”", "means": "The decider. There are no criteria, so define them: <b>[[0]]</b>. Examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) illustrate criteria; they come after."}, {"quote": "the dismissal rate did not change", "means": "Confidence wording failed, as the exam guide predicts."}, {"quote": "The team wants to keep the category, since real performance bugs have reached production", "means": "Pausing it (<b>[[2]]</b>) gives up the real catches. Tracking patterns (<b>[[3]]</b>) measures, but doesn't fix."}], "rule": "To cut false positives, define explicit criteria. “Only high-confidence” wording does not raise precision.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "Explicit criteria beat vague instructions"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s6-06", "was": "m7-s6-06", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 stop_reason-driven loop", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "The extraction agent runs in a loop with two lookup tools. The loop ends when Claude's reply contains the phrase “EXTRACTION COMPLETE”. Sometimes Claude writes that phrase while also asking for a tool call, and the loop exits before the tool runs. Other runs never write the phrase and stop at the iteration cap. How should the loop <b>decide when to stop</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Continue while <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>tool_use</code>, and end the loop when it is <code>end_turn</code>.", "why": "Best. The API reports on every response whether Claude wants a tool or has finished. That is the signal the loop is built on."}, {"t": "Keep the phrase, but run any requested tools first and stop on the next turn without a tool call.", "why": "Works, but still reads text. It fixes the early exit, but runs that never write the phrase still stop at the cap.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have Claude return a <code>status</code> field, and stop when it says complete with no tool call pending.", "why": "Works, but reinvents the signal. A structured flag is sturdier than a phrase, but Claude can still forget it, and the API already reports this.", "trap": "reinvents the signal"}, {"t": "End the loop from a hook once every required schema field has a value.", "why": "Works, but wrong signal. It ends many runs on time, but fields can fill before the lookups are done, and runs with a missing field still hit the cap.", "trap": "wrong signal"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Sometimes Claude writes that phrase while also asking for a tool call, and the loop exits before the tool runs.", "explanation": "An agent loop continues while stop_reason is tool_use and ends on end_turn. Parsing the reply text or using an iteration cap as the main stop are anti-patterns.", "eli5": {"question": "The loop stops when Claude writes a magic phrase. Sometimes it writes it too early, sometimes not at all. The clue: the loop is reading Claude's words to decide when the job is done.", "answer": "A waiter who waits for you to say “that's all” sometimes leaves while you're still ordering. Most restaurants just watch whether you've closed the menu. The API gives that closed menu on every reply: <b><code>stop_reason</code></b>. <code>tool_use</code> means “still ordering”; <code>end_turn</code> means “done”. <b>Rule: stop the loop on stop_reason, not on words.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Watch the menu, not the words.", "Better, but people who never say “that's all” still keep the waiter there all night.", "A “done” card on the table is better than words, but the menu already tells you.", "Leaving when the plates are full can be too early, or never happen."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the loop needs a clearer “done” message</b> from Claude. But the API already says, on every response, whether Claude wants a tool (<code>tool_use</code>) or has finished (<code>end_turn</code>). Any signal Claude writes itself, a phrase or a flag, can be missing or early.", "diagram": "response → stop_reason?\n  tool_use → run tool, append result, loop\n  end_turn → stop", "mapTitle": "How should an agent loop know it is done?", "map": {"head": ["Approach", "Problem", "Verdict"], "rows": [["<code>stop_reason</code>: continue on <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>", "None", "Use this"], ["A phrase in the reply text", "Early, missing or misread", "Anti-pattern"], ["A status field Claude writes", "Claude can forget or misjudge it", "Weaker copy of stop_reason"], ["Iteration cap as the main stop", "Cuts off real work or runs long", "Safety net only"], ["All fields filled", "Not the same as all work done", "Validation, not termination"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The loop ends when Claude's reply contains the phrase", "means": "The loop reads text to decide termination, an anti-pattern."}, {"quote": "Sometimes Claude writes that phrase while also asking for a tool call, and the loop exits before the tool runs", "means": "The decider. The response was really <code>tool_use</code>; the loop ignored it."}, {"quote": "Other runs never write the phrase and stop at the iteration cap", "means": "Any self-written signal (<b>[[1]]</b>, <b>[[2]]</b>) can go missing, and filled fields (<b>[[3]]</b>) can come before the work is done."}, {"quote": "How should the loop decide when to stop?", "means": "Use the API's own signal: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Continue on tool_use, stop on end_turn. Never parse text or rely on a cap to stop.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "Continue the loop when stop_reason == \"tool_use\"; terminate when \"end_turn\""}}}, {"id": "m7p-s6-09", "was": "m7-s6-09", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Stratified sampling and field-level routing", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "The team wants to send only doubtful fields to reviewers and post the rest directly. Accuracy was measured on a uniform random sample and is high overall. Scans from small carriers are rare in that sample. Compliance wants error rates measured for each document type before any field skips review. Which <b>two steps</b> together achieve this?", "options": [{"t": "Label a new sample with a quota for each document type, including enough small-carrier scans.", "why": "Best. A quota per type gives enough examples of each, including the rare scans, to measure each type's error rate."}, {"t": "Label a larger uniform random sample, and report accuracy for each field across all documents.", "why": "Works, but rare types stay rare. A bigger sample and per-field numbers help, but small-carrier scans are still too few to measure, and results are not split by type.", "trap": "only partly"}, {"t": "Have the model report a confidence for each field, and send any field below a fixed cutoff to a reviewer.", "why": "Works, but unchecked. Field-level routing is the right shape, but self-reported confidence is not matched against real errors, so the cutoff is a guess.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Calibrate each field's confidence on the labelled set, and send fields below its threshold to review.", "why": "Best. Thresholds set from labelled results route each field by its measured error rate, which is the routing the team wants."}], "answer": [0, 3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Compliance wants error rates measured for each document type before any field skips review.", "explanation": "High overall accuracy can hide weak document types. Measure with a stratified sample, then route fields by confidence calibrated on that labelled set.", "eli5": {"question": "The team wants to skip review for fields the model is sure about. The clue: compliance wants each type of document measured, and rare scans barely appear in the sample.", "answer": "A school says “95% passed”, but only two pupils from the small village took the test. To know how the village does, you test a set number from each place. That's a <b>stratified sample</b>. Then you only trust a pupil's “I'm sure” once you've checked how often “sure” was right. That's <b>calibrated confidence</b>. <b>Rule: measure each type on purpose, then route by confidence checked against real answers.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Test a set number from each type, rare ones included.", "A bigger random test still has hardly any village pupils.", "Trusting “I'm sure” without checking how often it's right is a guess.", "Correct. Set each cut-off from how often “sure” was really right."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>high overall accuracy</b> means it is safe to skip review. An average can hide a weak document type, and rare types barely show up in a uniform sample. Two things are needed: <b>measure each type</b> on purpose, and <b>route each field</b> by confidence that has been checked against labelled answers.", "diagram": "stratified sample (quota per type) → labels\n  → error rate per type and field\n  → calibrated thresholds → route doubtful fields", "mapTitle": "Is it safe to let some extractions skip human review?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Right step", "Why not the alternative"], "rows": [["Error rate for each document type, rare ones too", "Stratified sample with a quota per type", "Uniform samples barely include rare types"], ["Route individual fields, not whole documents", "Field-level confidence calibrated on labels", "Raw self-reported confidence is uncalibrated"], ["Check high-confidence results stay accurate", "Ongoing stratified random sampling", "Errors drift without a check"], ["A single headline accuracy number", "Not enough on its own", "Masks weak types and fields"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team wants to send only doubtful fields to reviewers and post the rest directly", "means": "Routing is per field, so the confidence must be per field and trustworthy: <b>[[3]]</b>, not a raw score (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Accuracy was measured on a uniform random sample and is high overall", "means": "An overall number can hide weak types."}, {"quote": "Scans from small carriers are rare in that sample", "means": "A larger uniform sample (<b>[[1]]</b>) still holds few of them."}, {"quote": "Compliance wants error rates measured for each document type before any field skips review", "means": "The decider: sample by type with a quota, <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Measure by type with a stratified sample, then route fields by confidence calibrated on labels.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Stratified random sampling measures high-confidence error rates; field-level confidence calibrated on labeled sets routes review"}}}]};
window.BEFORE = {"m7-s1-03": {"question": "Cancellations are handled by people, so the agent routes them through <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Product wants the agent to offer a pause before it escalates a cancellation. This is a retention nudge, not a policy. Missing it in some chats is acceptable. The change should be light to maintain. Which <b>TWO</b> changes fit best?", "options": [{"t": "Force <code>pause_subscription</code> through <code>tool_choice</code> on the turn where a customer first asks to cancel.", "why": "Incorrect. Forcing the tool pauses the subscription without the customer agreeing. The goal is to offer a pause, not to apply one."}, {"t": "Add a system-prompt instruction to suggest pausing deliveries before routing a cancellation request to a person.", "why": "Correct. A soft goal where occasional misses are fine is exactly what prompt guidance is for. It is one line to maintain."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that denies <code>escalate_to_human</code> on cancellations until a pause has been offered.", "why": "Runner-up. A hook is the right tool when a step must happen every time. Here misses are acceptable, so code that must detect cancellations and offers is more machinery than the goal needs."}, {"t": "Include two or three sample chats where the agent suggests a pause and then escalates once the customer declines.", "why": "Correct. A few examples show the order of steps and the tone, and make the nudge consistent without any code."}], "answer": [1, 3], "runnerUp": 2}, "m7-s1-06": {"question": "<code>lookup_order</code> takes a box ID, and its whole description reads “Looks up a box”. Customers usually name a box by when it came, such as “last Tuesday's box”. The agent then passes that date as the box ID, and the call fails. Each customer's box IDs are listed in the <code>get_customer</code> record. What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Expand the description with the ID format, a sample call, and how to go from a date to an ID.", "why": "Correct. Descriptions are the main way the model learns how to call a tool. This one gives no input format and no route from a date to an ID, and fixing that is cheap."}, {"t": "Return a structured validation error from <code>lookup_order</code> that names the expected ID format and an example.", "why": "Incorrect. A clear validation error helps the agent recover after a bad call. The first step is to stop the bad call."}, {"t": "Add a few-shot example to the system prompt that resolves a date-based request through <code>get_customer</code> first.", "why": "Runner-up. Examples help once the descriptions are clear but behaviour is still inconsistent. With a one-line description, the root cause is the description, and examples add tokens without fixing it."}, {"t": "Add a <code>lookup_order_by_date</code> tool that accepts a delivery date and returns the customer's matching box.", "why": "Incorrect. A new tool is more work than a first step, and with minimal descriptions the agent may misuse it too."}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2}, "m7-s2-02": {"question": "Accessibility conventions apply to every screen component. Screens are files named <code>*.screen.tsx</code>. Most sit in feature folders under <code>mobile/</code>, but some live in <code>packages/shared/ui/</code>. Today the conventions are in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so they load even for server work. Where should the conventions <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Into <code>mobile/CLAUDE.md</code>, plus a <code>packages/shared/ui/CLAUDE.md</code> file that imports it.", "why": "Runner-up. Directory CLAUDE.md files work when a convention belongs to a folder. Screens are a filename pattern across trees: this needs a file per tree and also loads for non-screen files there."}, {"t": "Into an <code>/a11y</code> skill whose description says to use it whenever a screen file is edited.", "why": "Incorrect. Skills load on demand; relying on Claude to invoke one from its description is less dependable than a rule that loads by path."}, {"t": "Into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> frontmatter is a glob for that filename pattern.", "why": "Correct. A rule with a paths glob loads whenever Claude works on a matching file, wherever it sits, and stays out of server sessions."}, {"t": "Into a separate markdown file pulled into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> through an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Incorrect. @import organises memory into modules, but an imported file still loads in every session."}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s6-01": {"question": "Many certificates print no deductible at all. The schema marks deductible as a required number. For these certificates the model returns 0 or copies the limit. Either value then posts as if it had been printed. Account assistants cannot tell these values from real ones. Which change <b>best stops these invented values</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep the field required, and have the model enter -1 as a marker whenever no deductible is printed.", "why": "Incorrect. A sentinel number is still a number: downstream code must know the convention, and -1 can be posted as a real value."}, {"t": "Make deductible nullable in the schema, and describe in the field when to return null.", "why": "Correct. A required number forces the model to invent one. A nullable field gives it an honest way to report \"not printed\"."}, {"t": "Add two worked certificates with no printed deductible to the prompt, each with its expected output.", "why": "Runner-up. Examples reduce extraction hallucination and would be the fix if the field were already optional. While the schema demands a number, there is no valid output to show."}, {"t": "Add a deductible_status enum, printed or not_printed, beside the number, and route not_printed.", "why": "Incorrect. It labels the gap, but the required number still forces an invented value into the record."}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2}, "m7-s1-10": {"question": "Spoilage disputes can run long. When a chat nears the context limit, the SDK summarises older turns. Afterwards the agent sometimes offers a smaller credit than it promised earlier. It also forgets which items the customer listed. The tool results in these chats are short. Which change <b>best fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep a running block of promised amounts, listed items and IDs, and put it at the top of every request.", "why": "Correct. Transactional facts kept outside the summarised history survive every summarisation pass, and placing them first keeps them prominent."}, {"t": "Trim each tool result to the few fields the agent uses before it is added to the conversation history.", "why": "Runner-up. Trimming is the fix when verbose tool output fills the context. These tool results are already short, so trimming saves little and the summaries still blur the promises."}, {"t": "Raise the context threshold at which older turns are summarised, keeping more of each chat verbatim.", "why": "Incorrect. This delays the loss rather than preventing it; long disputes still cross the threshold."}, {"t": "Ask the summariser to write longer summaries that quote every amount and item from the earlier turns.", "why": "Incorrect. A better summary prompt helps, but facts still pass through a lossy step each time. A separate facts block avoids that step."}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1}, "m7-s1-04": {"question": "<code>get_customer</code> returns the full card number on file, because the billing team's tools need it. That team owns the server and will not change it. Twice, the support agent read a card number back to a customer. Security says a card number must not reach a customer in any chat. Which change <b>meets this requirement</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a system-prompt rule to refer to cards by their last four digits only, with two example replies to copy.", "why": "Runner-up. A clear rule with examples is the right fix for a should-usually behaviour. Security needs a must-never, and prompt compliance has a non-zero failure rate."}, {"t": "Register a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that masks the card field in each <code>get_customer</code> result before Claude reads it.", "why": "Correct. The hook rewrites the result before the model sees it, so the agent cannot repeat a number it never had. It needs no change to the billing team's server."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that logs any card number found in a tool result for security to review.", "why": "Incorrect. Right hook, wrong action: logging records exposure after the model has the number; it does not stop the number reaching a reply."}, {"t": "Route <code>get_customer</code> through a subagent whose definition returns only name, plan and box IDs.", "why": "Incorrect. The subagent still receives the full number and decides what to pass on; its definition is an instruction, not a filter."}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s1-01": {"question": "The agent's loop ends a turn whenever Claude's response contains a text block. Claude often writes a short line, such as “Let me check that box,” in the same response as a request to call <code>lookup_order</code>. In those chats the lookup never runs. The customer waits for an answer that never comes. Which change to the loop <b>fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>\"any\"</code> on the first request after each customer message, then back to <code>auto</code>.", "why": "Runner-up. \"any\" is the right lever when Claude answers in text instead of requesting a tool. Here Claude did request the tool; the loop ended the turn before running it, and it would still do so."}, {"t": "Append each tool's output as a <code>tool_result</code> block carrying its <code>tool_use_id</code> before the next request.", "why": "Incorrect. Returning results correctly matters, but here the tool never ran, so there is no result to return."}, {"t": "Branch on the <code>stop_reason</code> of each response: run requested tools and loop on <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>.", "why": "Correct. A response can hold text and a tool request together. stop_reason is the signal for what happens next: tool_use means run the tools and continue, end_turn means the turn is over."}, {"t": "End the turn only when the reply text has no pending-action phrase, and cap the loop at a fixed number of rounds.", "why": "Incorrect. Matching phrases in the text and using an iteration cap as the stop are both loop anti-patterns; the response already carries an explicit signal."}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s1-08": {"question": "A customer writes: “My salmon was missing again. I want to speak to a real person.” The refund is well inside the agent's limit, so it could settle the case alone. The customer sounds calm. The request for a person is direct and unambiguous. How should the agent <b>respond</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Acknowledge the problem, offer the refund it can issue now, and escalate if the customer asks for a person again.", "why": "Runner-up. Offering a resolution first is right when a customer is frustrated but has not asked for a person. Here the request is direct, so the agent should not make the customer ask twice."}, {"t": "Run <code>lookup_order</code> to confirm the missing item first, then decide between refunding and escalating.", "why": "Incorrect. Investigating before honouring an explicit request delays the customer for a decision that is already made."}, {"t": "Check the customer's refund history first, and escalate only if the item has gone missing on an earlier box too.", "why": "Incorrect. Repeat issues can matter for policy, but the customer asked for a person; that trigger does not depend on history."}, {"t": "Call <code>escalate_to_human</code> straight away, with the customer ID, the box and the missing item in the handoff.", "why": "Correct. An explicit request for a human is an escalation trigger on its own. Honour it immediately and pass the facts so the person can pick up without re-asking."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s2-09": {"question": "Midway through generating a set of screens, an engineer sees the context is nearly full. Most of it is long test output from earlier runs. Claude's recent answers are still accurate and refer to specific files. Nothing has changed in the repository outside this session. What should the engineer <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude write its decisions to a notes file, then open a new session that starts by reading that file.", "why": "Runner-up. A scratchpad plus a fresh session is right when answers have degraded or the work spans sessions. Here answers are still specific and the session is current."}, {"t": "Start routing future test runs through a subagent that returns only the failing tests and their causes.", "why": "Incorrect. Good for future runs, but it does not free the context that is already nearly full."}, {"t": "Resume the same session by name with <code>--resume</code> in a new terminal and continue from there.", "why": "Incorrect. Resuming reloads the same conversation, with the same full context."}, {"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> to condense the conversation so far, then carry on generating the remaining screens.", "why": "Correct. The session is healthy and current; only space is short. Compacting frees context while keeping the work in the same session."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s3-02": {"question": "Each committee sets its own inclusion rules when it submits a request, such as a minimum trial size or excluded drug classes. The coordinator agrees these rules with the requester before delegating. The analysis subagent then includes studies the committee excluded. Its Task prompt names the drug and the outcome, but nothing else. The rules differ on every request. <b>Where should the inclusion rules go?</b>", "options": [{"t": "Into the analysis subagent's AgentDefinition system prompt, as a criteria section with included and excluded examples.", "why": "Runner-up. A subagent's system prompt is the right home for criteria that are the same on every run. These change per request, so a fixed prompt can't hold them."}, {"t": "Into a criteria section of the coordinator's system prompt, which the coordinator updates for each new request.", "why": "Incorrect. The coordinator's prompt is part of the coordinator's context. Subagents don't inherit it, so the analysis step still never sees the rules."}, {"t": "Into a case-facts block the coordinator maintains, listing the drug, the outcome and each inclusion rule.", "why": "Incorrect. A case-facts block keeps details safe in the coordinator's own long context. It doesn't reach a subagent unless it is written into the delegation."}, {"t": "Into each analysis Task prompt, stated in full for that request beside the drug and the outcome to study.", "why": "Correct. Subagents do not inherit the coordinator's conversation; per-request context must be passed explicitly in the Task prompt."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s3-03": {"question": "For each request, the coordinator runs three searches: journal literature, the trial registry and regulatory notices. None of the searches uses another's results. Today the coordinator sends one Task call, waits for its result, then sends the next. Committees say summaries take too long. Every request needs all three searches. <b>Which change best cuts the wait?</b>", "options": [{"t": "Let the coordinator judge each request's complexity and invoke only the search subagents that request needs.", "why": "Runner-up. Dynamic selection saves time when many requests skip some subagents. Here every request needs all three."}, {"t": "Have the coordinator emit the three Task calls together in one response and use each result as it arrives.", "why": "Correct. Independent subagents can run in parallel when the coordinator emits multiple Task calls in a single response."}, {"t": "Merge the three search subagents into one that holds all three search tools and runs the searches itself.", "why": "Incorrect. One subagent with all the tools still runs the searches one after another, and it mixes three roles in one context."}, {"t": "Have each search subagent return only trimmed fields, so the coordinator reads less from each result.", "why": "Incorrect. Trimming saves context tokens, but the wait comes from running independent searches in series."}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s3-05": {"question": "Every evidence summary follows the committee template. It has the same four sections, each researched the same way: efficacy, safety, cost and guideline status. No section's research depends on what another section finds. Auditors must be able to replay each run step by step and see the same structure. The team is redesigning how the coordinator splits up work. <b>Which decomposition fits best?</b>", "options": [{"t": "Chain fixed steps for each section in a set order, then run one integration pass over the four outputs.", "why": "Correct. When the steps are known in advance and don't depend on intermediate findings, a fixed pipeline (prompt chaining) is predictable and auditable."}, {"t": "Let the coordinator plan from early findings and add subtasks for each section as results come back.", "why": "Runner-up. Adaptive decomposition fits open-ended investigations where findings decide the next step. Here the steps never change, and audit needs repeatable runs."}, {"t": "Draft all four sections, have the coordinator check the draft for gaps, and re-delegate any thin section.", "why": "Incorrect. A refinement loop helps when coverage gaps appear. It makes run length vary, which works against step-by-step replay."}, {"t": "Let the coordinator size each request and invoke only the subagents that its four sections need.", "why": "Incorrect. Dynamic selection suits requests of varying size. Every request here has the same four sections."}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1}, "m7-s3-07": {"question": "The document-analysis subagent has two tools: extract_citation_metadata and analyze_findings. Each paper must go through extraction first, because analysis needs its page and DOI fields. The subagent calls a tool on every turn. But on some papers it calls analyze_findings first, and those findings carry no page numbers. <b>Which setting fixes this most directly?</b>", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite both tool descriptions to say which tool comes first and which fields the analysis step expects.", "why": "Incorrect. Better descriptions improve selection but stay probabilistic; the order is still left to the model."}, {"t": "On the first request for each paper, set tool_choice to the named tool for metadata extraction.", "why": "Correct. A forced tool_choice makes Claude call that specific tool, so extraction runs before analysis."}, {"t": "Add a PostToolUse hook that looks up page and DOI fields and adds them to each analysis result.", "why": "Incorrect. A hook can transform results, but repairing them afterwards hides the skipped step instead of preventing it."}, {"t": "Set tool_choice to any for every request the subagent makes while it is working on a paper.", "why": "Runner-up. <code>any</code> guarantees a tool call instead of text. The subagent already calls a tool every turn; it picks the wrong one first."}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 3}, "m7-s3-08": {"question": "All subagents are built from one AgentDefinition template that carries the full tool set. The synthesis subagent only needs to read analysis results and save drafts. It often runs registry and journal searches mid-draft, and sometimes saves drafts with the wrong tool. Every tool already has a detailed, distinct description. Most of the tools belong to other subagents' roles. <b>Which change best fixes its tool use?</b>", "options": [{"t": "Add a section to the synthesis system prompt naming the tools it may use and the tools that belong to other roles.", "why": "Incorrect. Telling the subagent which tools are off-limits helps, but it still holds them all; prompt guidance stays probabilistic."}, {"t": "Expose the analysis results as an MCP resource that the synthesis subagent reads before it starts each draft.", "why": "Incorrect. A resource can cut exploratory calls, but the subagent still holds every other role's tools."}, {"t": "Give the synthesis subagent its own AgentDefinition whose tool list holds only the read and draft tools it uses.", "why": "Correct. Too many tools degrade selection, and off-role tools get misused; scope each subagent's tools to its role."}, {"t": "Rewrite each tool's description with input formats, sample calls and a note on when synthesis should not use it.", "why": "Runner-up. Better descriptions are the first fix when descriptions are thin. These are already detailed; the trouble is how many off-role tools it holds."}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 3}, "m7-s4-01": {"question": "An engineer must remove the deprecated helper legacy_price_round. First they need every place that calls it. Calls could be in models, jobs, views or lib code. File names give no hint about which files use the helper. <b>Which approach finds the call sites most directly?</b>", "options": [{"t": "Read the helper's definition, then Grep for files that require its module and Read each of those files.", "why": "Incorrect. Files that load a module aren't the same set as files that call this helper; Rails autoloading means many callers never require it."}, {"t": "Use Bash to run the test suite with the helper stubbed to raise, and collect each failing call site.", "why": "Incorrect. Tests only reach code they cover; untested call sites stay hidden."}, {"t": "Use Grep for the helper's name across the repository, and review the file and line of each match.", "why": "Correct. Grep searches file contents, so it finds every call site wherever it lives."}, {"t": "Use Glob with **/*price*.rb to list the pricing files, then Read each one for calls to the helper.", "why": "Runner-up. Glob is the right tool when the target is defined by file names or paths. Here the names say nothing about which files call the helper."}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 3}, "m7-s4-08": {"question": "An engineer keeps several named Claude Code sessions. Last week, one called billing-audit traced how invoices are rounded. They want to continue that exact conversation today. Nobody has changed the billing code since then. Their most recent session was about something else. <b>How should they continue?</b>", "options": [{"t": "Start a new session and paste a structured summary of the billing-audit findings into the first prompt.", "why": "Runner-up. A fresh session with a summary beats resuming when files have changed and old tool results are stale. Nothing has changed here."}, {"t": "Start Claude Code with --continue and ask it to pick up the invoice rounding work from there.", "why": "Incorrect. --continue reopens the most recent session, which is the unrelated one."}, {"t": "Start a new session, run /memory, and load the billing-audit conversation from the listed files.", "why": "Incorrect. /memory shows which memory files are loaded; it doesn't restore a past conversation."}, {"t": "Start Claude Code with --resume billing-audit and carry on with the investigation where it stopped.", "why": "Correct. --resume with the session name continues that specific conversation, with its findings intact."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s5-06": {"question": "Developers dismiss most of the reviewer's performance findings. The prompt's only guidance for the category is \"flag performance problems\". Last month the team added \"report only findings you are highly confident about\". The dismissal rate did not change. The team wants to keep the category, since real performance bugs have reached production. What should they change <b>next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Replace that guidance with concrete conditions, such as a query run inside a loop over fetched rows.", "why": "Correct. Categorical criteria tell the reviewer exactly what counts. Vague wording plus a confidence plea does not."}, {"t": "Add three sample findings to the prompt, each with the flagged code and the reasoning for flagging it.", "why": "Runner-up. Examples are the best tool when written criteria exist but are applied inconsistently. Here there are no criteria yet, so examples would have nothing to illustrate."}, {"t": "Switch off the performance category until its precision improves, then bring it back.", "why": "Incorrect. Pausing a noisy category protects trust, but the team wants to keep catching real performance bugs."}, {"t": "Add a confidence score to each finding and post only the ones that score above a set threshold.", "why": "Incorrect. Self-reported confidence is the same lever as the failed \"highly confident\" line, in numeric form."}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1}, "m7-s6-06": {"question": "The extraction agent runs in a loop with two lookup tools. The loop ends when Claude's reply contains the phrase \"EXTRACTION COMPLETE\". Sometimes Claude writes that phrase while also requesting a tool call, and the loop exits before the tool runs. Other runs never write the phrase and stop at an iteration cap. How should the loop <b>decide when to stop</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude return a structured status field, and stop when it reads complete with no tool call pending.", "why": "Runner-up. Structured fields beat free-text parsing, and this covers the pending-tool case. But it is still model content acting as the stop signal; the API already reports why each turn ended."}, {"t": "Keep the phrase, but run any requested tools first and stop on the turn after they return.", "why": "Incorrect. It patches one failure but keeps text parsing as the stop, and runs that skip the phrase still hit the cap."}, {"t": "Add a PostToolUse hook that ends the loop once every required schema field has a value.", "why": "Incorrect. Code-driven, but it stops on a pre-set condition: the model may still be verifying or correcting values."}, {"t": "Continue while <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>tool_use</code>, and end the loop when it is <code>end_turn</code>.", "why": "Correct. The stop reason is the API's own signal: run the requested tools and continue, or finish."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s6-09": {"question": "The team wants to send only doubtful fields to account assistants and post the rest directly. Accuracy was measured on a uniform random sample and is high overall. Scans from small carriers are rare in that sample. Compliance wants error rates measured for each document type before anything skips review. Which TWO steps <b>together achieve this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the model report a confidence for each field, and send fields under a fixed cutoff to review.", "why": "Runner-up. Per-field routing is the right shape, but uncalibrated self-reported confidence is unreliable. Calibrated against labelled data, it becomes the answer."}, {"t": "Label a new sample with a quota per document type, including enough small-carrier scans.", "why": "Correct. Stratified sampling gives each document type enough labelled cases to measure its own error rate."}, {"t": "Label a larger uniform sample, and report accuracy for each field across all documents.", "why": "Incorrect. Per-field figures are useful, but a uniform sample still under-represents rare types and pools them with the rest."}, {"t": "Calibrate each field's confidence against the labelled set, and send fields under their cutoff to review.", "why": "Correct. Calibrated field-level confidence decides which individual fields are doubtful and go to a person."}], "answer": [1, 3], "runnerUp": 0}};
